NFL.put("recaps-2026",{"401872656":{"h":"Seahawks pick off Drake Maye 3 times to beat Patriots 13-10 in Super Bowl rematch","d":"— Sam Darnold left the game injured, the offense was stagnant and sloppy mistakes put Seattle in a hole through three quarters.","b":"SEATTLE -- — Sam Darnold left the game injured, the offense was stagnant and sloppy mistakes put Seattle in a hole through three quarters.\n\n\r\n              Then the Dark Side defense took over, Drew Lock made a few big plays and the Seahawks capped a Super Bowl banner-raising night with another win against the New England Patriots.\n\n\r\n              Lock led two late scoring drives and the Seahawks intercepted Drake Maye three times in the fourth quarter to overcome an early injury to Darnold and beat the Patriots 13-10 on Wednesday night in a Super Bowl rematch.\n\n\r\n              “I want to give our team a lot of credit because you know things weren’t really going where we wanted,” coach Mike Macdonald said. “We didn’t panic. We still liked our plan. We didn’t need to change what we were doing or anything like that. We just we wanted to stick to what we were doing, and the guys were just really excited to go back out there and play.”\n\n\r\n              The Seahawks were held to just a field goal through three quarters as Lock and the offense struggled to move the ball after Darnold injured his hip on the opening drive. Macdonald said initial tests showed Darnold didn't break a bone, calling the news “positive.”\n\n\r\n              But the costly interceptions by Maye — last season's MVP runner-up — turned the tide and made sure that the banner-raising night would end in happy fashion for the boisterous home crowd.\n\n\r\n              “We knew coming in it was going to be a four-quarter game and that’s who we are,” receiver Jaxon Smith-Njigba said. “We knew it’s going to be a dogfight and we love it. Whatever it looks like in the first half, second quarter, like we’re going to make it right. We’re going to keep fighting until the clock hits triple zero. So I was just happy to see that and to see that continue going forward this year.”\n\n\r\n              Maye overcame the first two picks to drive the Patriots into position to tie or take the lead in the final minute. But Josh Jobe made a toe-tapping interception in the end zone to seal the win.\n\n\r\n              “I was trying to get rid of it, and I realized Mack was back there in the back and I didn’t put enough on it to get it out,” Maye said. “It was unacceptable.”\n\n\r\n              Seattle trailed 10-0 but got a field goal in the third quarter and tied the game when Lock connected with Smith-Njigba on a 45-yard pass on fourth-and-1 following an interception by Nehemiah Pritchett.\n\n\r\n              Julian Love intercepted Maye on the next drive and the Seahawks took a 13-10 lead on a 26-yard field goal by Jason Myers. That set the stage for the defensive stand on the final drive.\n\n\r\n              The game started following a ceremony that featured the Super Bowl banner being raised and highlights being played on the video board. This was the third Super Bowl rematch in Week 1, with it last happening in 2016 when Denver celebrated its title with a win over Carolina.\n\n\r\n              The excitement turned to worry quickly as Darnold came up limping following a third-down sack by Dre’Mont Jones on the first drive. Darnold went immediately to the sideline medical tent and then back to the locker room before the next drive.\n\n\r\n              Lock, who threw just three passes last season, entered the game. Seattle’s offense was stagnant until the fourth quarter, but Lock made enough plays late to provide a signature moment of a career that has had few highlights since he was drafted in the second round by Denver in 2019. He won just eight starts in three seasons with the Broncos before coming to Seattle in the Russell Wilson trade. He left to be a backup for the New York Giants before returning to Seattle last season.\n\n\r\n              “It was a blast,” Lock said. “So proud of everyone around me tonight on the offensive side of the ball. I can't say enough about the defense turning over the ball.”\n\n\r\n              The Patriots didn’t fare much better on offense but got the only score of the first half after Jobe interfered with offseason acquisition A.J. Brown on a deep flea-flicker pass and Maye found rookie Eli Raridon for a 2-yard score on the next play to make it 7-0.\n\n\r\n              A penalty helped set up the second Patriots score as well. Chazz Surratt was flagged for roughing the punter on the opening drive of the second half, setting up Andy Borregales’ 50-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Patriots: Brown left the game with an injured right ankle in the third quarter and didn’t return.\n\n\r\n              Seahawks: S Nick Emmanwori (ankle) was ruled inactive before the game.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Patriots: Host Pittsburgh on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              Seahawks: Visit Arizona on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0910/03bfb965ddcd460bbaaf5941a3dc31e76/03bfb965ddcd460bbaaf5941a3dc31e76.jpg"},"401872657":{"h":"49ers rout Rams 27-7 in NFL's first Australia game behind Brock Purdy's 3 TD passes","d":"— The San Francisco 49ers enjoyed every moment of their weeklong Australian adventure. They gradually adjusted their body clocks, enjoyed Melbourne's sights and tastes — and then they dominated a division rival in the NFL's first regular-season game...","b":"MELBOURNE, Australia -- — The San Francisco 49ers enjoyed every moment of their weeklong Australian adventure. They gradually adjusted their body clocks, enjoyed Melbourne's sights and tastes — and then they dominated a division rival in the NFL's first regular-season game Down Under.\n\n\r\n              The Los Angeles Rams only boomeranged in 28 hours before kickoff, and they flew back out with their Super Bowl aspirations in disarray after getting thrashed in a historic season opener.\n\n\r\n              Mike Evans, Demarcus Robinson and Deebo Samuel caught touchdown passes from Brock Purdy, and the Niners routed the Rams 27-7 Friday.\n\n\r\n              Purdy passed for 205 yards against the Rams' ineffective defense at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in front of 100,021 fans, the seventh-largest regular season crowd in NFL history. Australia's sporting capital reveled in its first real taste of American football — and in a dominant win by the 49ers, who had already won many Aussies' affection by traveling to Melbourne a week earlier than the Rams.\n\n\r\n              “It was definitely worth it to come a little bit early,” said Evans, who caught six passes in a strong 49ers debut. “We definitely got acclimated. ... All the guys attacked it. We didn’t complain. We actually enjoyed being here in Melbourne.”\n\n\r\n              A party atmosphere prevailed even with a Friday morning kickoff necessary for U.S. television. The fans got another memorable chapter in this NFC West rivalry as the 49ers surprisingly blasted the touted Rams with superior play on both sides of the ball.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco coach Kyle Shanahan said the historic, hulking arena “felt gladiator-ish. ... It felt like home once we got here. I'm glad the guys played like it, too.”\n\n\r\n              Kyren Williams scored the NFL’s first Australian touchdown on a 5-yard rush to begin the second quarter, but the 49ers took control from there, scoring the final 24 points.\n\n\r\n              While Purdy's offense managed enough big plays to win, the San Francisco defense frustrated NFL MVP Matthew Stafford into a 15-of-25 performance for just 155 yards. The Niners defense now led by longtime Rams coordinator Raheem Morris held the NFL's best offense last season to 290 yards and one score, even stopping Stafford on a fourth-down quarterback sneak from the San Francisco 1 in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              “Probably only had two good drives, to be honest,” Stafford said. “Not enough good stuff on offense. We didn't do the things that we're accustomed to doing. Too many times, we were on different pages. That starts with me.”\n\n\r\n              Garrett has a quiet debut\r\n              While San Francisco's Fred Warner and Nick Bosa both had big moments in their first games back from lengthy injury absences, Myles Garrett was practically invisible in his Rams debut after missing much of training camp with aches, recording zero tackles. The reigning AP NFL Defensive Player of the Year finally acknowledged after the game that his knee injury has him at less than full health.\n\n\r\n              “This was the worst version of myself that I’ve put out there, and I’ve got to be better,” Garrett said.\n\n\r\n              Rams' travel plans are scrutinized\r\n              Much of the pregame talk for this trip centered around the teams’ starkly differing methods of preparation: The 49ers ingratiated themselves to the local fans as their body clocks adjusted, while the Rams stayed home until less than two days before kickoff, keeping their body clocks on California time.\n\n\r\n              “I know there’s gonna be a lot of narratives that appropriately put the blame on me, and then we’ll move forward the right way,” coach Sean McVay said. “I don’t believe (the travel plan) is why we didn’t play well enough. I really don’t.”\n\n\r\n              Niners take control\r\n              Both teams started with understandable sluggishness for a 10:35 a.m. kickoff, but Williams' TD run finally got the crowd cheering. The quarterbacks then traded interceptions before Purdy shook off a shaky start and found former Rams receiver Robinson on a 39-yard post route.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco surged out of halftime, with Samuel's 45-yard kickoff return leading to Evans' TD catch over new Rams cornerback Jaylen Watson.\n\n\r\n              The Rams drove deep into the red zone, but after Puka Nacua was ruled down inches outside the goal line, the 49ers stuffed Stafford. San Francisco's responded with a 99-yard drive ending in Samuel's TD grab.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              The 49ers host Miami on Sunday, Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              The Rams host the New York Giants on Monday, Sept. 21.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0911/84169ce1-54f2-4d32-89ef-63672e6b365c/84169ce1-54f2-4d32-89ef-63672e6b365c.jpg"},"401872925":{"h":"Bengals force 4 turnovers, Burrow throws for TD in 33-27 win over the Buccaneers","d":"— Coach Zac Taylor and the Cincinnati Bengals talked all offseason about how their defensive acquisitions would have a huge impact.","b":"CINCINNATI -- — Coach Zac Taylor and the Cincinnati Bengals talked all offseason about how their defensive acquisitions would have a huge impact.\n\n\r\n              They didn’t waste any time.\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati’s new-look defense forced four turnovers — including its first fumble recovery for a TD since 2022 — in a 33-27 victory over the Tampa Bay Buccaneerso on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “You felt their presence, all of them today,” Taylor said after the Bengals won their opener for the second straight year. “It was fun to watch those guys get in the action and create all those turnovers.”\n\n\r\n              It was a win where all three phases contributed, something that could rarely be said about the Bengals over the past couple of seasons.\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati scored 24 points off Tampa Bay’s mistakes. Three were committed by quarterback Baker Mayfield, who signed a $165 million three-year extension earlier in the week.\n\n\r\n              Two fumbles came while he was sacked and another on a scramble when the Buccaneers were on the verge of scoring.\n\n\r\n              It is the first time Mayfield has lost an opener in his four seasons with Tampa Bay.\n\n\r\n              “They upgraded the personnel a lot. They were running on some twists and being consistent. Relentless effort, you know. Like the last one, it’s (me) holding on to the ball too long, but those guys are running around the edge, so it’s relentless effort bringing it,” said Mayfield, who was 23 of 28 passing for 216 yards and also ran for a score.\n\n\r\n              Joe Burrow passed for 254 yards and a touchdown. After a three-and-out on the opening series, the seventh-year franchise quarterback led the Bengals to points on six of their next seven drives. The only hiccup was a pick-6 by Tampa Bay rookie linebacker Jeremiah Trotter.\n\n\r\n              “If I didn’t have that interception, I’m feeling great walking out here. Can’t make plays like that, so I got to get that fixed,” said Burrow, who completed 25 of 35 passes.\n\n\r\n              And Evan McPherson had four field goals, including from 55 yards and 58 yards.\n\n\r\n              Edge rusher Boye Mafe and safety Bryan Cook, both signed in free agency, had big games. Both had forced fumbles and recoveries, and Mafe also had one of Cincinnati’s four sacks.\n\n\r\n              “It was the second gear that we had, but that was kind of like a statement. I do want to finish a little better as well, but to come out and win like that, it’s a beautiful thing,” Cook said.\n\n\r\n              Bucs’ early fumbles play critical role\r\n              Tampa Bay scored on its first possession when Chase McLaughlin made a 34-yard field goal, but the Buccaneers turned it over on their next three possessions. Cincinnati converted all three fumbles into touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              Mayfield rolled right when Myles Murphy and Barrett Carter sacked him at the Buccaneers 26. Demetrius Knight picked up the loose ball at the 27 and ran up the sideline for his first NFL touchdown.\n\n\r\n              It was the Bengals’ first scoop-and-score since Sam Hubbard’s 99-yard touchdown in a 2022 wild-card round game against Baltimore. It also marked the third time that the defense has supplied Cincinnati’s first touchdown of the season.\n\n\r\n              “Somebody swiped my leg, so I’m thinking, ‘Please don’t go down right here.’ Like they say, a lesser athlete would have fallen, but it was an amazing opportunity,” Knight said.\n\n\r\n              Burrow’s 2-yard TD pass to tight end Mike Gesicki came seven plays after Mafe recovered Bucky Irving’s fumble at the Bucs 33.\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati extended its lead to 21-3 midway through the second quarter on Chase Brown’s 5-yard run. The Bengals got the ball on their 20 after Cook recovered Mayfield’s fumble in the end zone after Carter stripped the ball out of Mayfield’s hands at the Bengals 4 as the nine-year veteran was trying to stretch for extra yards.\n\n\r\n              Closing time\r\n              Mayfield’s 9-yard scramble TD pulled the Buccaneers within six with 3:19 remaining, but Cincinnati ran out the clock. Burrow’s 18-yard completion to Tee Higgins to the Bucs 26 on second-and-10 put it out of reach.\n\n\r\n              “We came out with more of an aggressive mindset. Credit to our guys for understanding how we wanted to run the clock there, while still getting maximum yardage,” Taylor said.\n\n\r\n              Trotter’s impressive debut\r\n              Trotter became the first player with a sack and pick-6 in his first game since sacks became an official statistic in 1982.\n\n\r\n              Both plays came in the third quarter. The second-round pick brought down Burrow for a 9-yard loss before jumping the route on a pass intended for Andrei Iosivas and ran it back 38 yards to get the Buccaneers within 27-20 with 2:16 remaining in the quarter.\n\n\r\n              “Good play on his part. It’s a play he blew from practice. He learned from it and made a play from there,” Bucs coach Todd Bowles said.\n\n\r\n              Long-range McPherson\r\n              McPherson had second-half field goals from 55 and 58 yards. He is the first kicker in Bengals history to have two from at least 55 yards in a game. It is the fourth time in league history this has happened in Week 1.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Buccaneers: CB Jacob Parrish tweaked his back during the second quarter and did not return. Bowles said Parrish will undergo an MRI.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Buccaneers: host Cleveland next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bengals: at Houston next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/61de66ee-435d-4dc4-91ed-9406828b5c0b/61de66ee-435d-4dc4-91ed-9406828b5c0b.jpg"},"401872923":{"h":"Lions escape with 31-30 win over Saints in OT after Kellen Moore chooses to go for 2 and the win","d":"— The Detroit Lions felt fortunate to escape with a 31-30 victory in overtime over New Orleans on Sunday for at least a few reasons.","b":"DETROIT -- — The Detroit Lions felt fortunate to escape with a 31-30 victory in overtime over New Orleans on Sunday for at least a few reasons.\n\n\r\n              The Lions had to defend a 2-point conversion to hold on for the victory. They blew a three-touchdown lead in the third quarter. And, a review went their way that overturned a touchdown for the Saints.\n\n\r\n              “Football is a weird game,” Jared Goff said.\n\n\r\n              Indeed.\n\n\r\n              The Saints had a chance to score a touchdown in overtime to extend the game and Tyler Shough connected with Noah Fant for an 8-yard throw in the end zone.\n\n\r\n              After coach Kellen Moore chose to go for the win instead of tying the game with time on the clock, Shough threw an incomplete pass in the end zone on a 2-point attempt.\n\n\r\n              “The Lions have a great offense, and I didn’t want to give them 1:34 to try to make a play and beat us,\" Moore said.\n\n\r\n              Detroit was at its best with the ball when Jahmyr Gibbs had it.\n\n\r\n              Gibbs finished with a career-high 29 carries for 156 yards and two touchdowns along with five catches for 30 yards.\n\n\r\n              “That guy is our hot hand,” Lions coach Dan Campbell said.\n\n\r\n              New Orleans rallied from a 21-0 deficit and pulled into a 24-all tie on Shough's 9-yard touchdown pass to Juwan Johnson with 1:07 left in the game.\n\n\r\n              Detroit went three and out on offense on the subsequent drive, giving the Saints the ball with a chance to win it but Ryan Wright missed a 62-yard field goal attempt as time expired.\n\n\r\n              “We hung in there and made the last play to win and that’s the most important thing,” Campbell said.\n\n\r\n              Goff was 26 of 39 for 206 yards with two touchdown passes without turning it over and that proved to be a difference in the game.\n\n\r\n              Shough, coming off a promising rookie year, threw two interceptions and lost a fumble before closing strong to finish 35 of 56 for 410 yards and three scores.\n\n\r\n              “Our defense did a lot of good things in the first half to keep us in the game,” Moore said. “We just couldn’t find a rhythm on offense.”\n\n\r\n              The Saints suddenly showed signs of life on offense late in the third, scoring two touchdowns on Kendre Miller’s 8-yard run and Slough’s 1-yard pass to a fully extended Devaughn Vele to pull within a touchdown.\n\n\r\n              Trailing by seven late in the game, Shough converted a fourth-and-12 from his 23 with a 15-yard pass to rookie receiver Bryce Lance. Three snaps later, he threw a 43-yard pass to Chris Olave to set up the touchdown pass to Johnson.\n\n\r\n              The Lions advanced to the New Orleans 1 late in the fourth with a chance to seal the victory, but a holding penalty pushed them back and led to a field goal for a 24-17 lead with 2:32 left.\n\n\r\n              Pivotal call\r\n              The Saints had a touchdown overturned by video review early in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              “I’ll be the first to say, I thought we lost that one,\" Campbell said.\n\n\r\n              Goff dropped back to pass and was sacked by Chase Young, forcing what appeared to be a fumble that was scooped up for a defensive touchdown.\n\n\r\n              The veteran quarterback said he didn't know why the rule went in his favor.\n\n\r\n              “I was trying to figure it out on the fly,\" Goff said.\n\n\r\n              While it looked like a fumble, the call on the field was changed by review because Goff started his throwing motion to make it an incomplete pass.\n\n\r\n              “It’s unfortunate, but we know what the rule says,” Moore said. “Once the arm starts forward it doesn’t matter where the ball ends up.”\n\n\r\n              Injury report\r\n              Saints: RB Alvin Kamara (knee) and DE Cameron Jordan (ankle) were inactive.\n\n\r\n              Lions: Starting OG Christian Mahogany injured his right knee early in the game and was carted off the field.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Saints: Play at Baltimore on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              Lions: Play at Buffalo on Sept. 17.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0913/dm_260913_NFL/dm_260913_NFL.jpg"},"401872924":{"h":"Breece Hall powers Jets past Titans 23-10 in a dominant season opener against their former coach","d":"— The New York Jets made some big moves and changes during the offseason trying to shake up a franchise mired in a decade of losing.","b":"NASHVILLE, Tenn. -- — The New York Jets made some big moves and changes during the offseason trying to shake up a franchise mired in a decade of losing. \n\n\r\n              For one game, they got exactly what they wanted.\n\n\r\n              Breece Hall ran for 102 yards and a touchdown, Geno Smith threw for 215 yards in his Jets' return and the Jets defeated Tennessee 23-10 on Sunday to spoil Titans coach Robert Saleh's debut against his former team.\n\n\r\n              “We’re just following our plan, and that’s it,” Jets coach Aaron Glenn said. “That’s it. We’re happy to get a win. It’s only one game.” \n\n\r\n              The Jets never trailed, led 10-3 at halftime with the TD scored by rookie tight end Kenyon Sadiq and held the ball close to 39 minutes. Smith, drafted by New York in 2013 and acquired from Las Vegas in March, finished 19 of 24 while efficiently moving new coordinator Frank Reich' s offense.\n\n\r\n              “We started fast, and obviously we kept our foot on the gas and just tried to stay even-keeled throughout,” Smith said.\n\n\r\n              The game matched franchises that went 3-14 last season and prompted the Titans to hire Saleh — who coached the Jets from 2021 before being fired after five games in 2024 — in January hoping to end four straight losing seasons. Tennessee also was the NFL's biggest spenders in March adding free agents.\n\n\r\n              “A little birdie told us that they wrote some checks they couldn’t cash today,” Hall said to laughter. \n\n\r\n              The Jets, meanwhile, have the longest playoff drought in the four major North American men’s sports leagues with Glenn starting his second season in New York. \n\n\r\n              The Jets left Music City with their first season-opening win since 2023. A team that began last season by losing its first seven under Glenn and dropped its last five dominated Tennessee with a 367-195 edge in total offense. The Jets also had a 19-play drive that used up nearly 12 minutes of the first half.\n\n\r\n              “I’m very disappointed,” Ward said. “We’re better than what we showed.”\n\n\r\n              With Glenn calling the defense this season, rookie edge rusher David Bailey, the No. 2 pick overall, had one of three sacks for the Jets, who chased Titans quarterback Cam Ward all game. Linebacker Demario Davis, among the free agent additions, led the defense in tackles, while offseason addition Kingsley Enagbare had a sack. \n\n\r\n              “They got after us,” said Saleh, who called the Titans’ plays on defense after not doing that with the Jets. \n\n\r\n              Ward was 19 of 32 for 140 yards and a late touchdown pass to Elic Ayomanor.\n\n\r\n              Glenn deactivated kicker Blake Grupe, who was claimed off waivers Aug. 31 from Indianapolis, and went with Jason Sanders, who was elevated from the practice squad. Sanders made field goals of 33, 51 and 52 yards, rebounding after yanking a 54-yard attempt short and left.\n\n\r\n              Titanic early mistakes \r\n              Titans kicker Joey Slye pushed the opening kickoff out of bounds for a penalty, and Saleh had to call a timeout to avoid a penalty for 12 men on the field. All-Pro returner Chimere Dike had a teammate pushed into him on his first attempt at fielding a punt. Once cleared from a concussion check, Dike lost a fumble on a kickoff. \n\n\r\n              Honoring Chris Johnson \r\n              Former running back Chris Johnson couldn’t make it Sunday with the Titans set to induct him into their ring of honor. The Titans went ahead with honoring the man nicknamed CJ2K after becoming the sixth man in NFL history to run for at least 2,000 yards. Johnson announced in June that he had been diagnosed with ALS.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Jets: Safety Minkah Fitzpatrick hurt his groin in the first quarter and didn't return. Rookie wide receiver Omar Cooper Jr. hurt an ankle in the first and never returned. Glenn said: “I’m not too worried about them at all.\"\n\n\r\n              Titans: Dike was hurt on his first chance to return a punt when teammate Micah Robinson was pushed backward into Dike. He was evaluated for a concussion and cleared to return. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Jets: Host Green Bay in their home opener on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              Titans: Host Philadelphia on Sept. 20. \n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0913/dm_260913_Titans_IA_on_Jets_loss/dm_260913_Titans_IA_on_Jets_loss.jpg"},"401872659":{"h":"Derrick Henry's rushes for 3 TDs and Lamar Jackson lights up the Colts in the Ravens' 41-23 win","d":"— Derrick Henry still looks ageless, and Lamar Jackson put on an MVP-like show again.","b":"INDIANAPOLIS -- — Derrick Henry still looks ageless, and Lamar Jackson put on an MVP-like show again.\n\n\r\n              Together, the Baltimore Ravens' offensive stars delivered a knockout punch.\n\n\r\n              The 32-year-old Henry rushed for 144 yards and three touchdowns as he moved into third place on the NFL’s career list, and Jackson rebounded from a frustrating 2025 season by throwing for 324 yards, with touchdowns passing and rushing, as Baltimore opened the season with a 41-23 victory over the Indianapolis Colts on Sunday in coach Jesse Minter's debut.\n\n\r\n              “Oh, King Henry, I love you,” Jackson said as he jumped off the podium and hugged his teammate following his postgame news conference.\n\n\r\n              That sentiment was unanimous inside the Ravens' locker room.\n\n\r\n              Henry opened his 12th season with yet another big day against Indy, a team he tormented during his time with the Tennessee Titans. He had 24 carries and finished with his 56th 100-yard game and his eighth game with three or more TD runs. Henry also passed two Hall of Famers, Marcus Allen and former Indy star Marvin Harrison, on the career touchdown list.\n\n\r\n              His 125 rushing TDs are two more than Allen, and Henry's 130 total TDs rank ninth, one ahead of Harrison.\n\n\r\n              Jackson, meanwhile, showed his subpar 2025 season could be an anomaly.\n\n\r\n              He was 17 of 25 passing, had seven carries for 40 yards and showed his elusiveness by escaping a sack near midfield and scrambling for a tone-setting first down on third-and-16 in the first quarter. Baltimore scored its second touchdown three plays later.\n\n\r\n              “We were all on point, from the play-calling to the execution,” Jackson said. “I've been dialed in since OTA (offseason team activities). We all have to stay locked in like this. I've got a ton of confidence in these guys that they can do that.”\n\n\r\n              The Ravens outgained the Colts 506 yards to 251 and improved to 13-6 in openers since 2008 while topping the 20-point mark in a league-best 10th consecutive Week 1.\n\n\r\n              Jackson's 10th 300-yard game started with a 229-yard first half as he connected with Zay Flowers five times for 150 yards and a 54-yard catch-and-run. Flowers did not play in the second half because of a hamstring injury.\n\n\r\n              Indy lost its 11th opener in 13 years. Daniel Jones was 19 of 31 for 166 yards with a TD and an interception in his first game since tearing his right Achilles tendon in December. Jonathan Taylor ran 19 times for 98 yards and two touchdowns but also lost a fumble that led to a Baltimore score.\n\n\r\n              “Obviously, we've got to be better,” coach Shane Steichen said after hearing boos from the Lucas Oil Stadium crowd. “We've got to coach better. We've got to play better. They had a ton of yards. We've got to be better there, but it's not just about one group. We weren't good enough.”\n\n\r\n              The Ravens started fast. After Taylor opened the game with a 1-yard TD run, Baltimore answered with Jackson's 5-yard scoring run, Henry's 4-yard TD run, Flowers' TD reception and Henry's 32-yard run to make it 28-6.\n\n\r\n              Indy could never catch up. Taylor answered with a 3-yard TD run late in the first half. The Ravens closed out the half with Tyler Loop's 57-yard field goal to make it 31-13 — the fifth time in franchise history they scored at least 31 points in the first half.\n\n\r\n              Spencer Shrader made a 58-yard field goal in the third quarter to get Indy within 31-16. But Henry's 3-yard scoring run with 12:46 left sealed it.\n\n\r\n              Another milestone\r\n              Defensive tackle Calais Campbell had a big day in his first game back with Baltimore. The 40-year-old joined Jim Marshall and Hall of Famer Bruce Smith as the only defensive linemen in league history to make 260 career starts. Marshall finished with 272, and Smith had 267.\n\n\r\n              Campbell’s 19-year NFL journey began in Arizona and has taken him to Jacksonville, Baltimore from 2020-22, Miami, back to Arizona in 2025 and now back to the Ravens.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Ravens: Flowers was ruled out during the third quarter, and it wasn't the only big hit to the receiving corps. Ja'Kobi Lane injured a wrist and did not return. CB Marlon Humphrey left briefly in the fourth quarter with what appeared to be a right leg injury. CB T.J. Tampa Jr. did not return after hurting his knee in the first quarter.\n\n\r\n              Colts: Indy appeared to avoid any significant injuries.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Ravens: Host New Orleans next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Colts: At Kansas City next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/bda875f2-806a-4e7b-9d2a-a98212517757/bda875f2-806a-4e7b-9d2a-a98212517757.jpg"},"401872658":{"h":"T.J. Watt's late pick-6 pushes sluggish Steelers past injury-laden Falcons 20-13","d":"— T.J. Watt began his 10th season by trying to move past the noise that followed his ninth.","b":"PITTSBURGH -- — T.J. Watt began his 10th season by trying to move past the noise that followed his ninth.\n\n\r\n              The noise that said the Pittsburgh Steelers star outside linebacker had lost a step, maybe two, after quiet and injury-marred 2025. The noise that wondered if his time as a singular gamewrecker was over as his 32nd birthday neared.\n\n\r\n              Watt provided an emphatic answer Sunday: not quite yet. \n\n\r\n              The former high school tight end turned back the clock in a 20-13 season-opening win over Atlanta, leaping to pick off a pass from Cooper Rush and racing — well, after stumbling over his own feet — 35 yards for a decisive fourth-quarter touchdown as Pittsburgh made Mike McCarthy's debut coaching his hometown team a success.\n\n\r\n               “That’s what he does,” longtime teammate Alex Highsmith said. “He's the best in the world.”\n\n\r\n              Even if Watt didn't play like it at times last season, when the four-time All-Pro finished with 7 sacks — tied for the second-fewest of his career — and missed several games late following a freak dry needling injury.\n\n\r\n              Watt already put a dent in that total in the opener, chasing down Rush twice as part of a defensive effort that sacked the late replacement for Tua Tagovailoa four times, swatted away six passes and never let him get comfortable.\n\n\r\n              That discomfort was never more noticeable on Watt's second career touchdown, when he anticipated a dump off to Robinson and snatched the ball out of midair just after Rush released it. The next few seconds were chaos, as Watt scrambled downfield, briefly lost his footing and popped back up before he was touched.\n\n\r\n              “I had no bearing at all,” Watt said. “I'm sure the fall didn't look too graceful but glad someone told me to get up and keep running.”\n\n\r\n              The Steelers needed a vintage Watt performance to overcome a sluggish start by Aaron Rodgers and company. Rodgers began his 22nd and final season by throwing for 221 yards and a touchdown to tight end Pat Freiermuth but was hampered by a couple of drops by DK Metcalf and a running game that did little to take pressure off him.\n\n\r\n              Yet there were also signs of potential, particularly late in the fourth quarter when the Steelers took over deep in their territory. Rodgers completed three passes during the clinching drive — one each to Metcalf, Michael Pittman and Darnell Washington — as Pittsburgh drained the final 4:16 off the clock. \n\n\r\n              “Being able to end the game on our terms, with Aaron on the ball, taking a knee, you know you can’t take that for granted,” Freiermuth said.\n\n\r\n              The ball eventually ended up in the hands of McCarthy, who grew up a few miles from downtown Pittsburgh and is well aware of the expectations that come with the job. The first of what he hopes are many victorious afternoons didn't go particularly smoothly, especially for the offense that he was hired specifically to fine tune.\n\n\r\n              Not that it mattered when Rodgers handed him the game ball, which McCarthy called a “special moment” as they started a reunion from their time in Green Bay that included a Super Bowl win. There's also a part of him that's ready to get all the firsts out of the way so he can lean into the rhythm of the season.\n\n\r\n              “It's time to move on and get team victories from here on,” McCarthy said. “I'm not one for attention. So the homecoming has been great. Just glad to get this one under our belt.”\n\n\r\n              Robinson a one-man show for Atlanta\r\n              The Falcons have plenty of questions to answer this season under first-year coach Kevin Stefanski. \n\n\r\n              Running back Bijan Robinson is not one of them.\n\n\r\n              The young star did everything he could to keep the Falcons in it, accounting for 173 of the Falcons' 238 total yards and Atlanta's lone touchdown on a swing pass in which he split three defenders and raced to the end zone.\n\n\r\n              “Bijan's a great player,” Stefanski said. “We're going to keep leaning on him in all these games, and we have to.”\n\n\r\n              It just wasn't enough for the Falcons to win in Pittsburgh for the first time in franchise history. Not on a day when Atlanta had 96 yards in penalties and kicker Nick Folk, who had just one missed field goal each of the past three seasons, was off target on his first two kicks when the outcome was still in doubt.\n\n\r\n              “Nick's not going to be perfect this year, I don't expect him to be perfect,” Stefanski said. “I don't expect our football team to be perfect. But you want your guys to respond to it, so I know he'll respond.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Falcons cornerback A.J. Terrell was hurt breaking up a pass in the third quarter. He missed a series but did return. Defensive tackle De'Shawn Hand exited with a knee injury in the first half and did not return.\n\n\r\n              The Steelers played without cornerback Joey Porter Jr., who missed most of training camp with a back issue. He did practice during the week, though McCarthy said Porter's absence was due to his health and not contract talks that broke down without Porter signing an extension.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Falcons: host NFC South rival Carolina next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Steelers: head to New England next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/94e9c638-3c5e-456a-b8a9-285a8f3326e5/94e9c638-3c5e-456a-b8a9-285a8f3326e5.jpg"},"401872661":{"h":"Caleb Williams and Bears trounce Panthers 59-37 in highest-scoring season opener in NFL history","d":"— Ben Johnson said he slept well Saturday night at the Bears' team hotel, sensing that quarterback Caleb Williams was more than ready for the season opener.","b":"CHARLOTTE, N.C. -- — Ben Johnson said he slept well Saturday night at the Bears' team hotel, sensing that quarterback Caleb Williams was more than ready for the season opener.\n\n\r\n              Chicago's coach had good instincts.\n\n\r\n              Williams, the No. 1 pick in the 2024 NFL draft, looked like a league MVP candidate on Sunday, throwing for 269 yards and two touchdowns and running for 65 yards and two more scores as the Bears trounced the Carolina Panthers 59-37 in the highest-scoring season opener in NFL history.\n\n\r\n              “I could kind of tell (Saturday) night when we did our quarterback meeting one last time that he was just on top of it,” Johnson said. “Everything he was rattling out, like where my eyes need to be and what I’m thinking. ... So I went to bed and just slept really well.”\n\n\r\n              Johnson's prolific Bears broke a franchise record for points in an opener, a mark that had stood since 1948, when Chicago hung 45 points on Green Bay at Wrigley Field.\n\n\r\n              The 59 points were the most allowed by the Panthers since they began play as an expansion team in 1995.\n\n\r\n              D'Andre Swift, who received a $33.75 million contract extension earlier this week, paid immediate dividends for the Bears, rushing for 124 yards on 18 carries with three touchdowns, while Kyle Monangai had 100 yards on the ground, including a 61-yard touchdown burst in the second half.\n\n\r\n              “He's had a good week, huh?” Johnson said of Swift. “He earned (the contract), every step of it. We really value what he brings to the table. He has had a great spring and I don't think anybody in our camp is surprised he is out of the gate in strong fashion.”\n\n\r\n              It was the fourth-most points in Bears history. The most came in their notorious 73-0 win over Washington in the 1940 NFL title game.\n\n\r\n              “That's how we want to look each week,” Williams said. “That's who we want to be each week.”\n\n\r\n              Chicago and Carolina scored eight more points than the previous record of 88 set in Tampa Bay’s 48-40 win over New Orleans in 2018.\n\n\r\n              Although Carolina's Bryce Young fell to 0-4 in openers, it was by far the best season debut of his career. The 2023 No. 1 overall pick threw for 361 yards and three touchdowns. He also ran for a score.\n\n\r\n              Jalen Coker, who emerged as Carolina's No. 2 receiver last year, had eight catches for a career-high 138 yards and two TDs.\n\n\r\n              But the Panthers were done in three turnovers — a muffed punt by Jimmy Horn, a fumble by Tetairoa McMillan fumble and a pick by Young — and a leaky defense that committed untimely penalties.\n\n\r\n              The Bears put up 552 yards on offense and had 26 first downs. They were 10 of 14 on third-down conversions.\n\n\r\n              “It was an incredible day by the Bears,” Panthers coach Dave Canales said. “There were some things that we did to give a really good offense more opportunities. They don’t need any more opportunities. They were already creating their own.”\n\n\r\n              The Bears turned the ball over on their first possession, but quickly recovered and scored seven touchdowns on their next 12 drives, including the final four possessions to close the game.\n\n\r\n              Williams showed pure command, completing several pinpoint passes while racking up 196 yards through the air in the first half, including a perfectly placed 22-yard touchdown strike to Cole Kmet.\n\n\r\n              “He couldn't have put ball in a better spot and Cole made a heck of a catch there, too,” Johnson said.\n\n\r\n              He also showed the elusiveness that makes him hard to contain. He ran for 52 yards and two TDs — including a 29-yard scamper — before the break as the Bears took a 31-24 lead into the locker room.\n\n\r\n              Bears shrug off a fourth-down try that went wrong\r\n              Chicago's lead might have been bigger had it not been for a questionable decision by Johnson.\n\n\r\n              With the Bears leading 21-14, Johnson went for it on fourth-and-1 at the Chicago 21, but Williams came up short on a QB sneak.\n\n\r\n              “I felt pretty about the call,” Johnson said.\n\n\r\n              In the end, it didn't matter. Mistakes ended the Panthers' hopes in the second half.\n\n\r\n              After Chuba Hubbard scored his second TD to tie the game at 31-all, Carolina defensive end Derrick Brown was flagged for a late hit on Williams, allowing a drive to continue. That led to Swift's 1-yard TD run.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers went for it on fourth down near midfield on their next possession but Young couldn't connect with McMillan on a lofted pass down the left sideline. Chicago cashed in on the next play as Monangai broke through a hole in the middle and raced 61 yards for a 45-31 lead — a play that Williams called his favorite of the game.\n\n\r\n              “That's a crushing thing when you're in that situation when you are midfield and you get a downhill run like that,” Williams said.\n\n\r\n              On Carolina's next drive, McMillan fumbled, giving the Bears the ball back. Williams cashed in with a scoring strike to a wide-open Jahdea Walker for a 52-31 lead with 6:27 remaining.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Bears: LT Braxton Jones left late in the third quarter with an upper-body injury. CB Tyrique Stevenson injured his shoulder. Neither appeared to be serious.\n\n\r\n              Panthers: DT Bobby Brown left in the second half with a back injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Bears: Host Minnesota next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Panthers: At Atlanta next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0913/dm_260913_Panthers_IA/dm_260913_Panthers_IA.jpg"},"401872922":{"h":"Trevor Lawrence throws 4 TD passes as the Jaguars rout the Browns 34-10 in season opener","d":"— Trevor Lawrence and the Jacksonville Jaguars waited seven agonizing months to play another meaningful game at EverBank Stadium.","b":"JACKSONVILLE, Fla. -- — Trevor Lawrence and the Jacksonville Jaguars waited seven agonizing months to play another meaningful game at EverBank Stadium.\n\n\r\n              This one won’t help them forget the previous one, a three-point loss to Buffalo in the first round of the playoffs. But it was the best possible way to begin one of the most anticipated seasons in franchise history.\n\n\r\n              Lawrence threw for 245 yards and four touchdowns, opening the season much like he closed last year, and the Jaguars routed rebuilding Cleveland 34-10 on Sunday to spoil the debut of new Browns coach Todd Monken.\n\n\r\n              “It was a great first step for where we're trying to get to,” Jaguars defensive end Josh Hines-Allen said.\n\n\r\n              Jacksonville scored on six of its first seven possessions, with Lawrence connecting with Parker Washington, rookie Josh Cameron, Brenton Strange and Jakobi Meyers for touchdowns. Three of four targets were wide open.\n\n\r\n              Lawrence completed 18 of 23 passes and finished with a career-high rating of 150.6. He was one of the best quarterbacks in the league down the stretch in 2025 while leading the Jaguars to the AFC South title.\n\n\r\n              His play under coach Liam Coen was a main reason Jacksonville entered this fall as a trendy pick to return to the postseason and maybe reach its first Super Bowl. The opener was a solid start.\n\n\r\n              “It was awesome,” Lawrence said. “Guys were ready to play.”\n\n\r\n              Washington, a pending free agent who’s in contract negotiations with Jacksonville, made a diving catch on Lawrence's first TD pass and flashed a “pay me” sign to the 42,000 in attendance inside a venue being fully renovated.\n\n\r\n              Lawrence made it look easy all afternoon against a defense that traded five-time All-Pro defensive end Myles Garrett in the offseason and was entering its first season under new defensive coordinator Mike Rutenberg.\n\n\r\n              Cleveland had equally troubling issues on the other side of the ball.\n\n\r\n              Deshaun Watson, making his first start in nearly two years, threw an interception on the opening drive. \n\n\r\n              “We did exactly what we wanted to start the game,” Monken said. “We’re going to try to put them on their heels and then we turn it over. And then we couldn’t get a first down to get our defense off the field. That is a recipe to get run out of the arena.”\n\n\r\n              Watson nearly threw another pick on the team’s second possession and later recovered his own fumble to avoid a turnover.\n\n\r\n              It was a stark contrast to his previous outings against the Jags. Watson had been undefeated in seven starts against Jacksonville, winning at home, on the road, in London with a swollen eye, in tight games, blowouts and even once while recovering from a broken rib and a bruised lung.\n\n\r\n              But he was overwhelmed Sunday, getting sacked five times and hit way more often. \n\n\r\n              Rookie receiver KC Concepcion Jr. also fumbled, and the Browns were penalized 11 times for 75 yards. They also went just 1 for 8 on third down and failed to score a touchdown on two trips inside the 20-yard line.\n\n\r\n              Cleveland finished with 272 yards and two highlights: Raheim Sanders returned a kickoff 74 yards to set up a 26-yard field goal in the third quarter, and Watson connected with Denzel Boston for a 46-yard TD with 1:52 remaining.\n\n\r\n              “We were hoping for a ‘W.’ We put in so much work, especially myself, to come in and be successful,” Watson said. “But it's the NFL; it doesn't go that way all the time. You play and you learn. ... The NFL, it's a hard league. You got to be week to week and try to be better each and every time.”\n\n\r\n              Key injuries\r\n              Browns: RB Dylan Sampson (knee) was ruled out in the first half. LG Zion Johnson (left knee) left in the first half but later returned.\n\n\r\n              Jaguars: DE B.J. Green (elbow) was ruled out at halftime. WR Brian Thomas Jr. (shoulder) went to the locker room in the second quarter but returned for the final series of the first half.\n\n\r\n              Van Lanen returns 8 months after knee surgery\r\n              Jaguars right tackle Cole Van Lanen started the opener eight months after knee surgery. Van Lanen tore a ligament in his right knee in the regular-season finale, missed most of training camp and didn’t play a down in the preseason. But he managed to make it back in time for the Browns.\n\n\r\n              He played the first half before giving way to backup Walker Little. It was a fairly quick return for a 312-pound lineman.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Browns play at Tampa Bay next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Jaguars play at Denver next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/534142c4-afbd-491f-b3ca-cdbdc553203c/534142c4-afbd-491f-b3ca-cdbdc553203c.jpg"},"401872660":{"h":"Josh Allen connects with Joshua Palmer for a late TD and Bills beat Texans 36-31 in wild opener","d":"— Josh Allen didn’t wait for the question he knew was coming on Sunday after finally leading the Buffalo Bills to a road win over the Houston Texans in a season-opening thriller.","b":"HOUSTON -- — Josh Allen didn’t wait for the question he knew was coming on Sunday after finally leading the Buffalo Bills to a road win over the Houston Texans in a season-opening thriller.\n\n\r\n              “We do like Reliant Stadium,” he said with a big smile. “Woo.” \n\n\r\n              Allen threw a 34-yard touchdown pass to Joshua Palmer with 1:36 remaining to lift the Bills to a 36-31 victory in a back-and-forth game.\n\n\r\n              Allen had 334 yards passing with two touchdowns and ran for two more scores after losing his previous four games at Reliant Stadium.\n\n\r\n              “It feels good to start the season off right,” Allen said. \n\n\r\n              It was the Bills' first win at Houston since Nov. 19, 2006. \n\n\r\n              The Texans led 31-30 when Allen split two defenders to find Palmer. The 2-point conversion failed.\n\n\r\n              One play earlier, Houston's Kamari Lassiter got his hands on a pass from Allen in the end zone but couldn’t grab the interception.\n\n\r\n              “We got lucky,” Palmer said. “He dropped it. Next play I caught it.”\n\n\r\n              The Texans had a chance to win in the closing seconds, but Greg Rousseau had his second strip-sack of the day, taking down C.J. Stroud at the Buffalo 28. Terrel Bernard recovered to seal the victory.\n\n\r\n              It was a disappointing outing for Houston's vaunted defense, which led the NFL in yards allowed and ranked second in points allowed last season.\n\n\r\n              “We had multiple opportunities to make plays, had chances to make plays on the football and we didn’t make plays,” Texans coach DeMeco Ryans said.\n\n\r\n              Houston allowed its most points since a 36-22 loss to Cleveland on Dec. 24, 2023.\n\n\r\n              “We’ve got to do a better job, especially closing out the game,” defensive end Will Anderson said. “It just wasn’t our standard. That’s not who we are. That’s not who we want to be as a defense. ”\n\n\r\n              Dalton Kincaid had five receptions for 130 yards and DJ Moore had five catches for 100 yards and a touchdown in his Buffalo debut after a trade from Chicago.\n\n\r\n              Buffalo's Joe Brady started his head coaching career with a win after he was promoted from offensive coordinator following Sean McDermott’s firing after nine seasons.\n\n\r\n              “You saw a lot of the conviction, everything we sort of talk about,” Brady said. “We didn’t care how pretty. There’s no such thing as an ugly win.”\n\n\r\n              David Montgomery, who was traded from Detroit this offseason, had a three-TD day in his Houston debut. He had scoring runs of 1 and 18 yards in the first half and his 7-yard touchdown reception put Houston up 28-27 early in the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Stroud threw for 274 yards and two touchdowns but lost two fumbles.\n\n\r\n              Tyler Bass' 33-yard field goal made it 30-28 Buffalo with 10 1/2 minutes to go.\n\n\r\n              Ka'imi Fairbairn’s 58-yard field goal put Houston back ahead with about seven minutes remaining before Allen’s game-winning drive.\n\n\r\n              Buffalo went up 10-7 when Allen scrambled for a 21-yard touchdown early in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Montgomery’s second score came when he scampered 18 yards untouched to make it 14-10 with about five minutes left in the first half.\n\n\r\n              Lassiter was flagged for pass interference on Moore in the end zone to give the Bills the ball on the 1 on their next drive. Allen’s scoring dive two plays later put Buffalo up 17-14.\n\n\r\n              Moore grabbed a ball in front of a diving Reed Blankenship and dashed 43 yards to the end zone later in the second to push the lead to 24-14.\n\n\r\n              A 12-yard touchdown reception by Nico Collins cut the lead to three at halftime.\n\n\r\n              Rousseau sacked Stroud on Houston’s first drive, causing a fumble that Ed Oliver recovered on the Houston 32. That led to Bass' 46-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              A 1-yard run by Montgomery put the Texans up 7-3 near the end of the first quarter. The score was set up by a 25-yard reception by Collins three plays earlier.\n\n\r\n              Bills' receiving duo puts up big numbers\r\n              Moore and Kincaid gave the Bills just the second game in franchise history where a tight end and a receiver both had 100 yards receiving. The first time it happened was when Andre Reed had 144 yards receiving and Pete Metzelaars added 122 in Week 2 of the 1992 season. \n\n\r\n              Brady raved about what Moore brings to the team.\n\n\r\n              “He opens it up for everybody,” Brady said. “He opens it up for Dalton Kincaid, for the run game and James Cook. He opens it up for Keon Coleman... and Josh Palmer. And then on big-play opportunities, he made them. We’re so much better with him.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Houston LB Jake Hummel left in the second half with an abdominal injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Bills: Host Detroit on Thursday night in their first game at their new stadium.\n\n\r\n              Texans: Host Cincinnati next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/7e2157ef-ca97-4e2f-987d-8d31346053ae/7e2157ef-ca97-4e2f-987d-8d31346053ae.jpg"},"401872928":{"h":"Kirk Cousins hits 301 career TD passes as Raiders open the season with a 27-13 win over Dolphins","d":"— Kirk Cousins was brought in to be Las Vegas' present and a bridge to a future where top draft pick ","b":"LAS VEGAS -- — Kirk Cousins was brought in to be Las Vegas' present and a bridge to a future where top draft pick Fernando Mendoza is the Raiders' starting quarterback.\n\n\r\n              Cousins, now in his 15th season, quickly showed Sunday why the Raiders acquired him from Atlanta for that role, completing his first 10 passes and effectively running the offense in a 27-13 season-opening victory over the Miami Dolphins.\n\n\r\n              He became the 15th quarterback to throw at least 300 touchdown passes in his career after tossing three TDs. Cousins completed 21 of 30 passes for 160 yards, though he was intercepted twice. \n\n\r\n              He has 301 touchdown passes for his career, moving into 14th place and one TD ahead of John Elway.\n\n\r\n              “I think it's just a testament to trying to win the long game, trying to have good habits over a long period of time,” Cousins said. “I feel like it's also a testament to the people I've been able to work with — the coaches, the players around me. I'm really grateful for all the people involved in being able to do that.”\n\n\r\n              Cousins is the first Raiders quarterback to throw at least three TDs in the opener since Kerry Collins in 2005 against New England.\n\n\r\n              Two of his TD passes went to running back Ashton Jeanty, who also rushed for 102 yards and caught six passes for 45 more. This was the third career 100-yard game for Jeanty, the No. 6 overall pick in the 2025 NFL draft who showed no ill effects of an ankle injury he suffered on Aug. 23.\n\n\r\n              The Raiders totaled 290 yards in their first game under coach Klint Kubiak, the offensive coordinator on Seattle's Super Bowl-championship team last season. It was a noticeable improvement for an offense that last season was last in the league in yardage (245.2 per game) and scoring (14.2 points).\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas did it without star tight end Brock Bowers, who didn't play after undergoing knee surgery. Michael Mayer stepped up and caught six passes for 32 yards in Bowers' absence.\n\n\r\n              But Kubiak, who received a game ball in the locker room for his first career victory as a head coach, wasn't pleased with how the offense finished the game. Other than the final possession, when the Raiders went into victory formation, their four fourth-quarter drives ended in three punts and an interception.\n\n\r\n              “We have a lot to get better at, but I'm very excited for our team and our organization,” Kubiak said.\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas is about to embark on a six-game gauntlet against teams such as Kansas City, Buffalo and the Los Angeles Rams that will test how much the offense truly has improved.\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins, the league's youngest team with an average age of 25.5, weren't much of a test. But expectations aren't high for Miami this season under first-year coach Jeff Hafley.\n\n\r\n              He said he was pleased with his players' fight and the chance to make it a one-possession game late.\n\n\r\n              “We have to coach better and we need to play better,” Hafley said. “A lot of guys were out there who were out there for the first time. Some of them had to take a deep breath and settle in a little bit.”\n\n\r\n              Malik Willis, under pressure much of the game, passed for 220 yards with an interception. He was sacked five times, with 1 1/2 by Maxx Crosby, who became the third Raider to record at least 70 for his career since sacks became an official statistic in 1982.\n\n\r\n              “You can blame the sacks on me,” Willis said. “Sack is a quarterback stat.”\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas never trailed in taking advantage of a rare true home-field advantage. The Raiders used a 16-play, 93-yard opening drive that ate up 8:58 to take a 7-0 lead. They had just one TD drive of 90-plus yards all last season.\n\n\r\n              They led 17-3 at one point before the Dolphins cut the advantage to 17-13 early in the third quarter. But then Dylan Laube returned the ensuing kickoff 50 yards to midfield to set up Cousins' 13-yard scoring pass to Jeanty to again assume control.\n\n\r\n              “That's what we expect from our guys, no head down, no ‘woe is me,’” Kubiak said. “It's easy for a game to go the other way, and I was proud of our players, of how they responded and how the coaching staff got them ready to go for the next round.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Dolphins: LB Ronnie Harrison Jr. (hamstring) was injured in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Raiders: CB Darien Porter (foot) was injured in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Dolphins: Visit San Francisco on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Raiders: Visit the Los Angeles Chargers on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/50ae4155-a12e-4405-87d8-4c19f9deef41/50ae4155-a12e-4405-87d8-4c19f9deef41.jpg"},"401872927":{"h":"Vikings close with 29 straight points to beat rival Packers as Wentz replaces injured Murray","d":"— Carson Wentz has had little time to practice with Minnesota's voluminous playbook, a late arrival to the team last season who returned this year in more of a mentor role as Kyler Murray and J.J. McCarthy competed for the starting quarterback job ","b":"MINNEAPOLIS -- — Carson Wentz has had little time to practice with Minnesota's voluminous playbook, a late arrival to the team last season who returned this year in more of a mentor role as Kyler Murray and J.J. McCarthy competed for the starting quarterback job in training camp.\n\n\r\n              The Vikings brought the 11th-year veteran back for emergencies like these. \n\n\r\n              Wentz came in after Murray's first-quarter concussion in his Minnesota debut and led a spirited comeback against the rival Green Bay Packers, as the Vikings scored 29 straight points to close a 39-22 victory to open the season on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              “That's why he's here,” coach Kevin O'Connell said. “For him to come in and do what he did and just be part of that calming presence that allowed our team to continue playing, I can't say enough about him.”\n\n\r\n              Wentz had two touchdown passes to Justin Jefferson, matching the two-time All-Pro's 2025 season total, and the pair connected on a 2-point conversion when the Vikings took their first lead at 25-22 with 6:37 remaining. Green Bay extended the drive three times with crushing defensive penalties.\n\n\r\n              “Football is a funny game,” Wentz said, “but it's fun to be on this side of it.”\n\n\r\n              Jordan Love, who had been brilliant against a relentlessly blitzing defense, wilted along with the rest of the team by losing a fumble on a sack and throwing an interception deep in Packers territory.\n\n\r\n              Aaron Jones and Jordan Mason each had rushing touchdowns and T.J. Hockenson caught one of the three scores thrown on a turnover-free afternoon by Wentz, who was appointed the backup to Murray ahead of the 2024 first-round draft pick McCarthy for his considerable edge in experience.\n\n\r\n              Wentz helped the Vikings convert eight of 15 third downs and posted a 123.5 passer rating in another memorable comeback among many in five seasons under O'Connell, who has yet to win a playoff game.\n\n\r\n              “This one will stick with me for a long time,” O'Connell said.\n\n\r\n              Momentum shifted when the Packers were stopped on fourth-and-1\r\n              Love finished 21 for 42 for 387 yards and two first-half touchdowns to Christian Watson, but the two late turnovers and a 3-for-12 conversion rate on third downs loomed large as the Packers picked right up where they left off with a five-game losing streak that ended last season.\n\n\r\n              The Packers lost four times after leading in the second half by at least two possessions, including their wild card playoff collapse against another NFC North rival, the Chicago Bears, who outscored the Packers 25-6 in the fourth quarter of that game.\n\n\r\n              Green Bay coach Matt LaFleur might have mistakenly triggered this meltdown with his decision to take rookie Trey Smack's field goal off the board to set up a fourth-and-1 on an offside penalty. Love was stuffed at the line with a five-point lead, and the Vikings went the other way for the go-ahead score by Jones, the former Packers favorite who had yet to reach the end zone against his former team.\n\n\r\n              The Packers actually four defensive penalties on that drive, starting with a seemingly innocent encroachment call on Jonathan Ford.\n\n\r\n              Javon Bullard, whose injurious hit on Murray was flagged, was called for illegal contact to negate a third-down sack. Rookie Chris McClellan was penalized for a late hit on Wentz to wipe out a third-down incompletion. Then Xavier McKinney's defensive holding call erased an end-zone interception by Evan Williams, and Jones scored on the next play.\n\n\r\n              Though the penalties on McClellan and McKinney in particular were contested by the Packers, they had only themselves to blame for this collapse.\n\n\r\n              “Momentum is a strong thing,” Watson said, “and we didn’t respond.” \n\n\r\n              Injury report\r\n              Murray seemed to be doing well after the game, O'Connell said, but the timetable for his return was unknown. ... Packers LG Aaron Banks (knee) was inactive, giving Donovan Jennings his first career start.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Packers: at New York Jets on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              Vikings: at Chicago Bears on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0913/fc6b598489a7470cb56c7dd1e7a063be1156/fc6b598489a7470cb56c7dd1e7a063be1156.jpg"},"401872929":{"h":"Jalen Hurts throws 3 TD passes and Eagles stuff Commanders' late 2-point try for 24-22 win","d":"— Jalen Hurts can count on a few guarantees in each season opener.","b":"PHILADELPHIA -- — Jalen Hurts can count on a few guarantees in each season opener.\n\n\r\n              For sure, a new offensive coordinator. Though as long as Hurts is connecting on passes and extending drives with game-saving scrambles, the play-caller in his ear rarely seems to matter.\n\n\r\n              Hurts threw for 203 yards and three touchdowns, and the Eagles' Moro Ojomo stuffed the Commanders' attempt at a tying 2-point conversion with 1:01 remaining as Philadelphia squeaked past Washington 24-22 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “It’s just about meeting the moment when your opportunity comes,” Hurts said.\n\n\r\n              Plenty of his teammates met the moment when it was their time to deliver.\n\n\r\n              Jayden Daniels threw a 1-yard TD pass to on third-and-goal to Antonio Williams with 1:01 left for the Commanders' second touchdown of the fourth quarter. On the 2-point try, Washington called a shovel pass to tight end John Bates, who was wrapped up instantly by Ojomo.\n\n\r\n              “I got off on the ball,” Ojomo said. “Took a shot. You kind of got to play it free.”\n\n\r\n              Here's another guarantee: The Eagles improved to 6-0 in season openers under coach Nick Sirianni and Hurts since 2021.\n\n\r\n              Hurts had his moments for the two-time reigning NFC East champions in Sean Mannion's debut as offensive coordinator. Mannion is Hurts’ fifth coordinator in the last five seasons and their partnership showed early promise for the former Super Bowl MVP.\n\n\r\n              Mannion's system also worked out well for tight end Dallas Goedert, who had four catches for 77 yards and two touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              The early big play came late in the first quarter when Hurts had multiple targets open on a play-action and connected with Dontayvion Wicks for a 64-yard reception. Wicks, acquired in an April trade from Green Bay, had the longest reception of his career and gave the first signal that perhaps the Eagles will be just fine at wideout after trading disgruntled star A.J. Brown to New England.\n\n\r\n              Hurts finished the drive with a dart down the middle to Goedert for a 17-yard score and a 7-3 lead. If the Eagles had questions at wide receiver — and new No. 1 receiver DeVonta Smith had only two targets in the first half, and just three catches overall — they are fine at tight end. Goedert, who had a career-high 11 TD catches last season, again proved himself a trusted target.\n\n\r\n              Hurts added a 9-yard TD pass to Wicks in the second quarter for a 14-3 lead as the Eagles ran a clean, crisp offense under Mannion. Saquon Barkley, who tailed off last season after rushing for more than 2,000 yards in 2024 when the Eagles won the Super Bowl, ripped off a 42-yard run to set up Wicks' TD.\n\n\r\n              Yet the offense largely cooled off behind a shaky line that gave Hurts little time to throw. Meanwhile, the defense — anchored by all-everywhere linebacker Zack Baun and his 12 tackles — rarely let Daniels break free long enough to sting the Eagles until the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Daniels — who went 18 of 34 for 164 yards — avoided a sack, spun and scrambled for a 13-yard gain on the final play of the third quarter. He had time in the pocket to hit a wide-open Williams for a 33-yard gain and then hit Stefon Diggs after the veteran receiver beat Cooper DeJean in the slot for a 10-yard touchdown.\n\n\r\n              Diggs, whose string of personal issues made him available even after he had 99 catches for 1,123 yards for the AFC champion New England Patriots, was flagged for unsportsmanlike conduct when he jumped and bear-hugged the goalpost before he landed on his back.\n\n\r\n              Coach Dan Quinn said the Commanders would have gone for 2, but the 15-yard penalty forced them to settle for the extra point that made it 17-16.\n\n\r\n              Hurts extended the next drive with a 22-yard scramble on third-and-18 and hit Goedert on the next drive for a 43-yard score and a 24-16 lead.\n\n\r\n              “I think one of the things that makes Jalen such a special quarterback is that he can do it through the air. He can do it running the show,” Sirianni said. “He can do it with his legs when he needs to make plays, and obviously, that was a big play, and you need those.”\n\n\r\n              Drew Stevens kicked a 55-yard field goal for the Commanders and Jacory Croskey-Merritt added a 1-yard run that cut the Eagles' lead to 14-9 at halftime. Jalen Carter blocked the extra point.\n\n\r\n              “The miscues I thought hurt us in this game and those were the margins right there, division games or otherwise,” Quinn said.\n\n\r\n              Jake Elliott’s 35-yard field with 44 seconds left in the third quarter stretched the lead to 17-9.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Commanders: LB Frankie Luvu suffered a groin injury. TE Chip Okonkow had a hamstring injury. S Tyler Owens injured an ankle.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Commanders: Keep it in the NFC East with a game next Sunday at Dallas.\n\n\r\n              Eagles: At Tennessee next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0913/688b3c3ab08f46ceb5270ee7ef3a71811201/688b3c3ab08f46ceb5270ee7ef3a71811201.jpg"},"401872926":{"h":"Cardinals beat Chargers 26-14 as Mike LaFleur wins his head coaching debut","d":"— Coming off an injury-riddled three-win season and with a new coaching staff, the Arizona Cardinals were the NFL's biggest underdog going into Week 1.","b":"INGLEWOOD, Calif. -- — Coming off an injury-riddled three-win season and with a new coaching staff, the Arizona Cardinals were the NFL's biggest underdog going into Week 1.\n\n\r\n              Surprise!\n\n\r\n              Led by rookie head coach Mike LaFleur, the Cardinals defeated the Jim Harbaugh-led Los Angeles Chargers 26-14 on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              LaFleur became the first Cardinals coach to win his debut since Don Coryell in 1973. He won in the same SoFi Stadium where he worked his final game as the Los Angeles Rams’ offensive coordinator last year before being hired by Arizona in February. \n\n\r\n              “These last few days have been almost miserable waiting,” he said. “Just really happy for those guys.”\n\n\r\n              LaFleur spent his first 12 years in the NFL toiling as an assistant with five different teams. His older brother, Matt, is starting his eighth season as head coach of the Green Bay Packers. Harbaugh's older brother, Jim, coaches the New York Giants.\n\n\r\n              Jacoby Brissett threw for 277 yards and a touchdown for Arizona, which added 18 new players and several new assistants.\n\n\r\n              “I’m just so happy that we were able to go on the road and get a win,” Brissett said. “As a starting quarterback, it’s like, ‘Whew!’”\n\n\r\n              The Chargers were the NFL's heaviest favorite by 9 1/2 points, but they played from behind the entire game and had a miserable fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              “It’s not the start that we wanted, but credit to the Cardinals,” Harbaugh said. “It’ll be about our resolve, the way we bounce back.”\n\n\r\n              Chad Ryland had four field goals as the Cardinals won on the road against the Chargers for the first time since 2001. His only miss was a 54-yarder when the ball hit the right upright in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers made a mess of the fourth quarter. On their first three possessions, they had a blocked punt, Justin Herbert was intercepted and Los Angeles turned the ball over on downs on fourth-and-goal from the 3-yard line. They had another turnover on downs with less than 2 minutes remaining.\n\n\r\n              “It’s understood that every first week is kind of like a training camp game,” linebacker Daiyan Henley said. “You never know what’s going to come at you, but you got to be prepared for anything.”\n\n\r\n              Herbert was 17 of 27 for 209 yards with a touchdown and an interception, and was sacked three times.\n\n\r\n              “They played well, but it’s on us to play better,” Herbert said. “We didn’t play the way we wanted to.”\n\n\r\n              The Cardinals dominated possession, 37 minutes to 22, and ran 72 plays to 51 for the Chargers and compiled 393 yards of offense to 268.\n\n\r\n              Arizona scored on its first possession when Jeremiyah Love, the No. 3 overall pick, stiff-armed Cam Hart in the left corner of the end zone for a 5-yard TD run.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers answered with a 9-yard TD run by Omarion Hampton. He got stoned at the 5-yard line, but kept going as four Cardinals failed to stop him, tying it at 7.\n\n\r\n              Arizona led 10-7 on Ryland's 27-yard field goal in the second. \n\n\r\n              Herbert got sacked by Dante Stills on fourth-and-goal with two minutes left before halftime. The Cardinals got a 42-yarder by Ryland after Paris Johnson Jr.'s 15-yard face mask penalty to lead 13-7.\n\n\r\n              After the Chargers were forced to punt on their first possession of the third, Arizona got a 34-yarder by Ryland to extend its lead to 16-7.\n\n\r\n              Los Angeles closed to 16-14 on Herbert's 8-yard pass to Ladd McConkey, who barely got into the corner of the end zone. The Chargers were helped by Max Melton's 48-yard defensive pass interference penalty.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Chargers: McConkey took a hard hit to his hip in the third quarter. S Elijah Molden injured a hamstring. \n\n\r\n              Up next \r\n              Cardinals: Host defending Super Bowl champion Seattle next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Chargers: Host Las Vegas next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0913/d3056127-565e-4b49-ad61-d36d5850fe70/d3056127-565e-4b49-ad61-d36d5850fe70.jpg"},"401872930":{"h":"Giants' offense rolls over the Cowboys to give coach John Harbaugh a 28-20 win in his debut","d":"— Several months before coaching his first game for the New York Giants, John Harbaugh told fans he wanted to build a team that could beat...","b":"EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. -- — Several months before coaching his first game for the New York Giants, John Harbaugh told fans he wanted to build a team that could beat the Dallas Cowboys.\n\n\r\n              It took one game to accomplish that feat.\n\n\r\n              Jaxson Dart connected with Isaiah Likely for two touchdowns and Devin Singletary on another, Cam Skattebo ran for one in his first game back from injury and the Giants opened the season by rolling over Dallas 28-20 on Sunday night in Harbaugh's New York debut. It's just one victory, but it was a promising start of a new era.\n\n\r\n              “We took steps in that direction,” Harbaugh said. “I think we’re on our way.”\n\n\r\n              Dart made one smart throw after another, mixing in a series of well-timed runs to keep the defense off balance. He finished 23 of 29 for 230 yards passing, plus 54 rushing, including a couple of slides and a dive to avoid the unnecessary hits that punctuated his rookie year.\n\n\r\n              “Just seeing his arm talent, just seeing how he takes over the game — using his legs, making the right decisions, knowing the coverages,” No. 1 receiver Malik Nabers said. “He was not backing down. He was not going to lose, and I was right behind him.”\n\n\r\n              Likely made eight catches on eight targets for 78 yards in his New York debut after following Harbaugh from Baltimore, extending the training camp trend when he was consistently Dart's top option. Nabers, playing for the first time since going down with a torn ACL in his right knee 350 days ago, had six catches for 69 yards.\n\n\r\n              “He's just a guy where no matter what the coverage is, no matter who they put out there on him, you have all the confidence in the world that he's going to make a play,” Dart said. \n\n\r\n              Back on the field more than 10 months since breaking his right fibula and dislocating his right ankle in gruesome fashion, Skattebo rumbled for 81 yards on 18 carries. After getting in hot water for doing a backflip earlier this summer, Skattebo brought back his signature celebration after running into the end zone untouched for the Giants' first points of the season.\n\n\r\n              Dak Prescott threw a costly interception just before halftime but otherwise played well for Dallas, completing 22 of 34 passes for 175 yards and TD tosses to CeeDee Lamb and Javonte Williams, who also ran for a touchdown. Dallas benefitted from a series of defensive pass interference calls that extended drives.\n\n\r\n              The Cowboys hurt themselves with penalties — eight of them for 85 yards, not counting an early intentional delay of game. That included a taunting call on Williams after he scored that contributed to Brandon Aubrey missing the ensuing extra point from farther out and a face-masking flag on Donovan Ezeiruaku just before the two-minute warning.\n\n\r\n              “It comes down to discipline, and we had some self-inflicted wounds,\" coach Brian Schottenheimer said. “I own that, and they own that.”\n\n\r\n              Their defense also did not look as improved on defense as they had hoped, at least in Week 1. After allowing the most points in the NFL last season at 30.1 a game, this was not far off, despite a new coordinator and some additional talent.\n\n\r\n              “Overall as a football team, we were pretty poor,” Schottenheimer said. “That’s certainly not our standard. We certainly didn’t come out of the gate fast enough on either side of the ball. This is a humbling business, and we got humbled.”\n\n\r\n              Fumblin', bumblin', recoverin'\r\n              The Giants fumbled four times but only lost one: by Tyrone Tracy, who also put the ball on the ground during the preseason and missed a block that got Dart hit to go into Harbaugh's doghouse. Fullback Patrick Ricard fell on Likely's in the first quarter, guard Jon Runyan caught Dart's in the second and Skattebo recovered his own in the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Harbaugh makes a hat switch\r\n              The Giants honored 9/11 victims 25 years later in a pregame ceremony, and Harbaugh paid tribute on the sideline. He wore an NYPD hat in the first half, then switched to an FDNY cap for the second.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Cowboys: Safety Malik Hooker hurt a forearm making a tackle on the final play of the third quarter. ... Linebacker DeMarvion Overshown left with a hamstring injury early in the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Giants: Cornerback Deonte Banks was in and out during the second half while cramping up. ... Fellow cornerback Greg Newsome was evaluated for a concussion and cleared.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Cowboys: Host Washington next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Giants: Visit the Los Angeles Rams on Monday, Sept. 21.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0913/3525824f5e0c4ea3b35c35162f8c958c1425/3525824f5e0c4ea3b35c35162f8c958c1425.jpg"},"401872931":{"h":"Walker runs all over Denver, Mahomes looks healthy as Chiefs beat up Broncos 31-10","d":"— So many people seemed to be focused on Chiefs quarterback Patrick Mahomes, and how the two-time MVP's surgically repaired left...","b":"KANSAS CITY, Mo. -- — So many people seemed to be focused on Chiefs quarterback Patrick Mahomes, and how the two-time MVP's surgically repaired left knee would hold up in Kansas City's season opener against the Broncos, that they forgot all about Kenneth Walker III.\n\n\r\n              The reigning Super Bowl MVP won't be surprising anyone else anytime soon.\n\n\r\n              Walker ran for 173 yards and scored two touchdowns in his Chiefs debut, helping to take some of the pressure off Mahomes in his first game back, and Kansas City exacted some revenge on Denver for stealing its AFC West crown with a 31-10 victory Monday night.\n\n\r\n              Walker carried 23 times to finish with the most yards rushing by a Chiefs player since Jamaal Charles ran for 226 against the Colts in 2012, the year before Andy Reid became their coach. The ex-Seahawks star also had three catches for 18 yards.\n\n\r\n              “He did a really nice job,” Reid said, “and you never know going in how many times you're going to give him the ball. That's not a calculated thing. You have to be making yards. For him to play the way he did I thought was spectacular.”\n\n\r\n              Mahomes wasn't a slouch, either. With a brace protecting his left knee, he threw for 184 yards and two scores while also running for another score, helping the Chiefs snap a three-game skid against the rival that ended their nine-year run as division champs.\n\n\r\n              “They were in the AFC championship game for a reason,” Mahomes said. “For us to go out and win like that, that's the first step. It shows all the work in training camp paid off. But how can we be better?”\n\n\r\n              Good question.\n\n\r\n              The Chiefs helped to make Denver positively abysmal on offense in its first game with coordinator Davis Webb calling the plays.\n\n\r\n              Bo Nix threw for 131 yards with an interception and a lost fumble, and the Broncos finished with 176 yards of offense, all despite the offseason splash they made by trading a package of draft picks to Miami for wide receiver Jaylen Waddle.\n\n\r\n              Waddle had one catch for 2 yards. Evan Engram led the Broncos with four catches for 43 yards and their lone TD.\n\n\r\n              “As a whole offense, a whole team, we just didn’t play really good,” Nix said.\n\n\r\n              “Obviously not a good evening,\" Broncos coach Sean Payton added.\n\n\r\n              The looming question, even after Mahomes participated in the entire offseason program and every practice of training camp, was how his knee would hold up nine months to the day since he was hurt in a game against the Chargers.\n\n\r\n              He erased any doubts when he capped the Chiefs' opening drive with a 15-yard touchdown run.\n\n\r\n              “Once you put him in the game,” Reid said, “he's going to play, and you better be confident in that.”\n\n\r\n              It was Walker who did most of the running, though. The Super Bowl MVP for Seattle, who signed a three-year, $43.05 million deal in free agency, had 14 carries for 89 yards in the first half alone, along with a couple of catches out of the backfield.\n\n\r\n              Even when the Broncos tied the game on Nix's 19-yard touchdown pass to Engram, Walker assumed a heavy load as the Chiefs pieced together a 10-play, 80-yard drive late in the half. It ended when Mahomes found Rashee Rice, fresh off a knee injury of his own, and he caromed off a couple of Broncos defenders and into the end zone to make it 14-7 at the break.\n\n\r\n              Then it was Walker's turn to bounce off a defender, turning a fourth-and-1 carry into a 60-yard TD run to start the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              “I knew they were going to crash in. When they crashed in, I bounced out,” Walker said, “and I was off to the races from there.”\n\n\r\n              The teams traded field goals later in the quarter thanks to big plays by each side: Brandon Jones picked off Mahomes deep in Chiefs territory, setting up Wil Lutz's chip shot, and Mahomes connected with Travis Kelce for 59 yards to set up Harrison Butker's answer.\n\n\r\n              Nix's fumble on the ensuing possession allowed the Chiefs to put the game away.\n\n\r\n              Mahomes hit rookie running back Emmett Johnson on third down for a 26-yard gain, setting up first-and-goal at the Denver 2. And in the kind of creative — dare say, fun? — play that was reminiscent of the Chiefs' banner years, Mahomes lined up in a three-point stance alongside backup QB Justin Fields, took the snap and shoveled the ball to Walker, who scooted untouched for the score.\n\n\r\n              It was a play that Reid and his staff found in some black-and-white footage from the 1930s, and it worked flawlessly.\n\n\r\n              “It was hard to see,” Walker said of the film, “but we worked it over and over in practice. We felt it was going to work and it did.”\n\n\r\n              Arrowhead over Emmys\r\n              Taylor Swift chose Arrowhead Stadium over the Emmy Awards to cheer on Kelce. The pop star married the Chiefs tight end on July 3. Swift, who watched alongside Tom Cruise, had been nominated for outstanding variety special (pre-recorded) for “The Eras Tour: The Final Show,” but the category winner — “The Muppet Show” — had been announced earlier this month.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Broncos: WR Marvin Mims left briefly with a hip injury but returned.\n\n\r\n              Chiefs: CB Mansoor Delane landed on his right shoulder after an interception on the third play of the game. The No. 6 pick in the NFL draft was helped to the locker room and did not return. ... LB Cooper McDonald (knee) left in the first half and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Denver plays its home opener against Jacksonville on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Kansas City hosts Indianapolis on Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0914/41443ef7093647b7812736357855922f1406/41443ef7093647b7812736357855922f1406.jpg"},"401872932":{"h":"Josh Allen opens the Bills' new stadium with a 5-TD outing in a 41-31 win over the Lions","d":"— Forget the official name of the Buffalo Bills' new stadium. It’s Josh Allen’s house.","b":"ORCHARD PARK, N.Y. -- — Forget the official name of the Buffalo Bills' new stadium. It’s Josh Allen’s house.\n\n\r\n              The quarterback quickly put his stamp on Highmark Stadium by throwing three touchdown passes and running for two more scores in a 41-31 victory over the Detroit Lions on Thursday night in the first regular-season game at the $2.1 billion facility.\n\n\r\n              And the only time Allen felt emotional was just before kickoff, during the performances of the U.S. and Canadian national anthems.\n\n\r\n              “I caught myself taking it all in an understanding how cool what I get to do. My job is the coolest in the world,” Allen said. “It’s everything I ever wanted, to be the quarterback of an NFL franchise. I’m just very fortunate to have ended up in Buffalo.”\n\n\r\n              After rewriting many of Buffalo's scoring and passing records during his first eight seasons across the street at the old Highmark Stadium, the 30-year-old Allen looked at home at the team’s new digs.\n\n\r\n              Allen scored the first touchdown at the new stadium on a 1-yard plunge 5:51 minutes in. His second rushing TD, a 2-yard run, was the 83rd of his career tying him for 18th on the NFL list with Jim Taylor. And his sixth career five-TD outing, followed a four-touchdown performance in Buffalo’s season-opening 36-31 win at Houston.\n\n\r\n              “Anytime the ball’s in his hands, good things happen. And yeah, love that guy,” said Bills coach Joe Brady, who was so confident in his offense, he elected to take the opening kickoff after winning the coin flip.\n\n\r\n              “I wanted us to take the ball in this environment and set the tone,” Brady said. “That was the start that we needed.”\n\n\r\n              The Bills scored on each of their first four possessions with Josh Palmer’s 43-yard TD catch, followed by Dawson Knox’s 2-yard reception and Dalton Kincaid’s 16-yarder.\n\n\r\n              Allen finished 20 of 31 for 248 yards, and added 69 yards rushing. James Cook had 134 yards rushing and put the game away with a 1-yard TD run with 2:50 left.\n\n\r\n              Buffalo’s 41 points were the third-most by a team in its regular-season stadium debut, behind only Dallas, which scored 44 in its first game at Texas Stadium in 1971, and Miami scoring 42 in its first outing at Joe Robbie Stadium in 1987.\n\n\r\n              The Lions were undone by an injury depleted defense, and a banged-up offensive line that had difficulty opening holes for Jahmyr Gibbs and protecting Jared Goff.\n\n\r\n              “You play an opponent as potent as this, you don’t have the luxury of spitting and sputtering early,” coach Dan Campbell said of Detroit falling behind 21-0 with 11:48 left in the second quarter. “We needed to play. It didn’t have to be spotless, but it certainly needed to be a lot cleaner than that.”\n\n\r\n              The Lions’ defense got off to a dreadful start in allowing Bills to score 27 points and gain 299 yards in the first half alone, and 446 overall.\n\n\r\n              After rallying to cut Buffalo’s lead to 34-24 on Sam LaPorta's 1-yard TD catch four minutes into the fourth quarter, the Lions offense stalled on their next drive. Backed up at their own 10, the Lions went three-and-out and forced to punt after Goff had two passes tipped by Deone Walker, and Gibbs was stopped for no gain.\n\n\r\n              The Bills responded by putting the game away on Cook's TD.\n\n\r\n              Goff finished 26 of 38 for 327 yards and four touchdowns, with Amon-Ra St. Brown having nine catches for 142 yards and two scores. Gibbs also scored on an 11-yard catch but was limited to 52 yards rushing. Sam LaPorta also scored on a 1-yard catch.\n\n\r\n              “I think we knew what type of game it was going to be, and we dug ourselves in that hole,” Goff said. “Against a team like that with the offense they have, it was really tough to get back int it.”\n\n\r\n              St. Brown and Gibbs have now scored in the same game 22 times to move into a tie for fourth place in matching the Cleveland receiver/running back combo of Paul Warfield and Leroy Kelly\n\n\r\n              The Lions troubles began on Buffalo’s second drive when two defenders converged on DJ Moore in the flats and allowed Allen to find Palmer wide open deep for a 43-yard touchdowns. On Buffalo’s next drive, a holding penalty against Rock Ya-Sin, negated Skyler Gill-Howard’s interception of a tipped pass.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Lions: DB Avonte Maddox hurt his right foot during a three-player collision in the end zone in the second quarter and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Bills: Starting DL Ed Oliver was ruled out after hurting his hip during pre-game warmups, leaving the Bills with four healthy linemen. ... WR DJ Moore was ruled out after hurting his shoulder and head when Ya-Sin landed on the player attempting to make a catch in the end zone.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Lions: Host the New York Jets on Sept. 27.\n\n\r\n              Bills: Host New England on Sept. 27.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              This story has been corrected to show that Buffalo's opening win came at Houston.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0918/ss_20260918_082552050_3664378/ss_20260918_082552050_3664378.jpg"},"401872933":{"h":"Young throws 3 TD passes, including 2 to Waller, as Panthers dominate short-handed Falcons 34-3","d":"— In his previous visit to Atlanta, Bryce Young commanded the spotlight.","b":"ATLANTA -- — In his previous visit to Atlanta, Bryce Young commanded the spotlight.\n\n\r\n              This time, Young shared the glory with Devin Lloyd and the Carolina defense. \n\n\r\n              Young threw for 287 yards and three touchdowns, including two to Darren Waller, Lloyd returned one of his two interceptions for a touchdown and the Panthers routed the short-handed Atlanta Falcons 34-3 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers (1-1) earned their fourth straight win in the NFC South rivalry. Young completed 23 of 36 passes as he improved to 5-1 against the Falcons. He set a franchise record with 448 yards passing in a 30-27 overtime win to cap last season's sweep.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers defense enjoyed a dramatic turnaround one week after setting franchise records for points and rushing touchdowns allowed in a 59-37 loss to Chicago.\n\n\r\n              “It was a collective effort,” said coach Dave Canales. “We knew they were going to try to run the ball after last week. And the defense came together. We shored up some things.” \n\n\r\n              Lloyd had nine tackles, one sack and three pass deflections in addition to two of the Panthers' three interceptions.\n\n\r\n              “Man, it was special,” said Young of Lloyd's performance. \"He’s a leader. He does all the little things that, you know, people don’t see. For it to come out in a performance like that, it is really cool to see. I don’t know if I’ve been a part of something that cool. ... I mean, that’s surreal. It was really fun to watch.”\n\n\r\n              With fill-in starter Cooper Rush at quarterback, the Falcons (0-2) had more offensive woes following their 20-13 opening loss at Pittsburgh last week. Michael Penix Jr. (knee) and Tua Tagovailoa (oblique) again were inactive.\n\n\r\n              Rush completed 10 of 17 passes for 86 yards with two interceptions and also lost a fumble.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers held the Falcons to only 286 yards, including 72 rushing yards by Bijan Robinson. The Falcons’ only points came on Nick Folk’s 41-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              Connecting with Waller\r\n              Waller, 34, has developed a quick chemistry with Young after signing with Carolina on Aug. 13. He had three catches for 33 yards, including the scoring catches of 12 and 7 yards.\n\n\r\n              “He has played so much ball, so obviously he’s a pro,” Young said. \"Us getting on the same page quickly has been just a lot of conversation ... being able to have a guy that’s played so much ball that I trust.”\n\n\r\n              No Rush to assign blame\r\n              Following his first home regular-season game as Atlanta's coach, Kevin Stefanski said Rush did not deserve all the blame for the loss.\n\n\r\n              “Cooper’s giving us everything he’s got, and unfortunately, you know, we turned the ball over there, including on the first play, which should never be a turnover,” Stefanski said. “So those are the types of things ... those are correctable. We’ve got to get them fixed.”\n\n\r\n              Stefanski would not announce a starting quarterback for Thursday night's game at Green Bay.\n\n\r\n              Booing the Birds\r\n              Rush's struggles from his very first snap were so dramatic that some Falcons fans were booing six minutes into the game. He lost a fumble on a 9-yard run to open the game. Atlanta’s second possession ended when Rush’s pass intended for Robinson was intercepted by linebacker Bobby Okereke. There were more boos during the game.\n\n\r\n              “I love our fans a lot, every single one of them,” Robinson said. “If we let ourselves focus on the boos and things like that, that will creep into our mind and that will make us have a bad performance. I know that just comes with the game. It's our job for them to not boo.”\n\n\r\n              Rush was replaced by undrafted rookie Jack Strand late in the third quarter, prompting an ovation from Falcons fans. Strand's first possession ended badly as his pass for Jahan Dotson was intercepted by Lloyd and returned 16 yards for a touchdown. Strand lost a fumbled snap on his final possession.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Panthers safety Nick Scott (ribs) left in the first quarter and was ruled out. Linebacker Claudin Cherelus suffered a shoulder injury on kickoff coverage in the first quarter and was listed as questionable. Linebacker Trevis Gipson Sr. returned after being evaluated for a possible concussion. Running back Jonathon Brooks aggravated a groin injury. Defensive tackle Bobby Brown III (back) was inactive.\n\n\r\n              Falcons cornerback A.J. Terrell, who had been questionable with a shoulder injury, left in the first quarter with a groin injury. Linebacker Samson Ebukam (hamstring) left late in the first half. RT Jawaan Taylor (ribs) was listed as questionable in the fourth quarter. Right guard Chris Lindstrom (concussion protocol) started after being listed as questionable. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Panthers: at Cleveland next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Falcons: at Green Bay on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0921/7b9df6f4-6b43-4438-bdeb-806ba8f5eccc/7b9df6f4-6b43-4438-bdeb-806ba8f5eccc.jpg"},"401872937":{"h":"Rodgers and Turner help the Vikings shut down the Bears for a 9-3 win in the rain","d":"— Dallas Turner threw Tyson Bagent to the ground, then took off down the field in celebration. It rained on his parade, for sure, but that was just fine with him.","b":"CHICAGO -- — Dallas Turner threw Tyson Bagent to the ground, then took off down the field in celebration. It rained on his parade, for sure, but that was just fine with him.\n\n\r\n              Turner had two of Minnesota's four sacks, including his takedown of Bagent on the final play, and the Vikings shut down Caleb Williams and the Chicago Bears for a damp 9-3 victory on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “I feel like, just preparing myself for these moments, ever since I was in college,” said Turner, a third-year linebacker who also had a sack during a 39-22 win over Green Bay in Week 1. “I feel like it’s very important, just keep on showing up every single day to execute at a high level.”\n\n\r\n              Isaiah Rodgers recovered a fumble and blocked a late field-goal attempt as Minnesota improved to 2-0 for the first time since it opened the 2024 season with five consecutive wins. Aaron Jones rushed for 105 yards and Will Reichard kicked three field goals, including a 39-yarder with 1:09 left.\n\n\r\n              The Vikings earned their seventh consecutive win at Soldier Field, matching the franchise record for consecutive road wins over a division opponent. They won seven straight at Detroit from 1968-74 and seven straight on the road against Green Bay from 1971-77.\n\n\r\n              “Defense was unreal. It was a gritty game, gritty game for the whole team,” Minnesota quarterback Carson Wentz said.\n\n\r\n              Williams left with a right hamstring injury midway through the fourth quarter, silencing a drenched crowd of 58,914. The No. 1 pick in the 2024 draft was 15 for 26 for 138 yards, and he had five carries for 42 yards.\n\n\r\n              “We’ll see how bad it is tomorrow and we’ll go from there,” Bears coach Ben Johnson said.\n\n\r\n              Johnson called the plays as Chicago (1-1) rolled to 291 yards rushing and 552 yards overall in a 59-37 victory at Carolina in Week 1. It was the highest-scoring season opener in NFL history.\n\n\r\n              But Johnson and the Bears were stymied by Vikings defensive coordinator Brian Flores and his usual aggressive approach. Led by Turner and Blake Cashman, Minnesota limited Chicago to 297 total yards. Cashman had 10 tackles and a sack, and Byron Murphy Jr. intercepted an ill-advised throw by Williams in the first quarter.\n\n\r\n              “I really loved the game plan from the time Flo and I sat down on Tuesday, and just kind of the way him and the staff wanted to attack, wanted to make sure that we were just playing really good sound defense,\" Vikings coach Kevin O'Connell said. \n\n\r\n              Williams got hurt on a scramble deep in Minnesota territory. The third-year quarterback, who passed for two touchdowns and rushed for two more in the opener, was helped off the field.\n\n\r\n              Bagent came in and passed to D’Andre Swift for 1 yard before throwing an incomplete pass on third down. Johnson then sent Cairo Santos on for a 23-yard field goal, but it was blocked by Rodgers.\n\n\r\n              After Wentz drove Minnesota to Reichard’s third field goal, Chicago had one last opportunity. Bagent found Rome Odunze for 21 yards and Luther Burden for 8 to move the ball to the Vikings 12.\n\n\r\n              Following a pair of incompletions, Bagent was wrapped up by Turner on the final play.\n\n\r\n              “Man, D.T. just keeps showing up,” O'Connell said.\n\n\r\n              Wentz was 11 for 20 for 143 yards, and Justin Jefferson had three receptions for 55 yards. Wentz got the start with Kyler Murray working his way through the NFL concussion protocol.\n\n\r\n              “It’s been an interesting offseason for me personally and everything, but I knew I was here for a reason,” Wentz said. “I knew if called upon, I could help this team win games.” \n\n\r\n              Trailing 6-3, Williams drove Chicago to first-and-goal on the 1 on the first drive of the second half. But Swift fumbled on a hit by Jalen Redmond and Rodgers recovered for Minnesota (2-0).\n\n\r\n              “Even at the end we had a chance to win the game,” Swift said. “If I don’t give the ball up and we get points right there it might be a different outcome for us. I’ll be better.”\n\n\r\n              The teams exchanged field goals on the opening drives of the game. Jefferson’s 30-yard reception helped set up Reichard’s 35-yarder, and Williams’ 19-yard run was the big play in Chicago’s drive to Santos’ 50-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Vikings: DL Tyrion Ingram-Dawkins suffered a right hip injury. ... Cashman left with 10:35 left in the third but he returned. ... O'Connell said he thinks Murray will clear concussion protocol early this week.\n\n\r\n              Bears: CB Tyrique Stevenson had a hamstring injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Vikings: At the Tampa Bay Buccaneers next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bears: Host the Philadelphia Eagles on Sept. 28.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/654a2d93-9bcf-418a-b762-49dde81d9493/654a2d93-9bcf-418a-b762-49dde81d9493.jpg"},"401872939":{"h":"Jalen Hurts hits Darius Cooper for TD with 9 seconds left to lift the Eagles past the Titans 24-20","d":"— Philadelphia coach Nick Sirianni had only one thought with his offense on the field on a day so hot he needed intravenous fluids to cool off.","b":"NASHVILLE, Tenn. -- — Philadelphia coach Nick Sirianni had only one thought with his offense on the field on a day so hot he needed intravenous fluids to cool off.\n\n\r\n              Play to win.\n\n\r\n              Jalen Hurts threw a 3-yard touchdown pass to Darius Cooper with 9 seconds left, and the Eagles beat the Tennessee Titans 24-20 on a steamy Sunday when the temperature on the artificial turf was measured at 157 degrees Fahrenheit.\n\n\r\n              “They fought and they clawed and they scratched and did anything they had to do to get the win,” Sirianni said of his Eagles.\n\n\r\n              Philadelphia (2-0) appeared ready to dominate Tennessee. The Eagles opened the game by stringing together three straight drives of at least 11 plays.\n\n\r\n              The Titans (0-2) sacked Hurts three times and picked him off twice, turning those into 10 points. Marcus Harris' interception set up Joey Slye's 33-yard field goal with 1:56 left, putting Tennessee up 20-17. \n\n\r\n              Hurts had 1:56 to drive the Eagles 65 yards for the win. He finished with 264 yards passing and two TDs. Hurts called the drive the result of execution, focus and perseverance. \n\n\r\n              “I’m not one to harp on the elements, but it was a challenge for us as a unit collectively,” Hurts said. “And I’m happy with how we weathered through that.”\n\n\r\n              The TD catch was the first of Cooper's career. DeVonta Smith had 10 catches for 117 yards, Tank Bigsby ran for a TD, and Jake Elliott kicked a 58-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              Cam Ward got the Titans to midfield, but his final pass under pressure hit the turf harmlessly as time expired.\n\n\r\n              “It’s not a moral victory, it’s a loss,” Ward said. “That’s all it comes down to.”\n\n\r\n              Philadelphia improved to 15-4 in September since 2021 and remained tied with Buffalo for the most wins in the NFL's opening month in that span. The Eagles had plenty of fans in the stands, making it feel more like a home game.\n\n\r\n              The air temperature reached 98 degrees Fahrenheit (37 Celsius) in the fourth quarter and felt like 106 degrees. It was so hot the Titans squirted water on players’ cleats on the sideline to cool them off. Eagles left tackle Jordan Mailata said he dismissed talk of the Titans' cleats melting last week.\n\n\r\n              “It’s like a very deceiving heat out there,” Mailata said. “It just sucks everything out of you.”\n\n\r\n              The Titans hadn't started a season with back-to-back home games since 2010. It didn't help new coach Robert Saleh as Tennessee lost its first two for a third straight season. Saleh said he thought his Titans outplayed the Eagles at times.\n\n\r\n              “They executed better in critical moments,” Saleh said.\n\n\r\n              Ward threw for 183 yards and ran for a pair of 1-yard TDs. \n\n\r\n              Three-time Pro Bowl running back Saquon Barkley suffered a stinger in his left arm on the opening play, though he returned to start the third quarter. He finished 9 yards on four carries, his fourth career game held under 10 yards rushing.\n\n\r\n              Philadelphia seemed ready to run the Titans off their own field, starting the game with a 15-play drive. But Hooker picked off Hurts’ pass to Smith in the end zone and set up Ward’s 1-yard TD run for the lead. \n\n\r\n              The Eagles led 14-10 at halftime despite holding the ball for nearly 22 minutes. Slye pulled the Titans closer with a 37-yard field goal as the second quarter expired.\n\n\r\n              Elite company for Sirianni and Hurts\r\n              The Eagles are 2-0 for the fourth time under Sirianni, which is the most by a coach in franchise history. Sirianni became just the fourth NFL coach to win the first six road openers of a career, joining Sean McVay and George Allen (seven) and Mike McCarthy (six), according to the Elias Sports Bureau. \n\n\r\n              Hurts trails Roger Staubach (eight) as the only NFL quarterbacks to start their careers winning at least their first six road openers. Hurts is the fifth quarterback in NFL history to win six straight road openers during any stretch of their career, joining Dan Fouts (nine), Staubach (eight), Bart Starr (seven) and Fran Tarkenton (seven).\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Eagles TE Dallas Goedert hurt a knee midway through the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              The Eagles visit Chicago on Monday, Sept. 28.\n\n\r\n              The Titans visit the New York Giants next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/90e36a9d-5357-43f6-8301-8b9bec7b81b1/90e36a9d-5357-43f6-8301-8b9bec7b81b1.jpg"},"401872946":{"h":"TreVeyon Henderson runs for a TD and the Patriots pummel Aaron Rodgers in a 20-3 win over Steelers","d":"— The New England Patriots have yet to find much of the offensive rhythm that propelled their Super Bowl run last season. The defense was there to pick up the pieces against the ","b":"FOXBOROUGH, Mass. -- — The New England Patriots have yet to find much of the offensive rhythm that propelled their Super Bowl run last season. The defense was there to pick up the pieces against the Pittsburgh Steelers.\n\n\r\n              Elijah Ponder had a 19-yard fumble recovery for a score, TreVeyon Henderson ran for a 39-yard touchdown in his season debut and New England (1-1) smothered Aaron Rodgers on the way to a 20-3 win over Pittsburgh on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              With the victory, Patriots coach Mike Vrabel avoided just the second 0-2 start of his eight-year coaching career.\n\n\r\n              The former linebacker has his defense to thank for that.\n\n\r\n              A year after leading the Steelers (1-1) to a win in New England, Rodgers struggled to deal with the Patriots’ pressure. The 42-year-old quarterback fumbled twice, including the one that led to Ponder’s score. \n\n\r\n              “That has to be the formula, we have to affect the quarterback each and every week,\" Vrabel said.\n\n\r\n              Patriots cornerback Christian Gonzalez shadowed Steelers receiver DK Metcalf at times, forcing Rodgers to look for other options in the passing game. It was an assignment Gonzalez was anticipating all week.\n\n\r\n              “As soon as I see the schedule,” Gonzalez said. “That’s what you love about being a corner, going out there and competing against the best.” \n\n\r\n              Drake Maye finished 14 of 22 for 208 yards and an interception. Romeo Doubs, who had no catches in his Patriots debut last week, had a 63-yard catch in the third quarter to set up Andy Borregales’ second field goal of the game.\n\n\r\n              “It is nothing to hang your hat on. It is early on, but at the same time, we have got to have some urgency and a little fire under us,” Maye said.\n\n\r\n              Rodgers finished 23 of 39 for 187 passing yards, an interception and was sacked four times. Metcalf had only four catches for 27 yards.\n\n\r\n              “We just didn't get into a rhythm. We had too many three-and-outs again,\" Rodgers said. \"I don't feel like there was anybody that had an exceptional game out there. We all played below our standard.”\n\n\r\n              The Patriots moved to the Pittsburgh 34 on their opening drive before Rhamondre Stevenson was stripped on a run near the sideline by linebacker Nick Herbig, giving the ball back to the Steelers. Stevenson also lost two fumbles in last season’s loss to Pittsburgh. \n\n\r\n              But a 15-yard taunting penalty on Jalen Ramsey on the play pushed the start of the ensuing drive back to the Steelers 16, and Pittsburgh quickly went three-and-out. \n\n\r\n              Following a 19-yard punt return by Marcus Jones, the Patriots took over with Henderson replacing Stevenson. Three plays later, Henderson found a crease and sprinted free for a 39-yard TD run.\n\n\r\n              Leading 13-3 in the fourth quarter, New England’s defense dialed up more pressure on Rodgers, first with Christian Barmore’s strip-sack that led to Ponders’ TD. On the Steelers’ next series, Rodgers was sacked for the third time on the day, forcing a quick three-and-out.\n\n\r\n              The Steelers were threatening on the Patriots 17 with less than five minutes to play before a short pass by Rodgers intended for Germie Bernard was bobbled and intercepted by rookie linebacker Gabe Jacas.\n\n\r\n              The Steelers' stale offense\r\n              The Steelers now have scored just one touchdown in 25 possessions through two games.\n\n\r\n              Coach Mike McCarthy said fixing that starts with being more creative and he put the onus on himself. \n\n\r\n              “We’ve got to give Aaron better platforms to throw off of and have the opportunities. I don’t want to dink and dunk and play keep away football,” McCarthy said. \n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Steelers: S Jaquan Brisker was shaken up in the first quarter but walked off on his own power. RB Rico Dowdle left in the second quarter with a toe issue. \n\n\r\n              Patriots: RG Mike Onwenu left late in the first quarter after having his right ankle rolled up on by T.J. Watt as he dove in to assist a sack of Drake Maye. Onwenu got up on his own power but had to hop to a waiting cart. ... LB Dre’Mont Jones (shoulder), TE Eli Raridon (thigh), S Dell Pettus (ankle) and CB Marcus Jones all exited in the second quarter. ... S Craig Woodson (shoulder) exited in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Steelers: Host the Bengals next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Patriots: Visit Jaguars next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0920/237e98d775384dd192c4bdb229ad207f1015/237e98d775384dd192c4bdb229ad207f1015.jpg"},"401872936":{"h":"Trey Smack’s OT kick lifts the Packers past the Jets, ending a 6-game skid","d":"— Jordan Love and the Green Bay Packers were down 10 points in the fourth quarter, piling penalties and staring at a seventh consecutive loss.","b":"EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. -- — Jordan Love and the Green Bay Packers were down 10 points in the fourth quarter, piling penalties and staring at a seventh consecutive loss.\n\n\r\n              They somehow pulled this one out — in front of a raucous crowd of white towel-waving New York Jets fans and despite all the yellow flags dropping in between the raindrops that drenched MetLife Stadium on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Trey Smack kicked a 26-yard field goal in overtime and the Packers bounced back from a sloppy performance to snap a six-game skid with a 20-17 victory.\n\n\r\n              “It’s a huge win,” Love said. “It’s one of those games where you just have to keep fighting. It wasn’t pretty a lot of that game.” \n\n\r\n              Love shook off a hit to his elbow that led to some numbness in his right hand to throw two touchdown passes for the Packers (1-1). Green Bay had 14 accepted penalties for 133 yards and appeared on its way to another ugly loss after a fourth-quarter collapse last week in a 39-22 loss at Minnesota during which it had 12 penalties.\n\n\r\n              “In two weeks, we’ve had a lot of penalties that have put us back,” Love said. “Those are things that stop drives, put you behind the sticks. It’s tough to overcome.” \n\n\r\n              But it was the Jets (1-1) who couldn't hold on after mostly outplaying the Packers and stifling Love and the offense for much of the game.\n\n\r\n              “I'll be honest, I'm highly upset,” New York coach Aaron Glenn said. \"Tough way to lose a game. There's a number of things that go through my head that, man, we have to fix. ... This loss is not going to define who we are and what we’re about, I will say that. It’s not OK and our guys are going to take it like that.”\n\n\r\n              The Packers also overcame a scary start when wide receiver Jayden Reed and right tackle Zach Bako-Bewele were carted off the field with injuries on consecutive plays on the opening drive. \n\n\r\n              Reed has a neck injury, but had movement in his extremities and coach Matt LaFleur said he'd stay overnight at a hospital for observation. Bako-Bewele hurt a knee and LaFleur said it “doesn't look good.” \n\n\r\n              The Jets won the overtime coin toss and chose to get the ball, but Geno Smith was sacked on consecutive plays and Isaiah Williams was called for a false start, making it third-and-30. Kenyon Sadiq caught a 12-yard pass, but New York was forced to punt.\n\n\r\n              Kaleb Johnson had runs of 11 and 3 yards, and then Love connected with Chris Brooks for a 15-yard gain — and Jarvis Brownlee Jr. was called for unnecessary roughness for hitting him late. After Matthew Golden's 14-yard reception put the ball at the 8, LaFleur opted for the field goal — and Smack sent the Packers home happy. \n\n\r\n              “For our guys to continue to battle, hopefully that will give us some momentum moving forward,” LaFleur said. “It was tough sledding there for about three quarters.” \n\n\r\n              Love finished 16 of 29 for 145 yards with TD tosses to Christian Watson and MarShawn Lloyd for the Packers, whose six-game skid — including the playoffs — was their longest since dropping seven in a row spanning the 1990 and 1991 seasons.\n\n\r\n              Smith was 27 of 41 for 247 yards and a touchdown pass to Garrett Wilson for the Jets, who were also sloppy in their home opener; they were called for 13 accepted penalties 156 yards. Braelon Allen had a 5-yard TD run.\n\n\r\n              Jets appeared to be in control of the game\r\n              Smith threw a 5-yard touchdown pass to Wilson to give the Jets a 14-7 lead late in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              The Jets' stifling defense forced a turnover early in the fourth when Kingsley Enagbare knocked the ball out of Lloyd's hands and Harrison Phillips recovered. It was the fourth fumble forced by the Jets in the game, but the first they recovered.\n\n\r\n              The drive stalled and New York settled for Jason Sanders' 32-yard field goal to go up 17-7 with 11:04 remaining.\n\n\r\n              Green Bay came right back when Love threw a 3-yard touchdown pass to Watson on a play-action fake.\n\n\r\n              After Skyy Moore returned a punt 63 yards to the Jets 19, Smack kicked a 34-yarder to tie it with 2:59 remaining.\n\n\r\n              Green Bay took a 7-0 lead with 9:35 left in the second quarter when Love threw a dump-off pass to Lloyd, who wriggled his way into the end zone for a 6-yard touchdown. It was the first NFL touchdown for Lloyd, a 2024 third-round pick who played in just one game during his first two seasons because of injuries.\n\n\r\n              The Jets responded on their next possession with Allen's touchdown run, tying it at 7. \n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Packers: G Aaron Banks injured a toe and Donovan Jennings, his replacement, hurt a hand. DT Anthony Campbell (ankle) was injured in the first half. WR Bo Melton left in the third quarter with a stinger.\n\n\r\n              Jets: LBs Marcelino McCrary-Ball (ankle) and Kiko Mauigoa (quadriceps) and DL David Onyemata (groin) were ruled out after halftime. TE Mason Taylor hurt a thumb in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Packers: Host the Atlanta Falcons on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              Jets: Face the Lions in Detroit next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/3f35a747-1cd7-4b84-84e0-140615e4d17a/3f35a747-1cd7-4b84-84e0-140615e4d17a.jpg"},"401872935":{"h":"Deshaun Watson outplays Baker Mayfield, leading the Browns to a 23-19 win over the Buccaneers","d":"— Deshaun Watson outplayed Baker Mayfield and earned at least a few more starts for the Cleveland Browns.","b":"TAMPA, Fla. -- — Deshaun Watson outplayed Baker Mayfield and earned at least a few more starts for the Cleveland Browns.\n\n\r\n              Watson threw two touchdown passes, including a go-ahead 17-yard toss to Blake Whiteheart in the fourth quarter, leading the Browns to a 23-19 comeback victory over the Tampa Bay Buccaneers on Sunday in a game that was delayed two hours, 12 minutes because of lightning. \n\n\r\n              Even though Browns coach Todd Monken wouldn't name him the starter for Cleveland's home opener against Carolina next week, Watson shouldn't have to worry about Shedeur Sanders taking his job this week.\n\n\r\n              “I'm so happy for him,” Monken said of Watson. “Good for him. Good for 22 months working his (tail) off to get to where he's at. I'm gonna leave it at that.”\n\n\r\n              Before starting a lopsided loss to Jacksonville last week, Watson hadn't played since 2024 because he tore his Achilles tendon twice. He was critical of Browns fans in the preseason, leading to speculation that he wouldn't start at home unless he played well. \n\n\r\n              He erased those thoughts by going 24 of 30 for 238 yards with no turnovers and a 121.9 passer rating in his best game since Sept. 24, 2023, against Tennessee. \n\n\r\n              “I'm just happy we got the win,” he said. “I'm right where I'm supposed to be. I'll leave the journey in God's hands and let the outcome be the outcome.”\n\n\r\n              The Buccaneers (0-2) were driving and had a second-and-4 at the Cleveland 28 when the game was suspended at the two-minute warning. \n\n\r\n              When it finally resumed, heavy rain started falling again.\n\n\r\n              Bucky Irving ran twice and Mayfield's pass to Ted Hurst got the Bucs to the 13 before a holding penalty set them back. Mayfield's pass on fourth-and-10 from the 20 to Emeka Egbuka in the end zone was out of his reach, sealing the win for the Browns (1-1).\n\n\r\n              Watson and Mayfield faced each other for the first time in a meeting between two quarterbacks who are linked together. \n\n\r\n              Mayfield, who was selected No. 1 overall by the Browns in 2018, was traded to Carolina after a blockbuster trade brought Watson and his off-field troubles to Cleveland in 2022. He was eventually released by the Panthers during that season and played for the Rams before rejuvenating his career in Tampa Bay, where he has made two Pro Bowls and received a $165 million, three-year contract extension earlier this month.\n\n\r\n              “Frustrated because it's close,” Mayfield said of the offense's woes. “We have to be self-critical, look at the tape, get stuff fixed and not make the same mistakes over and over.”\n\n\r\n              Watson is 10-11 in five seasons with the Browns and seemed to be running out of time as a starter before this effort.\n\n\r\n              Monken had a message for people who claimed this was the same old Browns after a 34-10 loss to the Jaguars.\n\n\r\n              “The Browns are gonna Browns? (Expletive) that,” Monken said. \n\n\r\n              Watson connected with Denzel Boston on a 55-yard TD pass to tie it at 16 late in the third quarter. Boston caught a slant, broke three tackles and sprinted into the end zone.\n\n\r\n              Watson converted four times on third down on the drive that ended with his TD pass to Whiteheart that gave the Browns a 23-19 lead with 3:51 left in the game.\n\n\r\n              Tampa Bay’s Chase McLaughlin and Cleveland’s Andre Szmyt combined to go 7-for-7 on field goals.\n\n\r\n              McLaughlin connected from 59, 45, 31 and 31 yards. Szmyt was good from 50, 34 and 34 yards.\n\n\r\n              Mayfield willed the Buccaneers into the end zone on the opening drive of the second half. He stiff-armed his way to a first down on third-and-9. On the next play, Mayfield eluded a sack, stepped up and threw a 5-yard touchdown pass to Egbuka, who had slipped and made the catch sitting on the ground.\n\n\r\n              Mayfield threw one interception that Cleveland turned into three points.\n\n\r\n              The Bucs got their best field position at the Cleveland 34 after Elijah Roberts stopped Quinshon Judkins on fourth-and-1. But the drive stalled after Tampa Bay reached the 11 and settled for a field goal that made it 9-6.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Browns: S Ronnie Hickman left in the fourth quarter. … CB Tyson Campbell left the game in the first half but returned.\n\n\r\n              Buccaneers: Rookie LB Josiah Trotter was shaken up but walked off the field in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Browns: Host the Carolina Panthers on Sept. 27.\n\n\r\n              Buccaneers: Host the Minnesota Vikings on Sept. 27.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/01373b70-7853-4859-acd7-b5775fed1ee0/01373b70-7853-4859-acd7-b5775fed1ee0.jpg"},"401872938":{"h":"Tyler Shough and the Saints produce two big drives at the end to topple the Ravens 24-17","d":"— Twice in a row, Tyler Shough disappeared into a pile of bodies at the goal line with the game tied late in the fourth quarter.","b":"BALTIMORE -- — Twice in a row, Tyler Shough disappeared into a pile of bodies at the goal line with the game tied late in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Officials ruled he was stopped on third down and scored on fourth — it could well have been the reverse — and that was good enough for the New Orleans Saints.\n\n\r\n              Shough scored on a fourth-down sneak with 1:28 remaining, and the Saints rallied to a 24-17 win over the Baltimore Ravens on Sunday. After Shough didn't score on third down, New Orleans elected to go for it on fourth instead of kicking for a three-point lead. The second sneak was called a touchdown, and that decision was upheld after a review.\n\n\r\n              “It's a hard play on the goal line because ... sometimes they lose sight of the football. I think it's a challenging play for everyone,” Saints coach Kellen Moore said. “Fortunate for us to get that. Obviously we're going to go for it in that situation. Chance to score a touchdown. If not, we're forcing them to be on the minus-1 and go the long way.”\n\n\r\n              New Orleans (1-1) had first down from the 2 before a penalty and a costly incomplete pass allowed Baltimore — with only two timeouts left — to stop the clock after the next two plays. Shough finally scored on a 1-yard sneak.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens, who didn't have a first down on either of their previous two drives, made it to the New Orleans 37, but Lamar Jackson's deep pass was intercepted by Julian Blackmon with 13 seconds left.\n\n\r\n              Baltimore had a 17-9 lead and forced a three-and-out with sacks by Trey Hendrickson and Mike Green. That gave the Ravens the ball at the New Orleans 49 late in the third quarter with a chance to make it a two-score game.\n\n\r\n              But Baltimore went backwards 16 yards and punted, then the Saints drove to tie it with 9:42 remaining on a 21-yard touchdown pass from Shough to Chris Olave. After a penalty on the Ravens (1-1) moved the 2-point conversion to the 1-yard line, Travis Etienne Jr. ran the ball in to make it 17-all.\n\n\r\n              After another Baltimore three-and-out — Jackson curiously threw long on third-and-1 and his pass to Chris Moore was broken up by Kool-Aid McKinstry — the Saints took over at their own 32 with 8:06 remaining. \n\n\r\n              “A play that we liked, thought we had a shot there for an explosive,” Ravens coach Jesse Minter said. “Obviously, didn’t work out, so it’s always going to be, you know, looking at it after the fact.”\n\n\r\n              New Orleans then drove about as methodically as possible, converting two third downs and two fourth downs to seize the lead.\n\n\r\n              “We'd like to not make it as close,” Shough said.\n\n\r\n              The Saints had to sweat out a review on the touchdown.\n\n\r\n              “We exhausted all available angles and could not prove clear and obvious that he did not break the plane of the goal line,” Mark Butterworth, NFL vice president of instant replay, told a pool reporter.\n\n\r\n              Baltimore beat Indianapolis 41-23 in Minter's debut as coach, but in Week 2 the Ravens did many of the things that got John Harbaugh's staff fired, collapsing in the fourth quarter and dropping yet another home game. Baltimore was 3-6 at M&T Bank Stadium last season.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens were without top receiver Zay Flowers against the Saints. Baltimore did have offensive linemen Ronnie Stanley and John Simpson after their status was in question, but Stanley (toe) left the game in the second half.\n\n\r\n              New Orleans lost left tackle Kelvin Banks Jr. to an ankle injury, but at the end the Saints moved the ball just fine.\n\n\r\n              Derrick Henry opened the scoring with a 1-yard run in the first quarter for Baltimore. After the Saints decided to kick a field goal on fourth-and-3 from the 5, Baltimore drove 80 yards and took a 14-3 lead on Jackson's 22-yard scoring strike to Rashod Bateman with 24 seconds left in the half.\n\n\r\n              Barion Brown's 53-yard kickoff return set up a New Orleans field goal before the end of the second quarter, and the Saints started the third with another three points on a 51-yard kick by Daniel Carlson. Tyler Loop connected from 49 for the Ravens to make it 17-9.\n\n\r\n              Saints go down to the wire, again\r\n              New Orleans lost in overtime at Detroit in Week 1 when its attempt at a game-winning 2-point conversion failed. This one came down to a huge play near the goal line as well.\n\n\r\n              The Saints trailed 21-0 in the second half before rallying to force OT against the Lions. Against the Ravens, they had 10 first downs to Baltimore's two in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              “Our guys stuck with it all the way to the end, both games,” Moore said. “For both games to come down to one opportunity at the end, one possession, it's a good experience for our guys.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              In addition to Banks, the Saints lost cornerback Martin Emerson Jr. to a shoulder injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Saints: Host Las Vegas next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Ravens: Face Dallas in Rio de Janeiro on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/a26c6c8b-bf2f-4d9e-94a2-6043a4a191d5/a26c6c8b-bf2f-4d9e-94a2-6043a4a191d5.jpg"},"401872934":{"h":"Ja'Marr Chase scores 2 TDs on passes from Joe Burrow to lead the Bengals past the Texans 20-6","d":"— Ja'Marr Chase reminded everyone of just how good he can be on Sunday after the five-time Pro Bowler was a non-factor in Cincinnati's season-opening win.","b":"HOUSTON -- — Ja'Marr Chase reminded everyone of just how good he can be on Sunday after the five-time Pro Bowler was a non-factor in Cincinnati's season-opening win. \n\n\r\n              Chase caught two touchdown passes from Joe Burrow to lead the Bengals to a 20-6 win over the Houston Texans.\n\n\r\n              The big day by Chase came a week after he had just two receptions for 12 yards in a victory over Tampa Bay. That prompted Burrow, coach Zac Taylor and offensive coordinator Dan Pitcher to say that getting him the ball more had to be a priority against the Texans.\n\n\r\n              They showed they meant that early as Chase eclipsed last week’s performance with 14 yards receiving on Cincinnati’s first drive and didn’t slow down after that.\n\n\r\n              Chase said he wasn't concerned about his limited touches in Week 1 since the team got a win.\n\n\r\n              “We got weapons on this team at the end of the day,” he said. “So, I’m gonna just hold myself accountable and try to do whatever I can to make the team win.”\n\n\r\n              He put the Bengals (2-0) on top 7-0 when he managed to drag his toe inbounds on a spectacular 32-yard TD catch over Derek Stingley with about 3 1/2 minutes left in the first half. \n\n\r\n              Burrow said the catch was one of the best he's ever seen.\n\n\r\n              “I thought he was falling down,” he said. “I was about to argue for a pass interference and then somehow he stayed up and contorted his body and got two feet in. It was incredible.” \n\n\r\n              Chase called it the second-best catch in his career behind a TD grab he had in the Super Bowl as a rookie. \n\n\r\n              He also gave Burrow credit for his ball placement on the throw.\n\n\r\n              “He was still a little on top of me and I had to just give myself a position,” Chase said. “Joe threw a good enough ball to where the DB couldn’t get to it.”\n\n\r\n              The Bengals were up by seven when Burrow found Chase again. This time, he grabbed an 18-yard pass in the end zone in front of a diving Reed Blankenship to make it 17-3 with about three minutes left in the third.\n\n\r\n              Taylor was impressed with how Chase handled the week and by his performance Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “We’ve been in enough games together to know that can happen in this offense sometimes, and what’s important is the win, and Ja'Marr was a captain through the whole thing,\" Taylor said. \"I didn’t hear one word out of him. He’s positive. He’s great, helping everybody else. He knew this stuff’s going to come.”\n\n\r\n              Chase had seven receptions for 75 yards and Burrow was 20 of 31 for 207 yards despite dealing with back tightness that caused him to miss practice time this week. Tee Higgins added five receptions for 95 yards. \n\n\r\n              It was another disappointing game for the Texans, who are 0-2 after losing to Buffalo 36-31 in the opener. They’ll need to get back on track next week to avoid starting 0-3 for a second year in a row.\n\n\r\n              C.J. Stroud was 30 of 55 for 353 yards passing on a day the Texans had trouble running the ball. Their backs finished with just 18 yards rushing. They failed to score a touchdown while playing without star receiver Nico Collins after scoring 31 points last week.\n\n\r\n              “The one key to this victory was controlling the front and from an offensive perspective we did not control their front,” Houston coach DeMeco Ryans said. “We got outplayed up front. We couldn’t run the ball well and that forced us into a lot of passing situations.” \n\n\r\n              Kaʻimi Fairbairn’s 45-yard field goal got the Texans within 17-6 near the end of the third.\n\n\r\n              Evan McPherson’s 57-yard field goal pushed the lead to 20-6 early in the fourth.\n\n\r\n              The Texans were driving on the next possession when Stroud was flagged for intentional grounding to force a punt.\n\n\r\n              A 37-yard field goal by Fairbairn cut the lead to 7-3 with just over a minute until halftime.\n\n\r\n              McPherson’s 51-yard field goal made it 10-3 early in the third.\n\n\r\n              The Texans had some miscues in the first half that put them in bad spots. They fumbled the snap on a punt early in the second quarter and failed to convert on fourth-and-inches from the Cincinnati 18 when Dexter Lawrence stuffed David Montgomery on their next drive.\n\n\r\n              “When you’re playing the Houston Texans, it’s not always going to be pretty,” Taylor said. “That’s a team that I respect a lot, how they’re built, how they’re coached. And we knew this was going to be a really difficult, challenging game, and to come out with a 20-6 win, I’m proud of that.”\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Bengals: Visit the Steelers next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Texans: Visit the Colts next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/623b6828-39c4-4f43-be89-403d2ddd4830/623b6828-39c4-4f43-be89-403d2ddd4830.jpg"},"401872940":{"h":"Jaylen Waddle’s quiet Denver debut flips into a 138-yard day in a Broncos' 20-13 comeback over Jags","d":"— Same winning formula.","b":"DENVER -- — Same winning formula. \n\n\r\n              New faces doing the heavy lifting.\n\n\r\n              The Denver Broncos closed their game on a 17-point blitz Sunday for a 20-13 win over Jacksonville that snapped the Jaguars ' nine-game regular-season winning streak, and leading the way were trade acquisition Jaylen Waddle and rookie running back Jonah Coleman.\n\n\r\n              The Broncos (1-1) had 11 one-score wins last season and a dozen comebacks. After a season-opening dud at Kansas City in which Waddle and Coleman struggled, the duo came up big as the Broncos powered past Trevor Lawrence the Jaguars after falling behind 13-3 early in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              Waddle caught eight passes for 138 yards a week after catching just one pass for 2 yards in his Denver debut, and Coleman's first NFL score was a tiebreaking 3-yard touchdown run in the fourth quarter. He salted the game away with heady play down the stretch as the Jaguars burned all their timeouts but failed to get the ball back.\n\n\r\n              Waddle “showed his swagger all day,” and Coleman displayed his “great football IQ,” said Broncos quarterback Bo Nix, who improved to 24-11 as a starter, the same record John Elway had after 35 games. \n\n\r\n              “I’m glad he’s ours,” Broncos coach Sean Payton said of Waddle, the speedster the Broncos acquired from Miami for a first-round pick ahead of the NFL draft last April. \n\n\r\n              And he was glad he could turn to Coleman, a fourth-round pick out of Washington, who was pressed into duty with J.K. Dobbins and R.J. Harvey both out with hamstring injuries.\n\n\r\n              After allowing a field goal to open the second half, the Broncos (1-1) responded with a field goal, a 7-yard touchdown toss from Nix to Nate Adkins and Coleman's first career TD, which came with 7:21 remaining after Dobbins got hurt.\n\n\r\n              The Jaguars (1-1) had won their past nine regular-season games — by a combined 177 points — including a 34-20 statement win in Denver last December. \n\n\r\n              After Coleman's touchdown broke a 13-all tie, Denver's defense, which missed a whopping 28 tackles against the Chiefs in Week 1, forced a punt. The Broncos offense chewed up the final 4:41 behind Coleman's six carries for 31 yards, including a 12-yarder in which he he slid down inbounds at the 18 just ahead of the two-minute warning, allowing Nix to finish the win.\n\n\r\n              “He came up big when we needed it,” Payton said of Coleman, who played just three snaps and had zero carries in the opener but led the Broncos with 39 yards on 10 carries Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Lawrence led Jacksonville on three scoring drives as the Jaguars built a 13-3 lead on Bhayshul Tuten's 1-yard TD run and two field goals by Cam Little, from 44 and 23 yards. Little was wide left on a 54-yard try in the fourth quarter before Denver's go-ahead touchdown drive. \n\n\r\n              The Broncos started out slow, failing to punch the ball into the end zone after Waddle's 49-yard reception at the 1, settling instead for Wil Lutz's 20-yard field goal. And Nix was picked off in the end zone by Jourdan Lewis, which led to Little's 44-yarder as time expired, giving Jacksonville a 10-3 halftime lead.\n\n\r\n              The Jaguars stalled at the Denver 5-yard line on the opening drive of the second half and Little's 23-yarder made it 13-3. \n\n\r\n              The Jaguars caught a big break on the game's opening drive when an illegal contact flag on cornerback Riley Moss negated a strip-sack by Nik Bonitto and recovery by Alex Singleton deep in Jacksonville territory. Moss had a big interception late in the third quarter that led to Denver's tying touchdown.\n\n\r\n              “It was a good day for the team to bounce back,\" Moss said, “but you know there’s still things that we’ve got to work on.” \n\n\r\n              Ditto for the Jaguars, whose coach, Liam Coen, said he was pleased with the start but “the fourth quarter was when you needed a drive, right? You needed a stop, you needed a catch, you needed a play, we just didn’t do it. We just didn’t make it.”\n\n\r\n              “Definitely disappointing,\" Lawrence said. \"I felt like we played the opposite way in the second half than we did in the first half.”\n\n\r\n              So did Denver.\n\n\r\n              Mile High comeback\r\n              Tim Tebow returned to Empower Field at Mile High for the first time since throwing an 80-yard touchdown pass to the late Demaryius Thomas on the first play of overtime against the Pittsburgh Steelers on Jan. 8, 2012.\n\n\r\n              The former Broncos QB and Jacksonville native served as the Broncos' alumni flag planter and was recognized as the Broncos’ “Legend of the Game” for their home opener.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Dobbins promised during camp that he'd stay healthy for 17 games this season, and he'll need a quick recovery to keep that pledge.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Jaguars: host the New England Patriots on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Broncos: host the Los Angeles Rams on Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0921/2701347f-22a6-44f2-b285-a973f49d25a0/2701347f-22a6-44f2-b285-a973f49d25a0.jpg"},"401872941":{"h":"Kirk Cousins connects with Cody White for 2 TDs and Raiders beat Chargers 26-14","d":"— Kirk Cousins used to watch Cody White attacking the football at Michigan State. The veteran quarterback figured the same thing would work for the Las Vegas Raiders.","b":"INGLEWOOD, Calif. -- — Kirk Cousins used to watch Cody White attacking the football at Michigan State. The veteran quarterback figured the same thing would work for the Las Vegas Raiders.\n\n\r\n              Cousins connected with White for a go-ahead, 13-yard touchdown pass in the third quarter and an 11-yard score during the fourth, leading the Raiders to a 26-14 win over the Los Angeles Chargers in the teams' AFC West opener on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              It was White's first career game with multiple TD receptions.\n\n\r\n              “Cody showed me a lot today,” Cousins said. “I guess those were my bank reps, just watching him play for Michigan State and then when he arrived try to translate that to here and now.”\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas (2-0) beat the Chargers (0-2) on the road for the first time since 2020, a game that was played without fans because of the COVID-19 pandemic. The Raiders, who maintain a strong Southern California fan base, had lost four straight to the Chargers by double digits.\n\n\r\n              With No. 1 draft pick Fernando Mendoza watching from the sideline, Cousins was 19 of 29 for 253 yards with three TDs and an interception. He got sacked twice.\n\n\r\n              Cousins joined Ken Stabler in 1976 as only the Raiders quarterbacks to throw three or more TD passes in each of their first two games.\n\n\r\n              “He’s been phenomenal,” said Raiders coach Klint Kubiak, who enjoyed a successful run with Cousins when they were both with the Minnesota Vikings. \n\n\r\n              The Chargers took their first lead, 14-10, on Justin Herbert’s 10-yard TD pass to Oronde Gadsden. Herbert fumbled and teammate Keaton Mitchell recovered at the Las Vegas 10-yard line. On the next play, Herbert spun out of a potential sack with Raiders star Maxx Crosby looming to connect with Gadsden.\n\n\r\n              The Raiders answered right back with the TD catch by White, who was elevated from the practice squad a day earlier. He also played in Las Vegas’ 27-13 win over Miami in Week 1 but did not have a catch.\n\n\r\n              “It just meant the world,” said White, whose family was on hand. “Just being able to go out there and make the plays when Kirk was able to give me some opportunities. It just felt amazing to go out there and get a win.”\n\n\r\n              Leading 17-14, the Raiders’ Jeremy Chinn recovered a fumble and returned it 43 yards with 3:52 remaining in the game. Omarion Hampton had a costly fumble for the second straight week and Chinn recovered, returning it to the Chargers 16-yard line.\n\n\r\n              Cousins then directed a three-play, 43-second drive that ended with White’s second TD catch and a 24-14 lead in front of a raucous, predominantly Raiders-supporting crowd at SoFi Stadium.\n\n\r\n              “It’s still really early, but certainly the goal is to build something that way outlasts me,” the 38-year-old Cousins said.\n\n\r\n              The Raiders led 7-0 on Tre Tucker’s 40-yard touchdown catch on the Chargers’ busted coverage in the second quarter. Rookie Genesis Smith, replacing injured Elijah Molden, lost Tucker, leaving him wide open.\n\n\r\n              Matt Gay’s career-best 59-yard field goal extended the lead to 10-7 just before halftime.\n\n\r\n              In his 100th NFL game, Jim Harbaugh fell to 0-2 for the first time in his college or pro coaching career, with the heavily favored Chargers also losing to Arizona 26-14 last week.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers were done in by six fumbles, among other miscues. \n\n\r\n              “The fight was there,” Harbaugh said. “We’re losing in the margins. That’s what we have to get fixed.”\n\n\r\n              A visibly frustrated Herbert was 15 of 26 for 192 yards with one touchdown and two interceptions. He took three sacks.\n\n\r\n              “That’s not good enough and can’t expect to win games like that when we’re playing like that,” he said. “If we’re not playing up to our standard, that’s on us to fix it.”\n\n\r\n              The Chargers' Joe Alt, one of the league's top tackles, had three false-start penalties and an offensive holding call that led to a Raiders safety in the closing minutes.\n\n\r\n              “Thought I saw what I saw and didn't,” Alt said. “Got to be better.”\n\n\r\n              The Chargers' opening drive of the game ended with Cameron Dicker's 58-yard field-goal attempt hitting the crossbar. He'd previously made 59 of 63 field goals at home.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Raiders: S Treydan Stukes was hurt in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              Chargers: S Derwin James Jr. (hand) got hurt late in the first quarter, but returned before halftime. ... TE David Njoku (knee) was taken off in a cart early in the second quarter and did not return. ... DL Dalvin Tomlinson was hurt in the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Raiders: Visit New Orleans next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              Chargers: Visit undefeated Buffalo next Sunday in their first road game of season.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0921/779c32ad-9029-4e29-b2ed-a8988a24797b/779c32ad-9029-4e29-b2ed-a8988a24797b.jpg"},"401872944":{"h":"Prescott throws 4 TD passes as Cowboys beat Commanders, who again lose Daniels to dislocated elbow","d":"— Dak Prescott celebrated breaking Tony Romo's career franchise record for touchdown passes in a victorious locker room for the Dallas Cowboys.","b":"ARLINGTON, Texas -- — Dak Prescott celebrated breaking Tony Romo's career franchise record for touchdown passes in a victorious locker room for the Dallas Cowboys.\n\n\r\n              Somber would be an understatement for the postgame mood around the other starting quarterback.\n\n\r\n              Prescott threw four touchdown passes to break Romo's record of 248, and the Cowboys beat Washington 37-20 on Sunday after the Commanders' Jayden Daniels went down with another gruesome-looking dislocated left elbow.\n\n\r\n              Prescott's 249th TD pass was his second TD of the game to CeeDee Lamb, who had 153 yards receiving. Tight end Jake Ferguson had the other two scoring catches.\n\n\r\n              Lamb, whose 37 touchdown catches from Prescott are more than any other player, gave his quarterback the record-breaking ball, and there was more celebrating with teammates behind the scenes soon after that.\n\n\r\n              “That made me emotional,” said Prescott, who said he was also emotional hours before the game just thinking about his late mother as he pondered the possibility of getting the record with a four-TD day. “There’s tons of guys that aren’t in that locker room that I think about when I break this record. To see my teammates act that way, to see guys that only have been together for five, six months, have the joy, have the cheers that they do, it was emotional.”\n\n\r\n              The Cowboys (1-1) led 17-10 with the Commanders on the Dallas 1-yard line with 3 seconds remaining in the first half. Left guard Chris Paul stepped on Daniels’ foot as he backed away from center, and Daniels’ non-throwing elbow appeared to bend the wrong way as he braced himself and threw a wild pass that was incomplete as time expired.\n\n\r\n              Daniels stayed down momentarily, then started to walk to the sideline before going to the turf again. Upon seeing a video replay of the injury, Fox NFL analyst Tom Brady uttered a profanity.\n\n\r\n              Daniels’ head was covered in a towel as he left the field on a cart several minutes after all the Dallas players and most of the Washington players had gone to their locker rooms.\n\n\r\n              After the game, Commanders coach Dan Quinn confirmed the dislocation but said imaging showed no fracture and that Daniels would fly home with the team.\n\n\r\n              Daniels, who was replaced by Marcus Mariota, missed all but one game in the second half of last season after dislocating his left elbow in a blowout loss to Seattle last November.\n\n\r\n              “I thought he was really playing his (rear end) off,” Quinn said. “And we will rally so hard around Marcus until Jayden’s back. And that’s what we talked about as a team.”\n\n\r\n              Mariota connected with Stefon Diggs for his second TD catch of the game to get Washington (0-2) within 30-20 with 9 minutes remaining.\n\n\r\n              Prescott led an 11-play drive the other way that ended with Lamb's 24-yard touchdown. The 33-year-old in his 11th season went 26 of 32 for 279 yards without an interception and extended his home winning streak against NFC East rivals to 20 games.\n\n\r\n              “You can’t talk about Dak, the player, without starting with the man,” coach Brian Schottenheimer said. The man is unique. That’s why I was emotional watching the guys celebrate him in the locker room. The way he plays this game, the way leads us is special.”\n\n\r\n              Before his injury, Daniels kept the Commanders close with his mobility. He had an 18-yard scramble on a drive that ended in a field goal and then a 20-yarder before his 4-yard touchdown pass to Diggs.\n\n\r\n              The Commanders were out of timeouts when they had a first down at the Dallas 1 trailing 17-10 with 12 seconds left in the first half. Daniels threw two incompletions, leaving 3 seconds for the play on which he was injured.\n\n\r\n              Daniels had 69 yards on seven carries while going 11 of 17 for 96 yards passing.\n\n\r\n              Dallas defense shows some life\r\n              A week a dud of a performance in the opener and the debut of 34-year-old defensive coordinator Christian Parker, the Dallas defense at least put up some resistance.\n\n\r\n              Mariota led the Commanders to a field goal to start the second half, but his second drive ended on the first play when Dallas rookie linebacker Jaishawn Barham forced a fumble by Dyami Brown on a screen pass. Dee Winters recovered inside the Washington 20, and Prescott's second TD pass to Ferguson for a 27-13 lead came two plays later.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Cowboys: S P.J. Locke was helped off the field after injuring a foot away from contact on Dallas' first defensive series and didn't return. ... CB Cobie Durant injured a hamstring in the first half and didn't return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Commanders: Defending Super Bowl champion Seattle visits next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Cowboys: Plays Baltimore in Rio de Janeiro next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0920/8a65621aa1a94ec1b41b209fa7cff6fe1183/8a65621aa1a94ec1b41b209fa7cff6fe1183.jpg"},"401872943":{"h":"Drew Lock connects for 3 TDs with Jaxon Smith-Njigba; Seahawks roll past Cardinals 31-7","d":"— Drew Lock admits he did a little more preparation this week before his first start in nearly two years, watching more film and studying the...","b":"GLENDALE, Ariz. -- — Drew Lock admits he did a little more preparation this week before his first start in nearly two years, watching more film and studying the playbook with a more critical eye.\n\n\r\n              By Sunday, the quarterback's strategy was clear: Get the ball to Jaxon Smith-Njigba.\n\n\r\n              Lock threw for 235 and three touchdowns — all to Smith-Njigba — in a stellar first start subbing for the injured Sam Darnold and the Seattle Seahawks continued their dominance over the Arizona Cardinals with an emphatic 31-7 victory.\n\n\r\n              “Drew is a special talent,” Smith-Njigba said. \"He sees the field very well, he can make any throw and I know he's looking for me. I just want to be there for him and we've had a successful connection for a minute now.\n\n\r\n              “I'm super happy the way he came in, carried himself and carried this team.”\n\n\r\n              The defending Super Bowl champions have now won 10 straight over their NFC West rival going back to 2022 and closed this one by scoring the final 24 points. Smith-Njigba finished with nine catches for 155 yards and the career-high three TDs in one of the best performances of his four-year career.\n\n\r\n              Arizona has lost eight straight games at home in a streak that's lasted 360 days. The Cardinals finished with just 151 total yards.\n\n\r\n              Lock got off to a good start, taking advantage of a busted coverage by the Cardinals' secondary and hitting a wide-open Smith-Njigba in stride for an 82-yard touchdown on the team's second offensive play of the game for a 7-0 lead. \n\n\r\n              Lock said Smith-Njigba altered his route in real time, making a quick double move that left Arizona's defense in the dust.\n\n\r\n              “That's the beauty of playing with him,” Lock said. “He's going to find ways to get open. It might not be the most conventional way, but that's what makes him a ballplayer. That's what makes him one of the best receivers and the offensive player of the year.”\n\n\r\n              Early in the third quarter, Lock found the reigning AP Offensive Player of the Year on a crossing route for a 7-yard score and a 14-7 advantage.\n\n\r\n              That Seattle touchdown was set up by some good fortune on the opening kickoff of the second half. Rashid Shaheed fumbled on the return, but teammate Chazz Surratt picked up the ball and ran 35 yards all the way to the Arizona 37.\n\n\r\n              Seattle safety Julian Love intercepted Jacoby Brissett on the ensuing drive, making a spectacular sliding catch as he got his hand under the ball just before it hit the ground. It gave the Seahawks (2-0) great field position again at the Arizona 44, which they parlayed into Jason Myers' 33-yard field goal and a 17-7 lead.\n\n\r\n              Smith-Njigba's third touchdown came early in the fourth quarter when Lock threw a perfectly placed ball for a 12-yard strike and a 24-7 lead.\n\n\r\n              “Loved how we closed the game out,” Seattle coach Mike Macdonald said. “Just really proud of the effort, all four quarters and how we played in the second half.”\n\n\r\n              The Cardinals (1-1) opened the season with an impressive 26-14 road win over the Chargers in first-year coach Mike LaFleur's debut, but couldn't recreate that success against the talented Seahawks defense. Brissett threw a 2-yard touchdown to All-Pro tight end Trey McBride to tie the game at 7-all on the first play of the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Seattle had a chance to retake the lead late in the second quarter, but couldn't ram the ball into the end zone from the 1-yard line on fourth down. The game stayed tied going into halftime, but it was all Seahawks after the break.\n\n\r\n              Brissett threw for 95 yards, one touchdown and one interception. Rookie Jeremiyah Love led the team with 29 yards rushing and McBride caught eight passes for 41 yards.\n\n\r\n              “We're not going to hit the panic button, but when we play the best defense in ball, you've got to do all the little things right,” LaFleur said. “We did not today.”\n\n\r\n              Lock made his first start since the final week of the 2024 season, taking over for Darnold, who suffered a glute injury in the opener against New England. The 29-year-old played most of that game and performed well, completing 16 of 22 passes for 187 yards and a touchdown.\n\n\r\n              Macdonald said that Darnold is progressing well and has at least a chance to start against the Washington Commanders next weekend. But if he's not ready to go, the Seahawks look as if they're in good hands with Lock.\n\n\r\n              “Really proud of Drew, really happy for him,” Macdonald said. “We're on record about how we feel about him and he backed that up today.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Seahawks: DL Brandon Pili was evaluated for a concussion. RB Jadarian Price left in the second half with a chest injury.\n\n\r\n              Cardinals: CB Max Melton left in the third quarter with a toe injury. CB Will Johnson left with a neck injury in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Seahawks: At Washington next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Cardinals: At San Francisco next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/f58f3860-a5af-4002-807f-3e293b28dc92/f58f3860-a5af-4002-807f-3e293b28dc92.jpg"},"401872942":{"h":"49ers score TDs on 1st 5 drives to beat the Dolphins 35-13","d":"— The San Francisco 49ers handled the return from Australia just as well as the trip there and have started the season with two convincing victories.","b":"SANTA CLARA, Calif. -- — The San Francisco 49ers handled the return from Australia just as well as the trip there and have started the season with two convincing victories.\n\n\r\n              Christian McCaffrey became the fourth-fastest player in the Super Bowl era with 100 career touchdowns, Brock Purdy played a near-perfect game and the 49ers overwhelmed the Miami Dolphins 35-13 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              The 49ers showed no signs of fatigue after beating the Los Angeles Rams 27-7 in the opener in Australia and began the season with back-to-back wins of at least 20 points for the first time in franchise history.\n\n\r\n              “That’s what I was real proud of the guys,” coach Kyle Shanahan said. “That was the biggest challenge of this, just the human nature of what all went into Week 1 and the travel and everything and coming back after five days off. ... But we got a really cool team in terms of how much guys care about football how into it they are.”\n\n\r\n              The 49ers scored touchdowns on their first five drives and dominated the Dolphins, who spent the week since losing the opener in Las Vegas by practicing in nearby Napa.\n\n\r\n              Avoiding the travel was little help to an overmatched Miami team that came into the season with a record of more than $180 million in dead money on the salary cap.\n\n\r\n              Purdy went 20 for 22 for 287 yards, two touchdown passes and one TD run, completing 18 straight passes at one point. He connected on a 12-yard touchdown pass to George Kittle and a 6-yarder to Kyle Juszczyk on the first two drives of the third quarter to break the game open.\n\n\r\n              “For me, every play, every drive mattered. It wasn’t like we went out there and just blew these guys out. It was a game. and ... That where my head space was at. How can I move the ball, take the right looks and keep us ahead of the chains. That’s what my mindset is at.”\n\n\r\n              McCaffrey scored on a pair of 1-yard runs to give him 100 touchdowns in 114 games, with only LaDainian Tomlinson (89 games), Emmitt Smith (93) and Shaun Alexander (96) getting their faster in the Super Bowl era.\n\n\r\n              McCaffrey, who has 50 touchdowns with both Carolina and San Francisco, joined Marshall Faulk and Randy Moss as the only players to score at least 50 TDs with two franchises.\n\n\r\n              “It’s kind of crazy to hear that,” McCaffrey said. “I watched all those guys growing up. Marshall Faulk was somebody that I’ve probably seen every single snap he’s played. I don’t take it for granted. Randy Moss I played with a ton of time with Madden growing up. Those are huge, huge honors.”\n\n\r\n              Malik Willis went 14 for 23 for 197 yards for Miami but took four sacks and was unable to lead a touchdown drive until his 77-yard pass to Ryan Miller with 2:30 remaining in the game.\n\n\r\n              “They’ve got great players who are experienced and have been running this system for a very long time,” said Miami coach Jeff Hafley, who was an assistant for the 49ers in Shanahan's first two seasons in 2017-18. “When I saw Kittle and Juice after the game, they were here when I was here in 2017. So great players, great play-caller, consistency and continuity in one scheme over time usually equals success. That’s a really good team.”\n\n\r\n              Both defenses struggled to get off the field in the first half with the main difference being the Niners finished off their drives with touchdowns. Riley Patterson missed a 51-yard field goal on Miami's opening drive and the Dolphins settled for two field goals in the red zone on their other two possessions in the first half.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco got in the end zone with McCaffrey scoring on a 1-yard run and Purdy on a 2-yard run on the two drives for the 49ers, who led 14-6 at the half. \n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Dolphins: RB Jaylen Wright (stinger) left in the first half. ... WR Caleb Douglas (ankle) left in the second half.\n\n\r\n              49ers: WR Demarcus Robinson and DT James Thompson Jr. left with what Shanahan believes were high ankle injuries and could miss some time. ... WR Mike Evans left in the second half with a hip injury but likely could have returned if the game had been closer.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Dolphins: Host Kansas City on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              49ers: Host Arizona on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0920/badc336c-dc8f-401e-bdba-d9a6c9cfb4a4/badc336c-dc8f-401e-bdba-d9a6c9cfb4a4.jpg"},"401872945":{"h":"Mahomes and Walker shine, Butker hits winning 40-yarder as Chiefs beat Colts 33-30 in OT","d":"— The Kansas City Chiefs thought they had beaten the Indianapolis Colts on a bizarre fumble in overtime Sunday night.","b":"KANSAS CITY, Mo. -- — The Kansas City Chiefs thought they had beaten the Indianapolis Colts on a bizarre fumble in overtime Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              The Colts ended up with a chance to win instead, yet chose to play for a tie.\n\n\r\n              And once again, Patrick Mahomes and the rest of his crew made an opposing team pay for giving them one last chance.\n\n\r\n              After the Chiefs and Colts traded field goals in the extra session, Mahomes followed a couple of strong runs by Kenneth Walker III by finding his running back for a long completion, setting up Harrison Butker's 40-yard field goal as time expired and lifting the Chiefs to a 33-30 victory — the second straight year they've beaten Indianapolis on a field goal in overtime.\n\n\r\n              “I mean, I think guys were resilient, and we just strained to finish,” Mahomes said. “It wasn’t always pretty. There were times we didn’t execute at a high level. But guys continued to fight and continued to push and we found a way at the end.”\n\n\r\n              Mahomes finished with 382 yards passing and three touchdowns, Walker rumbled for 117 yards, and tight end Travis Kelce looked like a younger version of himself, catching nine balls for 101 yards and a score as the Chiefs improved to 2-0 on the season.\n\n\r\n              It took a little longer than they first thought.\n\n\r\n              Butker had kicked a 31-yard field goal in overtime to give them the lead, and the Colts (0-2) responded on their possession by having Jones throw deep to Laquon Treadwell, who had beaten L'Jarius Snead. Treadwell appeared to be bobbling the ball as he hit the ground, got up after he had completed the catch, had the ball popped loose and watched as the Chiefs pounced on it.\n\n\r\n              The play was ruled a fumble on the field, but the officials reviewed it and determined Treadwell was down by contact. That gave the Colts a chance to score a go-ahead touchdown, but three plays left them facing fourth-and-8 and a decision.\n\n\r\n              “Had a play called. Had to love the look to run it there,” Colts coach Shane Steichen said. “We took the delay and kicked the field goal.”\n\n\r\n              Turned out to be the wrong decision.\n\n\r\n              The Chiefs still had 3:11 to play in the extra session when they got the ball back, and they handed off to Walker on three straight plays to reach midfield. As the Colts began to stack the line of scrimmage to stop him, Mahomes threw a nifty pass to his running back, and he turned it into a 22-yard gain that set up Butker's winning kick.\n\n\r\n              “We had worked throughout the week dropping it over the top,” Mahomes said. “They were in a pressure look. We were able to change the play. You get the ball in his hands, Rashee (Rice) did a great job of blocking on the perimeter. That got us in field goal range.”\n\n\r\n              Got them the win, too.\n\n\r\n              “Overall we fought like crazy,” Steichen said, “but it wasn’t good enough. And then they find a way to make a play at the end.”\n\n\r\n              The Colts didn't look a whole lot better on defense after a disastrous Week 1 performance against Baltimore that they had chalked up to communication problems. They tried to solve the issue by having linebacker Akeem Davis-Gaither relay the plays rather than safety Cam Bynum, but the tweak didn't seem to make a whole lot of difference as Kansas City marched up and down the field.\n\n\r\n              Each of the Chiefs’ first four drives would have produced points had Butker not missed a 51-yard field goal try.\n\n\r\n              Yet the Colts were able to match Mahomes and Co. blow-for-blow over the first 30 minutes. Tyler Warren had three catches and a touchdown, Josh Downs picked apart a secondary missing safety Chamarri Conner for a second straight game, and Taylor had 75 of his rushing yards — the highlight a 24-yard touchdown scamper that gave Indianapolis a 20-17 lead at the break.\n\n\r\n              The Chiefs allowed Denver to run for just 61 yards total in their 31-10 win last week.\n\n\r\n              “We did some good things. Thought the run game was good. Moved the ball. I thought we were pushing,” Jones said.\n\n\r\n              Kansas City finally slowed the Colts’ offense by refusing to give them the ball. The Chiefs held it for all but 2:22 of the third quarter, getting a go-ahead TD pass to Jalen Royals and setting up Butker’s field goal to start the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Indianapolis answered with an 8 1/2-minute drive that Taylor finished with his second TD run to tie it at 27l with 5:37 to go.\n\n\r\n              Neither offense could produce points down the stretch, though, leaving the game to be decided in overtime.\n\n\r\n              “It was great that the guys competed for four quarters,” Reid said. “You're going to have this. The parity in this league is crazy. You're going to have some once-score games. You have to bear down and and our guys did that.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Colts: WR Alex Pierce was carted to the locker room with a left heel injury early in the second quarter. ... DE Michael Clemons left late in the first half with a toe injury. ... CB Charvarius Ward hurt his groin early in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Chiefs: RT Jaylon Moore left briefly in the third quarter but was able to finish the game.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Colts: Host the Texans next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Chiefs: Visit the Dolphins next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0921/bba56cdbbfcc451599b7046dd298af5f5/bba56cdbbfcc451599b7046dd298af5f5.jpg"},"401872947":{"h":"Davante Adams, Matthew Stafford lead Rams past Giants 28-6 after Jaxson Dart's injury","d":"— The Los Angeles Rams' dismal season opener in Australia and their proliferating injury woes were nothing Davante Adams and Matthew Stafford couldn't overcome back home with a vintage performance against the Giants.","b":"INGLEWOOD, Calif. -- — The Los Angeles Rams' dismal season opener in Australia and their proliferating injury woes were nothing Davante Adams and Matthew Stafford couldn't overcome back home with a vintage performance against the Giants.\n\n\r\n              And after New York lost Jaxson Dart on the opening drive, it didn't stand a chance.\n\n\r\n              Adams made eight catches for 195 yards and hauled in two of Stafford’s four touchdown passes, and the Rams welcomed Aaron Donald back to the NFL with a 28-6 victory Monday night.\n\n\r\n              Stafford passed for 327 yards for the Rams (1-1), who capitalized after the Giants (1-1) lost Dart to a left knee injury on their seventh snap. Los Angeles outgained New York with 481 yards to 182, even overcoming two turnovers in a swagger-restoring blowout.\n\n\r\n              “This team, we got to the confidence level that we're at because of the work we put in, and we're not going to put any governors on what we can do here,” said Adams, who had the fourth-highest yardage total of his 13-year career. “If you're trying to be great, be a Super Bowl-caliber team, you've got to hold yourself to a high standard. We can do even a little bit better than what we did, but this is on track with what we're capable of.”\n\n\r\n              The Rams played without injured All-Pros Puka Nacua and Myles Garrett, but Stafford and Adams were in top form while the preseason Super Bowl favorites rebounded competently from last week’s 27-7 debacle of a Week 1 loss to the 49ers.\n\n\r\n              Adams’ catches and yards receiving were both his best performances in a Rams uniform, while league MVP Stafford moved into sixth in NFL history with his 426th career TD pass, topping Philip Rivers when Adams adroitly tracked down an 11-yard fade in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              Adams was extraordinary in the second game of his 13th NFL season. He made a two-handed grab high above his head before taking it for a 31-yard touchdown on the Rams’ second possession, and he made a 64-yard catch on their third drive to set up Kyren Williams’ 10-yard TD catch.\n\n\r\n              “(Adams) was special, and it showed all night,” coach Sean McVay said. “I thought he made some great plays, but you can see there's a lot of trust he and Matthew have. It's only going to continue to build, and I was really happy for him.”\n\n\r\n              Jameis Winston went 11 of 27 for 111 yards with an interception in place of Dart, who left the field shakily after taking a huge hit from Byron Young and Josaiah Stewart. The second-year quarterback, who looked great in last week's win over Dallas, watched the second half in a track suit.\n\n\r\n              After his first loss with the Giants, coach John Harbaugh wouldn't confirm Dart's injury is a sprained knee ligament, but said it's “in the neighborhood of it.”\n\n\r\n              “It's tough to come in, (but Winston) executed and he operated well,” Harbaugh said. “But as a group, as a team, we just didn't do what we needed to do to even have a chance to win the game. We didn't play up to that level across the board.”\n\n\r\n              Winston and the Giants’ offense couldn’t get much going after Dart’s exit. Winston threw an end-zone interception to Trent McDuffie in the second quarter, and New York managed only a field goal after Jevon Holland forced and recovered a fumble at the Los Angeles 11 right before halftime, with Donald stuffing Najee Harris on a classic third-down tackle.\n\n\r\n              “No excuses,” said running back Cam Skattebo, who managed only 36 yards on 12 carries. “They outperformed us, they out-executed us, and we just need to go back to the film room and fix some things.”\n\n\r\n              Stafford put it away on a scoring pass to Terrance Ferguson with 8:57 to play.\n\n\r\n              AD's return\r\n              The night was also a milestone for Donald, who had three tackles for the Rams while completing his comeback with his first NFL game action in 32 months. The three-time AP NFL Defensive Player of the Year, who skipped the Week 1 trip to Melbourne, got wild cheers from Rams fans at every opportunity at SoFi Stadium.\n\n\r\n              “It felt really good to be back, coming out, being embraced how I was,” Donald said. “I think the defense played great. Felt like we played how we're supposed to play.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Giants pass rusher Brian Burns went down with what looked like a serious left ankle injury in the fourth quarter, but he turned down a cart ride postgame, saying he felt surprisingly good. ... WR Malik Nabers missed time in the first half while popping his dislocated shoulder back into place, but returned to play. He'll have an X-ray Tuesday, as will Dart. ... Rams RB Ronnie Rivers injured his calf.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Giants: Host Tennessee on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Rams: At Denver on Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0921/114fcea7a78248b492eda2229525d4561357/114fcea7a78248b492eda2229525d4561357.jpg"},"401872953":{"h":"Allen, Bills outlast Chargers 24-16 despite committing 5 turnovers","d":"— Buffalo Bills safety C.J. Gardner-Johnson didn’t miss a beat when asked if he’d ever been part of a win when his team commits five turnovers.","b":"ORCHARD PARK, N.Y. -- — Buffalo Bills safety C.J. Gardner-Johnson didn’t miss a beat when asked if he’d ever been part of a win when his team commits five turnovers.\n\n\r\n              “Yeah,” the ninth-year player said, his eyes widening while breaking into a big laugh. “Today.”\n\n\r\n              Gardner-Johnson’s interception of Justin Herbert’s pass intended for Marquez Valdes-Scantling at the goal line punctuated a defensive-driven win by bailing out Josh Allen’s sloppiness in a 24-16 victory over the winless Los Angeles Chargers on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              The interception came three plays after Allen fumbled at his own 28. And it set up the quarterback capping the ensuing drive with a 1-yard touchdown to give the Bills their first lead with 7:35 left.\n\n\r\n              About four minutes later, and after the defense stopped Herbert on fourth down, James Cook scored on a 3-yard run to secure the win.\n\n\r\n              “I’m used to this. I’ve been in two Super Bowl bowls, conference championships. It ain’t nothing new,” Gardner-Johnson said.\n\n\r\n              “Teams like that, that ain’t really been to the top, they don’t know how to finish,” he said, referring to LA. “And we got the playmakers over here that know how to finish. So, no disrespect. Just that’s what it is.”\n\n\r\n              Allen scored two touchdowns rushing and overcame an outing in which he also threw two interceptions. And Buffalo is 3-0 for the third straight year and fifth time in nine seasons under Allen.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers, meantime, opened with three losses for the first time since 2017, and feature a listless, sputtering offense that lacks finish under first-year offensive coordinator Mike McDaniel.\n\n\r\n              Following up 26-14 losses to Arizona and Las Vegas, the Chargers managed a touchdown and three field goals despite having six drives that ended inside the Buffalo 30.\n\n\r\n              “We did not win the game. That’s painful. That hurts,” coach Jim Harbaugh said. “There was a lot of opportunity. And we’ve got to capitalize on that. We have to be better.”\n\n\r\n              Harbaugh then pushed back on the suggestion of changes being required on offense.\n\n\r\n              “I’m not singling out anybody. We win these games as a team, we lose as a team,” he said. “Not where we want to be. Only one way to correct it: Just keep chipping away.”\n\n\r\n              The Chargers have plenty to chip away at on an offense that through three games has combined for more punts (10) and committed more turnovers (six) than scored touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers built a 10-0 lead off Buffalo’s first two turnovers in the first quarter, before being limited to two Cameron Dicker field goals. The outcome was decided after Dicker hit a 33-yarder with 1:56 left, with Buffalo recovering the ensuing onside kick.\n\n\r\n              The latest offensive dud spoiled a defensive effort in which the Chargers limited Allen to 204 yards passing and being sacked four times.\n\n\r\n              Herbert finished 20 of 34 for 226 yards with a touchdown — a 1-yard shovel pass to Keaton Mitchell — and interception, and rookie safety Genesis Smith had both interceptions.\n\n\r\n              Allen’s two touchdown runs upped his career total from scrimmage to 87 (85 rushing and two receiving) — tying Hall of Famers Thurman Thomas and Andre Reed for the team record. With six rushing TDs already, Allen joined running backs Hall of Famers Jim Brown, Marshall Faulk and LaDainian Tomlinson as the only players to score six or more in each of their first nine seasons.\n\n\r\n              Cook led Buffalo with 154 yards rushing, while losing a fumble on the opening snap. Tight end Dalton Kincaid also lost a fumble while battling for extra yards in the third quarter at the Chargers 17.\n\n\r\n              Allen acknowledged the Bills may have stolen a win, while paying full credit to Buffalo’s defense.\n\n\r\n              “Sitting there to be tied at halftime felt like a dream, to be honest,” Allen said referring to the score tied at 10. “Our defense played fantastic. It’s not a game that you expect to win, but we’ll take them any way we can get them.”\n\n\r\n              The previous time Buffalo won when committing five or more turnovers in a game came in a 37-35 victory against Indianapolis on Sept. 21, 1997.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Chargers: CB Donte Jackson was ruled out after sustaining a concussion in the first half.\n\n\r\n              Bills: CB Christian Benford went down with a toe injury late in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Chargers: at the Seattle Seahawks on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bills: Close three-game homestand by hosting the New England Patriots on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/811e5e532ec4440faea5c045e2121e391124/811e5e532ec4440faea5c045e2121e391124.jpg"},"401872949":{"h":"Deshaun Watson sparks a late Browns rally, sealing a 21-18 win over the Panthers","d":"— It came five weeks later than he thought, but Deshaun Watson earned cheers from Cleveland Browns fans.","b":"CLEVELAND -- — It came five weeks later than he thought, but Deshaun Watson earned cheers from Cleveland Browns fans.\n\n\r\n              After struggling for most of the game, Watson came through when it counted on Sunday. He led a late fourth-quarter drive and threw a 14-yard touchdown pass to Harold Fannin Jr. with 1:48 remaining as the Browns rallied for a 21-18 victory over the Carolina Panthers.\n\n\r\n              It was Watson’s 10th fourth-quarter comeback and game-winning drive, but possibly the sweetest of his 10-year career after working 22 months to come back from tearing his right Achilles tendon twice.\n\n\r\n              “It was a warm moment. I got a little teary-eyed feeling that energy because I put so much work to get back here, to be in this moment, to show that I can finish these games and get W’s for the city and this organization,” said Watson, who completed 16 of 30 passes for 144 yards and two TDs — both to Fannin — along with 45 rushing yards.\n\n\r\n              Browns coach Todd Monken — Watson’s biggest supporter — was happy to see his quarterback come up big in the late moments.\n\n\r\n              “He probably didn’t play as well as he wanted. But to get knocked down, come back and make a play, then a 2-point conversion, this is about him. I’m really happy for him,” Monken said.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers took an 18-13 lead with 4:25 remaining when John Metchie III hauled in an 8-yard touchdown pass from Bryce Young. But the Browns (2-1) went 65 yards in eight plays for their second straight comeback win.\n\n\r\n              Watson was 3 of 6 for 44 yards on the decisive drive. He also had a 21-yard scramble to the Panthers 21 on third-and-3 to move the sticks.\n\n\r\n              It appeared the Browns’ chances for a comeback were dashed when Akayleb Evans picked off Watson’s pass intended for KC Concepcion in the end zone with 2:29 remaining. But Carolina linebacker Bobby Okereke was called for roughing the passer.\n\n\r\n              “We extended the drive for them, and if you give a guy like Deshaun Watson a little bit of extra time, and a few more opportunities, he’s going to get creative and find something,” Carolina coach Dave Canales said.\n\n\r\n              Watson came out for one play after the hit knocked the wind out of him but returned to cheers. The 10-year veteran QB and his teammates feared he would be showered with boos in the home opener after a negative reception during a preseason game against Buffalo on Aug. 22.\n\n\r\n              There were some boos during the third quarter when the Browns went three-and-out on three straight possessions, but everyone knew it was because of the team’s performance.\n\n\r\n              After overthrowing Denzel Boston in the end zone on second-and-10 at the 14, Watson stepped up in the pocket and rolled left before finding Fannin open in the end zone.\n\n\r\n              “During that whole drive, I told the offensive line that if something happens, give me a lane and I’m going to make something happen with my feet,” Watson said. “Harold, I just put the ball in the air and gave him a chance.”\n\n\r\n              Watson then connected with Boston for a 2-point conversion to push the margin to a field goal.\n\n\r\n              Carolina (1-2) made a late drive, but Ronnie Hickman picked off Young’s pass at the Browns 46 with 9 seconds remaining. It was the Panthers' second turnover.\n\n\r\n              Watson took one snap and knelt to run out the clock. He kept that ball and also got a game ball from Monken.\n\n\r\n              “To finish a game like this, regardless of the energy outside, shows the character and person I am, and how strong mentally I am. Regardless of what’s going on, I fight like hell to accomplish what I want, no matter the chaos outside, good or bad. It was a great win, definitely one of the top ones,” Watson said.\n\n\r\n              Tough day for Young\r\n              Young, who came into the game tied for the NFL lead in passing touchdowns and second in passing yards, completed 26 of 48 passes for 291 yards with a touchdown and an interception.\n\n\r\n              Young appeared to tweak his ankle after Mason Graham sacked him in the second quarter and briefly entered the medical tent during the third.\n\n\r\n              Canales said that Young is working through a lower-body injury and would be evaluated.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers got four field goals from Ryan Fitzgerald before they found the end zone.\n\n\r\n              Browns take advantage of Panthers’ fourth-down choices\r\n              Cleveland capitalized on a couple of bad fourth-down decisions by Canales.\n\n\r\n              The Browns opened the scoring with the first of Andre Szmyt’s two field goals. Cleveland got the ball on the Panthers 47 after Graham sacked Young.\n\n\r\n              The Panthers went for it again on their next series, this time on fourth-and-short from their own 40, but Young was short on a throw intended for tight end Tommy Tremble. The Browns found the end zone seven plays later when Watson found Fannin on a crossing route in the right back corner of the end zone on fourth-and-goal from the 2 to extend the lead to 10-0.\n\n\r\n              Fannin led Cleveland in receiving with seven catches for 51 yards.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Panthers: CB Mike Jackson suffered a groin injury in the first quarter and did not return. WR Jalen Coker (quad) came out in the third quarter and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Browns: WR Tylan Wallace sustained a knee injury in the fourth quarter and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Panthers: Host Detroit next Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              Browns: Host Pittsburgh on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0927/c4f3c2a4-8d05-4bff-81de-2a61b7bd45b3/c4f3c2a4-8d05-4bff-81de-2a61b7bd45b3.jpg"},"401872954":{"h":"Jahmyr Gibbs' 3rd touchdown with 2:25 left lifts Lions to 31-24 win over Jets","d":"— Jahmyr Gibbs took a toss sweep to the right, reversed field and raced to the left side of the end zone for a 17-yard touchdown.","b":"DETROIT -- — Jahmyr Gibbs took a toss sweep to the right, reversed field and raced to the left side of the end zone for a 17-yard touchdown.\n\n\r\n              He was just getting warmed up.\n\n\r\n              Gibbs ran for another touchdown in the second half and had a go-ahead 11-yard reception with 2:25 left for his third score of the game and the Detroit Lions held on to beat the New York Jets 31-24 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “Gibbs hit another gear once we got him going in the second half,” coach Dan Campbell said.\n\n\r\n              The Lions (2-1) leaned on their dynamic running back a lot — on the ground and through the air — and he delivered another fantastic performance.\n\n\r\n              Gibbs said he was inspired by Pro Football Hall of Famer Barry Sanders being in the house.\n\n\r\n              “Anytime he’s here, I’ve got to step up my game,” he said.\n\n\r\n              Gibbs had 20 carries for 99 yards with two touchdown runs in the second half, along with seven receptions for 65 yards and score. The fourth-year pro is the first player in NFL history with at least 300 yards, four rushing touchdowns, 150 yards receiving and two touchdown receptions through three games.\n\n\r\n              “He’s so explosive and nifty with the ball in his hands,” Jared Goff said.\n\n\r\n              The Jets (1-2) overcame a 14-point deficit in the fourth quarter with Geno Smith's 4-yard touchdown pass to Jeremy Ruckert, a 23-yard TD throw to Garrett Wilson and a 2-point conversion pass to Sterling Shepard to make it 24-all.\n\n\r\n              New York's defense — led by head coach Aaron Glenn, Detroit's former defensive coordinator — then had two blown coverages as Goff and Gibbs marched the Lions down the field to quickly regain the lead. \n\n\r\n              With a chance to tie or perhaps go for the win, the Jets were on the Detroit 28 when Smith lost a fumble after blitzing safety Chuck Clark sacked him to seal it.\n\n\r\n              New York came up short again, one week after losing a 10-point lead in the fourth quarter against Green Bay and losing 20-17 in overtime.\n\n\r\n              “We’re fighting to close that gap and we will,” Glenn said. \"We’ll be on the other side of these games. I truly believe that”\n\n\r\n              Smith finished 31 of 37 for 321 yards with three touchdowns and no interceptions. Wilson had 10 catches for 107 yards and a touchdown, but was on the sideline on the final offensive play after getting banged up and evaluated for a head injury.\n\n\r\n              “We have a lot of real good players,” Smith said. “We’ve just got to finish games.”\n\n\r\n              Goff was 25 of 32 for 269 yards with two touchdowns and no turnovers.\n\n\r\n              The Lions got off to a slow start against Glenn, who was Dan Campbell's defensive coordinator from 2021 through 2024, before closing strong to score 31 points for the third straight week.\n\n\r\n              Detroit put the ball in Gibbs' hands to set up Jake Bates' 30-yard field goal early in the second quarter. \n\n\r\n              New York's offense suddenly started moving the ball on the ensuing possession as Smith completed passes on four straight snaps for 72 yards. Rookie Kenyon Sadiq fully extended on a 24-yard touchdown catch in tight coverage put the Jets up 7-3. Sadiq had seven receptions for 105 yards and the score, a boost for a short-handed receiving corps.\n\n\r\n              Detroit converted a fourth-and-2 from the New York 9 with a pass to Isaac TeSlaa to set up Goff's 4-yard toss to Amon-Ra St. Brown's back shoulder for a 10-7 lead late in the first half.\n\n\r\n              It extended Goff's streak with a touchdown pass to 26 games and was St. Brown's fifth catch for a score in 10 quarters this season.\n\n\r\n              On Gibbs' first of three touchdowns, Goff ran interference on the path to the end zone.\n\n\r\n              “He wouldn’t have gotten in if I didn't block for him,” the quarterback joked.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Jets: RB Breece Hall (thigh) and LG Dylan Parham (knee) were injured during the game. WR Adonai Mitchell (finger) was inactive while TE Mason Taylor (thumb), S Minkah Fitzpatrick (groin) and LB Kiko Mauigoa (quadriceps) were previously ruled out.\n\n\r\n              Lions: RT Blake Miller and LG Christian Mahogany played after missing last week’s game with injuries.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Jets: At Chicago next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Lions: At Carolina next Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              —\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/dm_260927_Jets_lose_to_Lions_IA/dm_260927_Jets_lose_to_Lions_IA.jpg"},"401872951":{"h":"Colts end 9-game skid with 19-17 victory over Texans as Shrader kicks winner with 16 seconds left","d":"— The Indianapolis Colts kept talking about turning the page on their season-ending seven-game losing streak that cost them a playoff spot last year.","b":"INDIANAPOLIS -- — The Indianapolis Colts kept talking about turning the page on their season-ending seven-game losing streak that cost them a playoff spot last year. \n\n\r\n              Two more losses to start the season only exacerbated the pain.\n\n\r\n              On Sunday, they finally put an end to the league's longest active losing streak — and perhaps much of the chatter. \n\n\r\n              Spencer Shrader fought through a groin injury to make four field goals, including a 43-yarder with 16 seconds to go, and the Colts' beleaguered defense held up long enough to finally lose out a 19-17 victory over the Houston Texans — their first win since last November and their first win on American soil since last October.\n\n\r\n              “It's awesome, obviously, to get that victory,” coach Shane Steichen said. “To get that win against a division opponent is huge for our football team and now we've got to go to London and try to get that done over there, too.”\n\n\r\n              For the Colts (1-2), the celebration inside the locker room was a welcome respite from the loud critics outside of it.\n\n\r\n              No, Indy didn't win many style points.\n\n\r\n              Daniel Jones was 24 of 36 for 235 yards and one touchdown, but also fumbled three times and lost two. The second set up Houston's late go-ahead touchdown. \n\n\r\n              Jonathan Taylor had 23 carries for only 68 yards. Keenan Allen hauled in six passes for 63 yards and his first TD with the Colts and Josh Downs caught five passes for 77 yards. The defense also gave up a touchdown with 1:48 to play, once again putting the Colts hopes in peril.\n\n\r\n              This time, though, Jones made the most of his last chance. He hooked up with Laquon Treadwell for 20 yards. Taylor added a 5-yard run and Allen's 5-yard catch on third down came up 1 yard short of a first down, but it was enough to set up Shrader for the 53-yarder.\n\n\r\n              “I feel like today was one of my best days with how I was striking the ball,” said Shrader, who injured his groin after the opening kickoff. “So I kind of wonder whether it was one of those things where you know sometimes if you slow down a little bit, you actually end up getting a better result because you're smoother? I felt like might have happened today.\"\n\n\r\n              Houston's five-game winning streak in the series ended when C.J. Stroud's desperation heave sailed out of bounds short and wide of the end zone as time expired. The Texans are 0-3 for the straight year as the offense sputters.\n\n\r\n              C.J. Stroud was 16 of 27 for 167 yards with one TD. The Texans rushed 21 times for 70 yards and had only 223 total yards against a team that allowed more than 500 yards in each of its first two games.\n\n\r\n              “These are the types of games we're supposed to win,” Stroud said. “We fought, but, at the end of the day, there's a lot of things that need to be fixed.”\n\n\r\n              It looked as though Indy's struggles would continue when Jones lost the football and Tommy Togiai recovered at the Texans 46-yard line with 4:40 left in the game. Seven plays later, Woody Marks scored on 1-yard run and the extra point gave Houston a 17-16 lead with 1:48 to go.\n\n\r\n              Instead, the Colts rallied to end their skid.\n\n\r\n              “Happy,” Steichen said, describing the locker room. “Very happy.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Texans: LB Azeez Al-Shaair left in the first half with a groin injury, returned at the start of the second half, left again in the third quarter after re-injuring his groin and returned again late in the game. DE Mario Edwards and WR Xavier Hutchinson both left with injuries late in the fourth quarter. Edwards went to the locker room with a left leg injury. Coach DeMeco Ryans had no postgame updates. \n\n\r\n              Colts: DE Laiatu Latu left early with a chest injury. He returned in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Moving up\r\n              Allen's first catch of the day was not only his first score with the Colts, it also sent him past former Texans star and Hall of Famer Andre Johnson, for 12th on the NFL's career list. He now has 1,068 receptions — trailing Colts receivers coach Reggie Wayne by two for No. 11.\n\n\r\n              Allen also had his 71st career TD reception, moving him into a tie for 50th all-time with Hall of Famer Bob Hayes and Wesley Walker.\n\n\r\n              Instructional lesson\r\n              Referee Ronald Torbert gave the officiating crew a vulgar instruction while his microphone remained on after announcing the fumble on the kickoff had been reversed.\n\n\r\n              “You guys better get your (expletive) together,” Torbert blurted out, drawing laughter inside the stadium.\n\n\r\n              The reversal gave Houston the ball, instead of setting up Indy at the Texans 28, and Stroud led Houston to its first TD, a 1-yard pass from Stroud to Foster Moreau to make it 10-7 late in the third quarter..\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Texans: Return home to face rival Dallas.\n\n\r\n              Colts: Travel to London next Sunday to face Washington.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0927/6d642b41-81e6-41eb-ad43-b31d69c68480/6d642b41-81e6-41eb-ad43-b31d69c68480.jpg"},"401872952":{"h":"Kenneth Walker III has 2 TDs as Chiefs beat Dolphins 24-10 after De'Von Achane exits with injury","d":"— Patrick Mahomes arrived in South Florida this weekend and noticed a fun coincidence. He was back in the same hotel room he stayed in when he won his first Super Bowl title.","b":"MIAMI GARDENS, Fla. -- — Patrick Mahomes arrived in South Florida this weekend and noticed a fun coincidence. He was back in the same hotel room he stayed in when he won his first Super Bowl title.\n\n\r\n              “You feel that on the field and seeing the stadium where you won your first Super Bowl,” Mahomes said. “It was cool to be back here.”\n\n\r\n              The Chiefs didn't win any trophies on Sunday, but they got to 3-0. And that was good enough.\n\n\r\n              Kenneth Walker III had a touchdown catch and rushed for a score, and Mahomes threw for 246 yards and two TDs to help the Chiefs beat the Miami Dolphins 24-10.\n\n\r\n              “Obviously we didn’t play to our standard that we would hold for ourselves,” Mahomes said. “But I thought we found a way to come together at the end of the game.”\n\n\r\n              The Chiefs entered averaging more than 450 yards and 32 points but struggled to pull away from the winless Dolphins, who were missing arguably their best player for much of the game after Pro Bowler De'Von Achane left with a knee injury on the opening drive.\n\n\r\n              Dolphins coach Jeff Hafley said the team was still gathering information on the extent of Achane's injury, but added it did not “sound optimistic.”\n\n\r\n              Mahomes completed 20 of 24 passes with an interception. He threw a 5-yard touchdown pass to Walker, and his 11-yard TD pass to Travis Kelce put the game out of reach.\n\n\r\n              Mahomes found Kelce in the end zone with 2:55 left to put the Chiefs up by two scores after Kansas City stopped the Dolphins on fourth down at Miami's 48. The Chiefs intercepted Malik Willis on the ensuing drive, and Mahomes moved Kansas City into scoring position before Harrison Butker missed a 50-yard field-goal attempt.\n\n\r\n              Rashee Rice led the Chiefs in receiving with seven catches for 88 yards, including receptions of 15 and 34 yards on Kansas City's last scoring drive.\n\n\r\n              “We knew it’d be four quarters of football, and that’s what it took,” coach Andy Reid said. “We’ve got plenty of things to work on, but it’s great to be 3-0. There were some real good things we could take out of it, and there’s some things we can definitely work on going forward.”\n\n\r\n              Kelce opened the game with a 48-yard catch-and-run but didn't have another catch — or target — until the fourth-quarter score. Walker was held under 100 yards rushing for the first time this season with 18 carries for 70 yards. \n\n\r\n              “We're not going to be mad about winning,” Mahomes said, adding the team's youth played a factor in some of Sunday's lulls. “We won the football game. You think about last season — we were losing some of these games.”\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins (0-3) pulled within 14-10 on a nine-play scoring drive in the third quarter highlighted by tight end Greg Dulcich's 42-yard catch and capped by Riley Patterson's 37-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              Willis was 20 of 36 for 210 yards with the interception and a lost fumble. He also rushed for 56 yards.\n\n\r\n              Ollie Gordon had 17 carries for 41 yards and a 3-yard TD. The Dolphins turned to the third-stringer after Achane suffered the knee injury on an 11-yard run on Miami's first drive. Backup Jaylen Wright (stinger) was inactive.\n\n\r\n              Kansas City scored touchdowns on its first two drives, and it looked like Miami's defense was in for another long afternoon, one week after allowing San Francisco to score touchdowns on its first five possessions.\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins' young defense settled in, though. Miami forced a turnover on downs on Kansas City's third drive, and rookie linebacker Jacob Rodriguez came up with a diving interception of Mahomes before halftime. \n\n\r\n              “It's never as good as it was, never as bad as it was until you watch the tape, but I'm pleased with that,” Rodriguez said. “I'm pleased with the intensity. I'm pleased with the physicality of this defense and of this team. And I believe in everybody. ... Now, we have to get better at the football part of it.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Dolphins: Gordon left late in the third quarter because of cramping but returned.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Chiefs: At Las Vegas next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Dolphins: At Minnesota next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/dm_260927_Chiefs_beat_Dolphins_IA/dm_260927_Chiefs_beat_Dolphins_IA.jpg"},"401872956":{"h":"Giants hold on to beat the Titans 12-7 in their first game without quarterback Jaxson Dart","d":"— Playing their first game without quarterback Jaxson Dart, the New York Giants got just enough from replacement Jameis Winston on offense and another big play from safety Jevon Holland...","b":"EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. -- — Playing their first game without quarterback Jaxson Dart, the New York Giants got just enough from replacement Jameis Winston on offense and another big play from safety Jevon Holland to win an ugly football game in the middle of a nor'easter.\n\n\r\n              Winston completed 12 of 18 passes for 117 yards, Holland intercepted Cam Ward with 17 seconds left and the Giants held on to beat the Tennessee Titans 12-7 on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              Coach John Harbaugh thought Winston did what was required and necessary in rainy, windy conditions. \n\n\r\n              “Today is the only day that we have, and today it was enough,” Winston said. “I’ve had games where I’ve thrown for 500 yards, and the game didn’t turn out the way I wanted it to be. Any time, any day, I would rather win.”\n\n\r\n              In the aftermath of Dart having surgery on his left knee that will sideline him for at least the remainder of the regular season, the Giants (2-1) lost their most important defensive player when Brian Burns injured his right knee in the fourth quarter. Burns is set to have further testing to determine the severity.\n\n\r\n              “There is some hope there, but we won’t know until we see the MRI,” Harbaugh said.\n\n\r\n              If it is significant like Dart’s injury, it is another blow to the Giants after Harbaugh’s tenure as coach got off to a promising start. Burns ranked second in the NFL in sacks last season behind record-setter Myles Garrett.\n\n\r\n              Offensively, Harbaugh and offensive coordinator Matt Nagy kept the ball on the ground, in part because of the conditions, and piled up 138 yards rushing with no turnovers.\n\n\r\n              “Everybody saw how windy it was,” center John Michael Schmitz said. “Everybody saw how wet the ball was. I feel like our offense did a great job protecting the ball.”\n\n\r\n              The Titans (0-3) narrowly avoided becoming the first NFL team in eight years to not pick up a first down before halftime. The offense run by former Giants coach Brian Daboll was held off the scoreboard until Cam Ward connected with Wan’Dale Robinson for the game’s only touchdown with 5:35 left.\n\n\r\n              They would have scored earlier had Ward not missed Carnell Tate wide open in the end zone. Ward found a rhythm late but was picked off by Holland in the end zone, his first interception this season.\n\n\r\n              “This was a hard-fought win,” said Holland, who had an interception for a third consecutive game. “For us to come out here and get this done, it was nitty gritty. But it was important.”\n\n\r\n              Tennessee had blunders on offense, defense and special teams. Chimere Dike, an All-Pro as a rookie, fumbled the opening kickoff. It's the second fumble he has lost this season.\n\n\r\n              “We’re in a place right now we’re 0-3,” defensive tackle Jeffery Simmons said. “We need to do more. We’ve got to stop talking: say less, do more.”\n\n\r\n              Simmons shines\r\n              Simmons was one of the few bright spots for the Titans. He sacked Winston in the first half, when he made a career-high seven tackles. No other defensive tackle has had as many in a half this season.\n\n\r\n              “Jeffery Simmons is on the other side of the ball, and you try to trap him — he’s an animal,” Giants running back Cam Skattebo said. “ He wrecked a couple plays, and shout-out to him, man. He’s a respected player in this league. He destroyed some plays.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Titans: Starting right guard Fernando Carmona left with an ankle injury on the team's first offensive play. ... Running back Tyjae Spears injured an ankle in the second half. \n\n\r\n              Giants: Fullback Patrick Ricard had a cut on his nose, Harbaugh said.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Titans: Visit Baltimore next Sunday\n\n\r\n              Giants: Host Arizona next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/0ace16b2ce0d4b2daec243ca325ce1e91123/0ace16b2ce0d4b2daec243ca325ce1e91123.jpg"},"401872950":{"h":"Aaron Rodgers outduels Joe Burrow as the Steelers edge the Bengals 30-27 on a late Boswell kick","d":"— Mike McCarthy and Aaron Rodgers have been around each other a long time.","b":"PITTSBURGH -- — Mike McCarthy and Aaron Rodgers have been around each other a long time. \n\n\r\n              So much so that when the Pittsburgh Steelers coach told Rodgers and the rest of the offense it was time to “cut it loose” before Sunday's early-season litmus test against Cincinnati, it didn't really register with the quarterback he's known for 20 years.\n\n\r\n              “I feel like he says that every week,” Rodgers said, then added with a laugh, “It's not like, ‘Hey, this week ...’”\n\n\r\n              The message was not new. The results were another matter.\n\n\r\n              Rodgers bounced back from a nightmarish performance in a dismal loss to New England by throwing for 292 yards and three touchdowns to outduel Joe Burrow as the Steelers pulled out a 30-27 thriller.\n\n\r\n              “I think resilience is critical to any, any type of success, whether it’s playing one division game (or) overcoming a loss last week,” McCarthy said.\n\n\r\n              Pittsburgh (2-1) did both by showcasing the kind of explosiveness Rodgers has said was possible if everyone just settled down and did their jobs.\n\n\r\n              Rodgers completed 19 of 34 passes, including a 47-yard catch-and-run to massive tight end Darnell Washington that helped set up Chris Boswell’s go-ahead, 33-yard field goal with 2:18 remaining.\n\n\r\n              The Steelers (2-1) rolled up 411 yards on a revamped Cincinnati defense that had fueled the Bengals' promising start. Jaylen Warren, dealing with a shoulder injury that limited him in practice, had 176 total yards (127 rushing, 49 receiving), numbers that could have been a little bit higher if he hadn't tripped over his own feet while running free inside the Cincinnati 10 in the late going.\n\n\r\n              Pittsburgh was forced to settle for Boswell's third field goal, giving Burrow and the Bengals plenty of time to respond.\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati made its way into Steelers territory in the final minute and was on the fringe of kicker Evan McPherson's range when Steelers defensive lineman Derrick Harmon crashed into Burrow. The ball squirted free, and Pittsburgh's Sebastian Joseph-Day fell on it to spoil the Bengals' chances at the franchise's first 3-0 start since 2015.\n\n\r\n              “Trying to take a shot at the end zone, trying not to take the sack, trying to get the ball out, (and I) fumbled it,” Burrow said.\n\n\r\n              Bengals lament their missed opportunities\r\n              The Bengals' opportunity to stamp themselves as front-runners in the AFC North disappeared with some self-inflicted wounds.\n\n\r\n              A roughing-the-punter penalty in the third quarter extended a Pittsburgh drive that ended with a touchdown pass from Rodgers to DK Metcalf. Cincinnati's Ke'Shawn Williams later muffed a punt deep in Bengals territory to set up the second of Boswell's three field goals that tied the game at 27 with 8:45 remaining.\n\n\r\n              “Some games, you just go get your butt kicked and there's not much to say about it,” Bengals coach Zac Taylor said. “This one, we had our chances. Pittsburgh took advantage of theirs. We didn't take advantage of ours.”\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati backed up Pittsburgh deep in its territory late in the fourth, but the Steelers responded even after a run of 80-plus yards by Warren was wiped out by a holding penalty on Metcalf. \n\n\r\n              Rodgers, playing behind a new-look offensive line that included rookie first-round pick Max Iheanachor at right tackle, guided the Steelers 77 yards anyway. Boswell's winner helped Pittsburgh improve to 10-2 in its last 12 games following a loss of 15 points or more.\n\n\r\n              It also gave the Steelers a dose of belief following an ugly performance against the Patriots in which Rodgers was sacked four times and the line struggled to protect him.\n\n\r\n              The 42-year-old four-time MVP insisted it was not time to panic. Iheanachor's promotion added a dose of physicality on the line, and Pittsburgh was dynamic. Rodgers hit nine receivers and Warren cut loose for field-flipping runs that were hard to come by during the opening two weeks.\n\n\r\n              Warren's shoulder was so tender on Saturday that McCarthy advised Rodgers not to throw it to him during a walkthrough. There were no such issues 24 hours later, when Warren's punishing approach helped ignite the Steelers on a day the offense needed to pick up the slack for a defense that let Burrow play pitch-and-catch with Ja'Marr Chase and Tee Higgins.\n\n\r\n              Yet with the clock ticking down, Harmon provided Pittsburgh's only sack. He wasn't trying to strip Burrow. It just kind of happened, and Joseph-Day fell on it.\n\n\r\n              McCarthy had jotted down in his notes this week that Pittsburgh would need 31 points to win. The Steelers fell one shy, not that it mattered.\n\n\r\n              “We've got to play more sound football than we did today to beat a team like that,” Burrow said.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Bengals: LB Demetrius Knight left in the first quarter ... WR Colbie Young (knee) exited in the first half with a knee injury.\n\n\r\n              Steelers: CB Brandin Eichols was evaluated for a concussion after colliding with Williams during the botched second-half punt return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Bengals: At Jacksonville next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Steelers: Visit Cleveland on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/dm_260927_Benglas_loss_vs_Steelers_IA/dm_260927_Benglas_loss_vs_Steelers_IA.jpg"},"401872955":{"h":"Commanders snap Seattle's 12-game winning streak, beat the Seahawks 33-31","d":"— Two quarters after Washington linebacker Leo Chenal was carted off with a scary neck injury, backup Kain Medrano delivered the biggest play of the game.","b":"LANDOVER, Md. -- — Two quarters after Washington linebacker Leo Chenal was carted off with a scary neck injury, backup Kain Medrano delivered the biggest play of the game.\n\n\r\n              Medrano returned an interception 50 yards for a touchdown with 3:57 to play, and the Commanders held on for a 33-31 victory Sunday, handing the Seattle Seahawks their first loss since November.\n\n\r\n              Marcus Mariota — another backup forced into duty — threw three touchdown passes for Washington, which forced three turnovers in its home opener.\n\n\r\n              “One turns into two, two turns into three. Ball just falls that way,” Medrano said. “That momentum that we have, we're hunting the ball, and that's what we do as a defense.”\n\n\r\n              Washington came into the game without linebacker Frankie Luvu, who was out with a groin injury. Chenal started, but then he went down late in the second quarter. It was a difficult moment — his teammates all came off the sideline to surround him as he was being tended to on the field. \n\n\r\n              Coach Dan Quinn said after the game Chenal was at the hospital but had feeling in his extremities.\n\n\r\n              “We're definitely thinking of him,” Quinn said. “He's a great teammate. We love him. Anytime a guy gets banged up like that, you're just thinking of him and pulling for him as much as you can.\"\n\n\r\n              Without Chenal, Washington's linebackers were still a factor. Rookie Sonny Styles had an interception, and after Washington had taken a 27-24 lead, Medrano picked off Sam Darnold’s pass over the middle and ran it back all the way to put the Commanders (1-2) two scores ahead.\n\n\r\n              “I was on the tight end, just playing my coverage. I'm about to get the PBU,” Styles said. “He just steps in front of me. I'm like, ‘Oh my gosh.’ I'm running down the sideline with my hands up. It was crazy. It was a crazy play.”\n\n\r\n              A penalty during the celebration moved the extra point back 15 yards, and Drew Stevens missed it. Then Darnold immediately found Jaxon Smith-Njigba for three straight completions, and his 7-yard scoring pass to Cooper Kupp cut the lead to two with 2:51 to go.\n\n\r\n              Washington, which was 1 for 11 on third down to that point, converted two on the final drive to run out the clock. Rachaad White caught the Seahawks off guard on a third-and-9 run, going 19 yards around right end.\n\n\r\n              “They got to an audible against zero,” Seattle coach Mike Macdonald said. “They made a good check, and we didn't make the play.”\n\n\r\n              Then Mariota ran for 9 yards on third-and-7 to seal the win.\n\n\r\n              The Seahawks (2-1) had won 12 straight, including their postseason run to last season's Super Bowl title, but Mariota was solid in place of the injured Jayden Daniels.\n\n\r\n              “The more that adversity hits, the more that the circumstances kind of pin us against the wall, these guys find a way,” Mariota said. “I'm grateful to be a part of it.”\n\n\r\n              Smith-Njigba caught 10 passes for 128 yards and two touchdowns for the Seahawks, and he even completed a short throw on a trick play in the final quarter, but Darnold was intercepted twice in his return from his Week 1 glute injury.\n\n\r\n              Seattle's Jadarian Price fumbled early in the game, giving Washington the ball at the Seattle 21, and then the rookie running back didn't touch the ball again until the second half. The Commanders took advantage of the short field, opening the scoring on Mariota's 6-yard touchdown pass to White.\n\n\r\n              Washington nearly took a 14-0 lead, but Jacory Croskey-Merritt's long TD run was called back for an illegal formation penalty. Seattle tied it at 7 in the second quarter on Darnold's 1-yard scoring pass to Smith-Njigba.\n\n\r\n              The Commanders went back ahead on an 11-yard touchdown strike from Mariota to Treylon Burks. Then Jason Myers and Stevens traded 57-yard field goals in the final minute of the half.\n\n\r\n              Mariota threw a 30-yard touchdown pass to Terry McLaurin to make it 24-10. The Seahawks answered with a 60-yard touchdown drive. On first down from the Washington 13, Darnold found tight end Eric Saubert, who appeared to lose the ball as he neared the goal line, only to regain control of it in the end zone. The touchdown cut the lead in half.\n\n\r\n              An 11-yard touchdown pass from Darnold to Smith-Njigba with 11:53 left in the fourth tied it at 24.\n\n\r\n              Impressive company\r\n              Smith-Njigba became the second player to produce at least 120 yards receiving and a touchdown catch in each of his team's first three games of a season. The other was Hall of Famer Jerry Rice, in 1989 with San Francisco.\n\n\r\n              Smith-Njigba already has six touchdown receptions.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Seahawks: S Nick Emmanwori injured a hamstring in the first half.\n\n\r\n              Commanders: OL Lucas Patrick injured an ankle in the first half. ... S Percy Butler was in the concussion protocol.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Seahawks: Host the Los Angeles Chargers next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Commanders: Face Indianapolis on Sunday in London.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0927/45a7b466-d6ab-4ecb-8614-7d628109f7af/45a7b466-d6ab-4ecb-8614-7d628109f7af.jpg"},"401872957":{"h":"Lawrence throws 3 TD passes as Jaguars rout Patriots and raise more questions about Maye","d":"— Jacksonville Jaguars coach Liam Coen spent all week telling his players that what mattered most was how they responded.","b":"JACKSONVILLE, Fla. -- — Jacksonville Jaguars coach Liam Coen spent all week telling his players that what mattered most was how they responded.\n\n\r\n              It would have been tough to imagine anything better than this.\n\n\r\n              Trevor Lawrence threw three touchdown passes, including one on which Parker Washington made a fingertip catch in the end zone, and the Jaguars bounced back from their worst offensive performance in nearly a year to dominate New England 35-6 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “You get what you emphasize so much with this team,\" said Coen, who became the first NFL coach to win 15 of his first 20 games since Green Bay's Matt LaFleur in 2020.\n\n\r\n              The Jaguars (2-1) won their sixth consecutive regular-season home game and raised more questions about Drake Maye’s regression with the Patriots.\n\n\r\n              Maye fumbled twice, losing one, and threw two more interceptions before getting pulled in the fourth quarter. He now has one TD pass and six INTs in three games. He threw 31 touchdowns and eight picks last season for the AFC champion Patriots (1-2).\n\n\r\n              “I’m always going to be confident in myself to make plays,\" Maye said. \"If there’s one thing, I’m going to go out there and compete. I’m going to leave it all out there. Competing comes with being confident and being confident in this league is realizing that these mistakes, you got to eliminate them. \n\n\r\n              \"More that we get out of our own way, the more the confidence in myself and this team will build. That’s something that starts with practice and it starts with me.”\n\n\r\n              Maye’s performance in this one was more alarming than his three-interception fourth quarter in the season opener at Seattle. The ball slipped out of his right hand with no one around him during one play. He threw into coverage on a third-and-goal play, resulting in Travis Hunter’s first career interception. He also threw a pass directly to cornerback Jarrian Jones that set up another score for Jacksonville.\n\n\r\n              “I think he knows what to do,\" Patriots coach Mike Vrabel said. \"I think we have to do it better. We all want to make plays. We just have to make some good ones. If it’s not there, I think sometimes you can want it too badly, and we can’t force it.”\n\n\r\n              New England had other issues, too: Andy Borregales missed a 38-yard field goal; Kyle Williams mistakenly stepped out of bounds on a kickoff return; and Mack Hollins was flagged for taunting at the end of a 9-yard reception, turning a fourth-and-1 play into a field goal.\n\n\r\n              The Jaguars played much cleaner.\n\n\r\n              Lawrence completed 19 of 29 passes for 182 yards, with an interception on a tipped pass. He connected with Jakobi Meyers and Josh Cameron for TDs. Cameron got significant snaps in place of Brian Thomas Jr., who dropped two passes in last week’s 20-13 loss at Denver.\n\n\r\n              Meyers had seven receptions for 64 yards against his former team, and Bhayshul Tuten added 73 yards rushing and a touchdown — after Lawrence took a knee at the 1 to try to milk the clock with more than eight minutes left.\n\n\r\n              “We were just ready to play,” Lawrence said. “Guys were flying around. At the end of the day, that's what it's about. It's the players executing the plan. You got to have a good plan, and we did. But it's about us going out there and owning it, playing fast and executing. We did all those things today.”\n\n\r\n              Patriots pull off a crazy play\r\n              Facing a first-and-30 play after back-to-back penalties, the Patriots dialed up some trickiness in Jacksonville territory. And it worked despite a lot of adversity.\n\n\r\n              Maye threw a backward pass to Efton Chism III, who had to elude Jones’ tackle near the sideline. Chism stayed on his feet and completed a pass to Mack Hollins that was tipped by cornerback Travis Hunter. It was far from pretty but resulted in a 16-yard gain that led to a field goal.\n\n\r\n              Little nearly hit an NFL record field goal\r\n              Jacksonville’s Cam Little just missed making a 70-yard field goal, which would have broken his NFL record. Little attempted the long kick at the end of the first half and pushed it just a couple feet right.\n\n\r\n              Little had the distance, which was no surprise considering he made a 70-yard in a preseason game against Pittsburgh in 2025. He set the real NFL mark later that year with a 68-yarder at Las Vegas in early November.\n\n\r\n              Key injuries\r\n              Patriots: RG Greg Van Roten left in the first half with a thigh injury and did not return. DT Christian Barmore (shoulder) and CB Carlton Davis III also missed snaps but returned.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Patriots: Play at Buffalo next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Jaguars: Play at Cincinnati next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/9dc1af8cf9844626b2405a1e0f83cd431118/9dc1af8cf9844626b2405a1e0f83cd431118.jpg"},"401872958":{"h":"Purdy throws 4 TD passes to lead the injury-depleted 49ers to a 36-30 win vs. the Cardinals","d":"— The San Francisco 49ers keep losing key players to injuries and still keep winning, thanks in large part to the play of quarterback Brock Purdy.","b":"SANTA CLARA, Calif. -- — The San Francisco 49ers keep losing key players to injuries and still keep winning, thanks in large part to the play of quarterback Brock Purdy.\n\n\r\n              Purdy threw four touchdown passes, with the first coming on a trick play, and the injury-depleted 49ers held on for a 36-30 victory over the Arizona Cardinals on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco lost wide receiver Mike Evans to a rib injury in the first half and left tackle Trent Williams to a stinger in the second half, but still managed to hold on to start 3-0 for the third time in the last four seasons.\n\n\r\n              “It’s the NFL, it sucks when your teammates go down, but that’s part of the game,” Purdy said. “As a quarterback, it’s also part of handling that and doing what I can, which is my job.”\n\n\r\n              Purdy is doing that very well with his 10 total touchdowns — pass and rushing — tied for the most in franchise history after three games as he leads the league with a 133.1 passer rating.\n\n\r\n              “Brock is amazing,” linebacker Fred Warner said. “The offense is humming right now. It’s our job as a defense to make sure that we handle our business.”\n\n\r\n              The game began well for the Niners before the injuries started mounting and they found themselves in a tight game against the Cardinals (1-2), who have lost 22 of their last 26 games in the division.\n\n\r\n              Purdy helped ice it when he threw a 20-yard TD pass to George Kittle with 1:31 to play. The Cardinals added a late field goal, but Deebo Samuel recovered an onside kick to seal the win. \n\n\r\n              Coach Kyle Shanahan dialed up a hook-and-ladder call on San Francisco’s third play from scrimmage to turn a precarious third-and-12 from deep in 49ers territory into an 82-yard score. Purdy threw a quick 6-yard pass to Evans, who flipped it back 4 yards to a sprinting Samuel as he was being tackled well short of the first down. Samuel then sprinted down the sideline, ran through a tackle attempt by Budda Baker near midfield and scored to make it 7-0 on an 82-yard play.\n\n\r\n              “It was crazy because we walked through that play like once this week,” Samuel said. “It’s kind of crazy. Once I saw Mike catch the ball and there was two guys on him, I was like if I get around here, there won’t be anybody. Budda showed up and I showed the speed that people don’t think I got.”\n\n\r\n              Purdy then connected on a 23-yard TD pass to Evans on the second drive and the Niners built the lead to 23-6 when Christian McCaffrey scored on a 1-yard run on the opening drive of the second half. \n\n\r\n              But Arizona fought back as Jacoby Brissett threw two TD passes on the next three possessions, connecting on a 5-yarder to Michael Wilson and a 1-yarder to Jeremiyah Love after the Niners lost the ball on a muffed punt.\n\n\r\n              But the 49ers answered with a touchdown drive that was aided when Josh Sweat was called for roughing the passer after an apparent third-down stop. Purdy then found Kittle on a 10-yard score to make it 29-20 even though Eddy Pineiro missed his second extra point of the game. \n\n\r\n              Purdy finished 15 for 27 for 297 yards. \n\n\r\n              Brissett scored on a 1-yard sneak with 2:51 to play to make it 29-27, but it wasn't enough as the Cardinals were hurt by 10 penalties and settling for two field goals on drives to the red zone in the first half.\n\n\r\n              “Any time in the first half when we got something going, and then we’d have self-inflicted wounds,” Brissett said. “Against good teams, it’s like being a death wish. Against good teams, you can’t do that. You can’t beat yourself and try to beat them.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              49ers: Shanahan had no update on the extent of the injuries, but the one to Evans is most concerning as San Francisco is already without receivers Ricky Pearsall, De'Zhaun Stribling and Demarcus Robinson. ... Williams returned to the sideline after being taken off on a cart, but didn't come back into the game. ... LB Dre Greenlaw left in the second half with a quadriceps injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Cardinals: Visit the New York Giants on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              49ers: Host Denver on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0928/dm_260928_Niners_beat_Cardinals_IA/dm_260928_Niners_beat_Cardinals_IA.jpg"},"401872959":{"h":"Vikings beat Buccaneers 23-16 but lose Jefferson to injury. Bucs' Mayfield dislocates thumb","d":"— A stifling defense and a big play on special teams helped the Minnesota Vikings stay undefeated.","b":"TAMPA, Fla. -- — A stifling defense and a big play on special teams helped the Minnesota Vikings stay undefeated.\n\n\r\n              Myles Price returned a punt 86 yards for a score, Kyler Murray threw a 41-yard TD pass to Jordan Addison and the Vikings beat the Tampa Bay Buccaneers 23-16 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              It was a costly game for both teams.\n\n\r\n              Baker Mayfield dislocated his right thumb on a desperation pass late in the fourth quarter. He was screaming in pain on the ground before walking off and was replaced by rookie Jalon Daniels in the final minute. Coach Todd Bowles said X-rays were negative, but Mayfield will have an MRI on Monday to determine the extent of the injury.\n\n\r\n              Two-time All-Pro wide receiver Justin Jefferson didn’t return for Minnesota after injuring his right ankle in the first quarter. Coach Kevin O'Connell said Jefferson was close to returning but couldn't quite get the push-off he needed.\n\n\r\n              The Vikings are 3-0 for the second time in three years. The Buccaneers are 0-3 for the first time since 2014, when they finished 2-14 and got the No. 1 overall draft pick, taking Jameis Winston.\n\n\r\n              “We knew that was going to be an all-out, full team effort,” O'Connell said. “We have to get better in all three phases. I got to do a better job but to be 3-0, to have the type of support we had today from the fans, unbelievable to have that many in the house.”\n\n\r\n              Murray was 15 of 29 for 168 yards with one TD pass and one interception in his first full game with the Vikings. He sustained a concussion in the first quarter of the season opener and Carson Wentz led Minnesota to consecutive wins.\n\n\r\n              “There were some throws I missed. For my first my first full game since last October, I felt it started to slow down for me but, at the end of the day, offensively we just weren't on the same page,” Murray said. \n\n\r\n              Mayfield hasn’t played like a $55 million-a-year quarterback since the Buccaneers gave him a $165 million extension before the season. He was 17 of 34 for 217 yards with one TD and one interception, taking six sacks.\n\n\r\n              “I'm sure it looks bleak,” receiver Chris Godwin Jr. said. “It's not ideal. Definitely not the start we wanted and we never want to lose (Mayfield) but that's where we are right now and the reality is we have to pull ourselves out of this hole.”\n\n\r\n              Tampa Bay’s woeful offense couldn’t do much against coordinator Brian Flores’ defense.\n\n\r\n              Mayfield had an 85-yard touchdown pass to Godwin on third-and-30 negated by a holding penalty on Luke Goedeke in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              That summed up the offense’s struggles.\n\n\r\n              Mayfield’s 14-yard scramble on second-and-9 later was negated by a holding penalty on Payne Durham. But the Bucs got a first down on third-and-29 when Joshua Metellus hit Emeka Egbuka helmet-to-helmet. Again, the offense had to settle for a field goal. Chase McLaughlin’s 54-yarder cut it to 17-16.\n\n\r\n              Will Reichard’s 56-yard field goal extended Minnesota’s lead to 20-16 with 3:10 remaining. He added a 43-yarder to cap the scoring.\n\n\r\n              Murray connected with Addison on a deep throw on third-and-8 to give the Vikings a 17-10 lead in the second quarter. Addison beat Jacob Parrish in one-on-one coverage, making a smooth adjustment on the ball.\n\n\r\n              After A’Shawn Robinson intercepted Murray’s pass to set up a first down at the Vikings 22 in the third quarter, the Bucs went nowhere. Bucky Irving ran 3 yards and then dropped a pass. Mayfield got sacked on third down and McLaughlin’s 38-yarder cut it to 17-13.\n\n\r\n              The Buccaneers fell behind 10-0 after just one possession. Following a three-and-out, Price darted up the middle, cut to the right and sprinted down the sideline for his first career touchdown.\n\n\r\n              Price said he had a dream earlier in the week that he scored on a return. Then he dreamed about it again while taking a pregame nap.\n\n\r\n              “It means everything,” Price said. “I'm so excited.”\n\n\r\n              It wasn't Minnesota's first punt return for a TD since 2016.\n\n\r\n              Mayfield fired a 40-yard TD pass down the seam to rookie Ted Hurst III to cut it to 10-7.\n\n\r\n              The Buccaneers had a first down at Minnesota’s 7 after Mayfield and Cade Otton connected on a 37-yard pass. But consecutive holding penalties on Cody Mauch and Graham Barton derailed the drive and McLaughlin kicked a 28-yard field goal to tie it.\n\n\r\n              Reichard can't miss\r\n              Reichard has made 26 consecutive field goals, dating to last season.\n\n\r\n              Brotherly love\r\n              Buccaneers left guard Ben Bredeson played against his younger brother, Vikings rookie fullback Max Bredeson. Their older brother, Jack, is the director of player information for the New York Mets. All three went to Michigan.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Vikings: Jefferson injured his right ankle when a defender fell on it. He walked to the locker room after watching a couple of series. ... P Brett Thorson (hamstring) was inactive. ... CB Charles Demmings left with a hamstring injury. ... TE Josh Oliver had a biceps injury.\n\n\r\n              Buccaneers: Edge rusher Rueben Bain Jr. (groin) and LB Josiah Trotter (shoulder) were inactive. ... WR Jalen McMillan left in the first quarter with a knee injury and didn't return. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Vikings: Host Miami next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Buccaneers: Host Green Bay next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/239cedc558074514a553752bb3c2ed9c1182/239cedc558074514a553752bb3c2ed9c1182.jpg"},"401872960":{"h":"Jackson's pass, alert timeout call lead to Loop's 56-yard field goal as Ravens beat Cowboys 34-31","d":"— Lamar Jackson used his big right arm and a little bit of quick thinking to lead the Baltimore Ravens to one of their shortest and most memorable drives in franchise history.","b":"RIO DE JANEIRO -- — Lamar Jackson used his big right arm and a little bit of quick thinking to lead the Baltimore Ravens to one of their shortest and most memorable drives in franchise history.\n\n\r\n              Jackson completed a 27-yard pass to Zay Flowers and alertly called a timeout with 1 second left, setting up Tyler Loop’s 56-yard field goal to cap a stunning 7-second drive that gave the Ravens a 34-31 victory over the Dallas Cowboys on Sunday in Brazil.\n\n\r\n              “Everyone was on the same timing,\" Jackson said of the final drive. “The line did a great job protecting, Zay ran a great route and I just had to deliver the baby. He caught it and as soon as he got down, I was like 'Please see me ref!'\"\n\n\r\n              The Ravens (2-1) and Cowboys (1-2) put on a entertaining show in front of an announced crowd of 72,792 during the NFL’s third game in Brazil and first in the city of Rio de Janeiro, engaging in a back-and-forth contest that featured plenty of big plays.\n\n\r\n              “Unbelievable play by Lamar and Zay,” Ravens coach Jesse Minter said. ”Heads up on the time. You practice for those moments, you wait for them to come up and it came up today and we were ready.\"\n\n\r\n              For a moment, it looked as though the Cowboys would win in regulation.\n\n\r\n              Ravens cornerback Nate Wiggins was called for pass interference with 23 seconds left when his arms locked with CeeDee Lamb, giving the Cowboys the ball at the Ravens 2. After an incomplete pass, the Cowboys were called for a false start, which backed them up to the 7. Dallas settled for Brandon Aubrey's 23-yard field goal to tie it at 31 with 7 seconds left.\n\n\r\n              And that set up the Ravens' improbable finish that was capped by Loop's kick sailing through the uprights and the jubilant Ravens swarming their kicker during a wild celebration at the Maracana.\n\n\r\n              “I am hurting, they are hurting,” Cowboys coach Brian Schottenheimer said. “It is a long trip (to Rio), but no one cares. We also have a long flight back.”\n\n\r\n              Jackson found rookie tight end Matt Hibner for a 2-yard touchdown for a 31-28 lead with 1:30 remaining. That gave the Cowboys and Dak Prescott enough time for the tying drive.\n\n\r\n              Baltimore’s Derrick Henry ran for 89 yards and two touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens were in control with a 24-13 lead in the third quarter, but the Cowboys weren’t done. Jake Ferguson atoned for a previous turnover when Prescott found him for a 19-yard touchdown. Dallas added a 2-point conversion on a nice catch by Lamb, cutting the deficit to 24-21.\n\n\r\n              Baltimore appeared it was about to go up by 10 a few minutes later, but Henry was stuffed in the backfield on fourth-and-inches at the goal line when rookie linebacker Jaishawn Barham sliced through the offensive line and made the tackle.\n\n\r\n              Dallas kept the momentum in its favor with a 96-yard touchdown drive that included a 49-yard pass to Lamb. KaVontae Turpin had a 9-yard TD run to give the Cowboys a 28-24 lead with just under 10 minutes left.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens’ defense came up big in the third quarter, forcing a Cowboys turnover when Ferguson fumbled a shovel pass from Prescott. Dallas tried for a 59-yard field goal later in the third, but Keondre Jackson blocked Aubrey’s kick to preserve the Ravens’ 17-13 lead and regain possession at mid field.\n\n\r\n              Jackson hit Chris Moore for a 4-yard touchdown four plays later and a 24-13 lead.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens recovered from a rough start, scoring 17 straight points to take a 17-13 lead into halftime.\n\n\r\n              Henry moves into 9th place on NFL rushing list\r\n              Henry had a 5-yard run early in the second quarter to move into ninth place on the NFL’s career rushing list, passing Pro Football Hall of Famer Eric Dickerson, who had 13,259. If the five-time Pro Bowl selection continues at his current pace, he could also pass Jerome Bettis (13,662), LaDainian Tomlinson (13,684) and even Curtis Martin (14,101) this season.\n\n\r\n              Playing surface holds up\r\n              After concerns about the playing surface this week, the grass field at the Maracana appeared to hold up pretty well.\n\n\r\n              The NFL has been playing in Brazil since 2024, but the two previous games were at the 48,000-seat NeoQuimica Arena in Sao Paulo. In the first year of the Brazil game, its turf was also criticized and forced players of the Philadelphia Eagles and the Green Bay Packers to change their cleats to stop slipping.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Ravens: Tight end Mark Andrews (hand) left in the first half, but returned later in the game.\n\n\r\n              Cowboys: Safety Jalen Thompson (hamstring) left in the second quarter. Safety Markquese Bell (dehydration), left in the third quarter, but returned.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Ravens: Host the Tennessee Titans next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Cowboys: At the Houston Texans next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0927/9f0d444ef845490a8fa5aa7f16edb7f91220/9f0d444ef845490a8fa5aa7f16edb7f91220.jpg"},"401872961":{"h":"Kirk Cousins passes for 3 TDs and the unbeaten Raiders come back to top the Saints 35-27","d":"— It took the Las Vegas Raiders an entire season to accumulate three victories in 2025.","b":"NEW ORLEANS -- — It took the Las Vegas Raiders an entire season to accumulate three victories in 2025.\n\n\r\n              Rookie coach Klint Kubiak and new quarterback Kirk Cousins have helped the Silver and Black match that total by Week 3 of 2026.\n\n\r\n              Cousins passed for 248 yards and three touchdowns, and the Raiders overcame an 11-point second-half deficit to beat the New Orleans Saints 35-27 on Sunday and remain unbeaten.\n\n\r\n              The 38-year-old Cousins “has the experience, he has the maturity and he’s making plays,” said the 39-year-old Kubiak, who parlayed his one-year stint as offensive coordinator of the Super Bowl champion Seattle Seahawks into his first head coaching job.\n\n\r\n              The game shifted on a play that will be seen as controversial and unjust by some — and certainly by Saints fans.\n\n\r\n              The series that led to the Raiders' go-ahead score began when Las Vegas defensive end Malcolm Koonce sacked Tyler Shough as the Saints QB started his throwing motion.\n\n\r\n              The ball went backward and was ruled a fumble that was recovered by the Raiders (3-0) — a different ruling than was made after a similar play involving the Saints (1-2) in Week 1 at Detroit. On that play, an apparent New Orleans strip-sack of the Lions' Jared Goff was ruled incomplete on video review after the Saints thought they had recovered a fumble for a touchdown.\n\n\r\n              Likewise, Shough said, “I thought my arm was going forward.”\n\n\r\n              “I’m not exactly sure on the rule,” he added. “It doesn’t really matter. I’ve got to get it out sooner, regardless.”\n\n\r\n              Boos rained down from Superdome stands, and Saints coach Kellen Moore gestured incredulously with his arms out to his side and palms up when officials, following a video review, upheld the call of a fumble.\n\n\r\n              “I just assumed it was a strip-sack,” Koonce said. “We just got a little bit of momentum and started balling.”\n\n\r\n              Six plays later, Cousins connected with Cody White for a 4-yard touchdown to make it 28-27.\n\n\r\n              “Momentum swung against us early in the third quarter, but the defense just kept showing up for us, creating turnovers and giving us short fields,” Cousins said. “We were able to capitalize on them.”\n\n\r\n              Cousins gave Las Vegas an eight-point lead with a 4-yard TD pass to Brock Bowers. The Saints got the ball twice more with a chance to tie it, but one drive stalled on Shough's fourth-down fumble and another on Juwan Johnson's fumble with 1:02 left.\n\n\r\n              “Turnovers in the fourth quarter got us and we weren’t able to overcome those. Those guys finished better than us,” Moore said. “We had our opportunities and we didn’t get it done.”\n\n\r\n              Shough passed for 255 yards and a career-high four touchdowns — including two to Johnson. Shough's other scoring passes went to Noah Fant from short range. But the second-year QB also turned the ball over three times, twice on fumbles and on an interception by cornerback Hezekiah Masses that set up the Raiders' final scoring drive.\n\n\r\n              Mike Washington ignited the Raiders’ comeback with a tackle-breaking 36-yard scoring run that cut New Orleans’ lead to 27-22 in the third quarter. Matt Gay kicked three field goals for Las Vegas, which also failed on a pair of 2-point conversions in the second half.\n\n\r\n              New Orleans’ Chris Olave caught nine passes for 107 yards, his second 100-yard game this season.\n\n\r\n              Bowers makes a difference in his season debut\r\n              Bowers, making his season debut after coming back from a knee injury during training camp, caught 10 passes for 116 yards and his short TD.\n\n\r\n              “To come off an injury and play the way he did was very impressive,” Kubiak said. “We needed it. We’re not surprised.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Raiders: WR Jack Bech left the game with a fractured forearm. Guard Jackson Powers-Johnson left with a groin injury.\n\n\r\n              Saints: LB Pete Werner left in the first half with a neck and shoulder stinger. RB Travis Etienne left with an apparent hamstring pull. Edge rusher Anfernee Jennings left with a knee injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Raiders: Host Kansas City next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Saints: Host Atlanta on Monday, Oct. 5.\n\n\r\n              ----\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0928/9062628f-e609-4964-9a7c-0d241b3724e3/9062628f-e609-4964-9a7c-0d241b3724e3.jpg"},"401872962":{"h":"Bo Nix and Talanoa Hufanga lead the Broncos past the Rams 30-26 in a Sunday night thriller","d":"- Bo Nix didn't flinch from his slow start and Talanoa Hufanga didn't lose faith after dropping two interceptions Sunday night.","b":"<p>DENVER -- Bo Nix didn't flinch from his slow start and Talanoa Hufanga didn't lose faith after dropping two interceptions Sunday night.</p>\n<p>Both bounced back in a big way, rallying the Denver Broncos past Matthew Stafford and the Los Angeles Rams 30-26 in the latest come-from-behind thriller that's become so common in the Mile High City.</p>\n<p>Nix's keeper from inches outside the goal line with 47 seconds left capped a wild final five minutes that included a 66-yard pick-6 by Hufanga, a quick touchdown response by Los Angeles and a game-sealing second picked-off pass by Denver's do-everything safety.</p>\n<p>\"I actually had to have a pep talk with myself,\" after dropping two interceptions earlier in the game, said Hufanga, who finished with a sack, a tackle for loss, a quarterback hit and three pass breakups to go with his pair of interceptions.</p>\n<p>The Broncos (2-1) trailed 16-0 at halftime and went three-and-out to start the second half but rallied for a comeback, one-score win for the second straight week.</p>\n<p>\"In the huddle we feel a lot of belief that we've been there before and we have what it takes to go down against a really good defense and score points,\" said Nix, who put the Broncos in front by reaching over the scrum with both hands squeezing the football.</p>\n<p>Matthew Stafford, whose 17 comebacks in the last 2 minutes are the most by any active quarterback, quickly drove the Rams to the Denver 14, where he spiked the ball with 2 seconds left.</p>\n<p>After false start moved the ball back to the 19, the Rams (1-2) sent out five receivers but Hufanga secured his second interception, this one in the end zone.</p>\n<p>\"Obviously a gutsy win,\" Broncos coach Sean Payton said.</p>\n<p>\"I mean, it's a tough one,\" Stafford said after the Rams, preseason favorites to represent the NFC in the Super Bowl, suffered a second loss in three weeks.</p>\n<p>Hufanga's 66-yard pick-6 with 4:49 left gave Denver its first lead at 23-19. He jumped in front of running back Kyren Williams, cradled Stafford's pass and weaved his way down the field, past the hard-charging QB and into the end zone.</p>\n<p>\"To be honest, Matt Stafford is a lot faster than you think,\" Hufanga said. \"I might be slow, but he's fast. I'll give him his credit because I had to cut back on that play.\"</p>\n<p>\"Hufanga did a really nice job of fighting through the Y (receiver) and I left it a little bit back behind him,\" Stafford lamented. \"If I just dirt it, or whatever, we come away with three points. I'll take myself trying to make those plays all day.\"</p>\n<p>Hufanga said he had told himself to stay patient for more opportunities to come after dropping two earlier potential interceptions of Stafford. \"And man, two big plays.\"</p>\n<p>Just four plays later, however, Stafford hit Konata Mumpfield from 48 yards out when cornerback Riley Moss fell down, restoring the Rams' lead at 26-23.</p>\n<p>Nix then drove Denver 65 yards in 10 plays with the aid of three defensive penalties and, with the ball nearly touching the goal line, leaped over the pile for the go-ahead TD. Nix had completions of 39 and 21 yards on that drive.</p>\n<p>Cornerback Josh Wallace would have had his second interception of the night on that drive, but he was whistled for defensive pass interference on Pat Bryant on third-and-6 from the LA 11. That gave Denver a new set of downs at the 7.</p>\n<p>\"You try to be physical as a DB,\" Rams safety Quentin Lake said. \"Sometimes, you know, I think at the end of the day, as you know, the rules are the rules. Can't be too handsy. It's unfortunate.\"</p>\n<p>The Rams led 16-0 at halftime but it was tied going into the fourth after Nix threw a pair of touchdown passes and made two 2-point conversion throws: \"We knew it was just a two-score game,\" said Nix.</p>\n<p>Harrison Mevis' fourth field goal, which followed an interception by Wallace, restored LA's lead at 19-16. He was also good from 36, 35 and 40 yards before halftime.</p>\n<p>The Broncos cut their deficit in half on Nix's 5-yard strike to Bryant and 2-point conversion pass to Jaylen Waddle on a throw between the hands of Wallace midway through the third quarter. The Broncos tied it on another touchdown and 2-pointer. Nate Adkins caught an 8-yard throw from Nix, who found Bryant again to tie the game.</p>\n<p>Stafford finished 30 for 55 for 390 yards. He threw 32 passes in the first half, the most of his career. With Puca Nacua out again with a hip injury, Davante Adams had seven receptions for 137 yards, giving him 15 catches for 332 yards over his last two games, the best such stretch of his career.</p>\n<p>Injuries Rams: CB Jaylen Watson (shoulder) got hurt just before halftime while guarding Courtland Sutton and was carted to the locker room from the sideline. LA lost two tight ends on its first touchdown drive, although Terrance Ferguson (ankle) returned on the Rams' next series. Also injured on the drive was Colby Parkinson (shoulder).</p>\n<p>Broncos: Denver was without its Game 2 hero, rookie RB Jonah Coleman, who went on injured reserve with a high ankle sprain after his TD run secured the Broncos' 20-13 win over Jacksonville.</p>\n<p>Up next Rams: At Philadelphia next Sunday.</p>\n<p>Broncos: Visit San Francisco next Sunday.</p>\n<p>------</p>\n<p>See AP's full NFL coverage here</p>","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/0928/cf5f25fc-0720-4c3d-bac2-e99c54536423/cf5f25fc-0720-4c3d-bac2-e99c54536423.jpg"},"401872963":{"h":"Case Keenum drives Chicago to 3 touchdowns as the Bears beat the Eagles 27-7","d":"— Case Keenum was on target and on time all night long.","b":"CHICAGO -- — Case Keenum was on target and on time all night long.\n\n\r\n              It turns out the third-string quarterback for the Chicago Bears is pretty good, too.\n\n\r\n              Keenum passed for two touchdowns in his first start in almost three years, and the Bears shut down Jalen Hurts and the Philadelphia Eagles for a 27-7 victory on Monday night.\n\n\r\n              Stepping in for Caleb Williams, Keenum was 24 for 34 for 247 yards. He also scored his first rushing touchdown since 2019 on a 1-yard plunge with 7:19 left, capping a 13-play drive that took more than eight minutes off the clock.\n\n\r\n              “He knows where everyone is supposed to be on every single play and so he is really quick to recognize coverage,” Bears coach Ben Johnson said. “He knows why we’re calling the play and what we’re looking for and if we’re not getting that he’s accelerating through it really quickly. That’s what you get when you’re 15-plus years playing that position in this league.”\n\n\r\n              It was Keenum’s first action since he started for Houston in a 36-22 loss to Cleveland on Dec. 24, 2023. Williams was sidelined by a right hamstring injury, and Tyson Bagent backed up Keenum after he cleared the NFL’s concussion protocol on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “To get a shot again like this? Yeah, you dream of it,” Keenum said. “That’s why I'm still here doing this when I get knocked down. I got knocked down my whole career. I just keep getting up, coming to work, just keep bouncing back.”\n\n\r\n              Kalif Raymond had six receptions for 90 yards and a touchdown as Chicago (2-1) rebounded from an ugly 9-3 loss to Minnesota.\n\n\r\n              Philadelphia (2-1) turned the ball over three times and failed to record a takeaway for the third consecutive game to begin the season. Hurts was 16 for 25 for 153 yards, and Saquon Barkley rushed for 82 yards on 15 carries.\n\n\r\n              “I think we all have to own that loss right there,” Eagles coach Nick Sirianni said. “I don’t think anybody coached well. I don’t think anyone played well. It was a true team loss. And when it’s a true team loss, you know I have to look at myself first.”\n\n\r\n              Down 20-7, the Eagles lined up to go for it on fourth-and-1 at their own 49 with about four minutes left in the third quarter. But Hurts was unable to draw a flag for offside, and the Eagles took a delay-of-game penalty before punting it away.\n\n\r\n              Hurts was picked off by T.J. Edwards on a fourth-and-5 play from the Chicago 46 in the final minute of the third. Edwards dropped a potential interception on third down on Philadelphia’s first drive of the second half.\n\n\r\n              “I liked the decision to go for it on fourth-and-5,” Sirianni said. “Hindsight, I feel like I should have went for it on that fourth-and-1 1/2.”\n\n\r\n              Keenum capped Chicago's first drive with a shovel pass to Luther Burden for an 8-yard touchdown, and he connected with a wide-open Raymond on a 41-yard TD in the second half.\n\n\r\n              On the drive that ended with his seventh career rushing touchdown, making it 27-7 with 7:19 remaining, Keenum drew an offside penalty on Moro Ojomo on a fourth-and-2 play from the Eagles 39.\n\n\r\n              “Poised, calm and honestly a guy who was having a lot of fun,” Raymond said of Keenum. “The whole week he was like, you guys having fun, because I am. That's the way he was. He loves the game.”\n\n\r\n              Trailing 7-0, Hurts drove Philadelphia deep into Chicago territory in the second quarter before he absorbed a late hit by linebacker D'Marco Jackson along the sideline. Hurts was pulled off the field to be checked for a concussion, and backup Andy Dalton was intercepted by rookie Dillon Thieneman in the back of the end zone with 6:14 left.\n\n\r\n              Hurts said he was waiting for clearance to go back in when Dalton turned it over.\n\n\r\n              “I was just waiting for the thumbs-up,” Hurts said. “I felt fine, we already checked it. I don’t know the extent of the process. Obviously wish we could have expedited things. I know I felt fine.”\n\n\r\n              Hurts scored on a quarterback sneak as time expired in the first half, but Chicago controlled the final two periods. Philadelphia gained just 62 of its 248 yards in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Chicago finished with 24 first downs and 128 yards rushing. The Bears had 28 first downs and 281 yards on the ground in a 24-15 win at Philadelphia last season.\n\n\r\n              He is back\r\n              Zach Ertz had one reception for 9 yards for Philadelphia in his first game of the season. The veteran tight end rejoined the Eagles when he was signed to the team's practice squad last week.\n\n\r\n              The 35-year-old Ertz spent his first eight-plus seasons with the Eagles, earning three Pro Bowl appearances.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Bears: LT Braxton Jones departed with 10:22 left in the third because of a knee injury. ... Raymond was shaken up on a punt return with 3:12 remaining in the third, but he returned to the game. ... DB Cam Lewis had a hip injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Eagles: Host the Los Angeles Rams on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bears: Host the New York Jets on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/0928/9bd3b7b7f9624f29b828c6e0d8cc1f261397/9bd3b7b7f9624f29b828c6e0d8cc1f261397.jpg"},"401872964":{"h":"Watson leads another game-winning drive, Szmyt hits 56-yard FG and Browns beat Steelers 27-24","d":"— Deshaun Watson and the Cleveland Browns are developing a knack for coming up big in the fourth quarter.","b":"CLEVELAND -- — Deshaun Watson and the Cleveland Browns are developing a knack for coming up big in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Watson led a go-ahead drive in the fourth quarter for the third straight week as he moved the Browns into position for Andre Szmyt's career-long 56-yard field goal with 10 seconds remaining that gave Cleveland a 27-24 win over the Pittsburgh Steelers on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              “I knew it was money. He told me the landmark, so he was just lining it up and we see it all the time,” Watson said about Szmyt’s second field goal, which gave the Browns a 3-1 record for the first time since 2001.\n\n\r\n              The Browns had a 21-7 lead late in the first half before the Steelers rallied to tie it at 24-all. Aaron Rodgers threw a 3-yard touchdown pass to Pat Freiermuth with 1:44 remaining and Rodgers sneaked in for the 2-point conversion.\n\n\r\n              Watson went 4 for 5 for 46 yards on the winning drive, including two completions to Jerry Jeudy for 27 yards. The 10-year veteran was sacked for a 4-yard loss with 19 seconds left, which put the ball on the Steelers 38, but it ended up not mattering as Szmyt drilled the kick down the middle to extend his streak of made field goals to 22.\n\n\r\n              \"I honestly said, ‘I’ll hit it from anywhere,\"' but I told him ‘let’s get to the 42, maybe a 60-yard field goal,' He got me a couple extra yards, so kudos to him,\" said Szmyt, who had his third field goal in the final 15 seconds since joining the Browns last season.\n\n\r\n              Cleveland has won eight straight Thursday night games, the longest active streak and fourth longest in NFL history. Four have come against the Steelers.\n\n\r\n              Pittsburgh (2-2) is 0-10 on the road against AFC North rivals on Thursdays.\n\n\r\n              Watson completed 24 of 33 passes for 268 yards with a touchdown and interception. He passed for 181 yards in the first half, his most since being traded by Houston to Cleveland in 2022.\n\n\r\n              “Happy for Deshaun. Not happy about the sack there at the end, but three straight fourth-quarter comebacks really cool for him and our team,” Browns coach Todd Monken said.\n\n\r\n              Rodgers, who was 22 of 40 for 299 yards with three touchdowns and two interceptions, had one final chance to get the Steelers in field goal range, but he was picked off by Grant Delpit at the Browns 18 on a Hail Mary throw with 1 second left.\n\n\r\n              Pittsburgh's Jaylen Warren rushed for 93 yards on 17 carries and DK Metcalf had five catches for 115 yards.\n\n\r\n              “I wouldn't say it was our cleanest game all the way around,” Rodgers said. “They were getting after us on some of the long yardage stuff that we weren't winning a bunch. I missed some throws I usually hit.”\n\n\r\n              The Browns defense got consistent pressure on Rodgers with five sacks and 10 quarterback hits.\n\n\r\n              “We had some protection challenges. It was a five-man pressure, man-to-man game. They established that right from the beginning,” Steelers coach Mike McCarthy said. “I think it was an aggressive game plan by them. I kind of expected some of it, and we were one play short.”\n\n\r\n              Big second quarter by Browns\r\n              Pittsburgh controlled the first 15 minutes and took a 7-0 lead when Roman Wilson took a shovel pass 12 yards around left end on a jet sweep.\n\n\r\n              Momentum shifted on the second play of the second quarter after Denzel Ward picked off Rodgers’ pass intended for Michael Pittman Jr. at the Browns 44.\n\n\r\n              The rest of the first half was all Browns. It was the first time since Week 13 of the 2020 season against Tennessee that the Browns scored at least 21 points in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              “Denzel flipped it, and then we kind of got things going a little bit, got in a good rhythm,” Monken said.\n\n\r\n              Jaleel McLaughlin tied it at 7 with 12:14 remaining in the first half on a 28-yard run around right end. The fourth-year running back broke a pair of tackles at the 22 and went through a narrow opening at the 15 before scoring.\n\n\r\n              Cleveland took a 14-7 lead midway through the second quarter on Quinshon Judkins’ 2-yard run off right guard. The touchdown came three plays after Watson found Denzel Boston for a 60-yard completion to the Steelers 14 on the first play of the drive.\n\n\r\n              Harold Fannin Jr. then hauled in his third touchdown in two games on a 2-yard pass from Watson on third-and-goal with 1:22 remaining.\n\n\r\n              The Steelers got within 21-10 on Boswell’s 31-yard field goal with 8 seconds left before halftime.\n\n\r\n              Rodgers rolled right and connected with Washington for a 21-yard touchdown early in the fourth quarter to bring Pittsburgh within 21-16. The 2-point conversion attempt failed.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Steelers: DT Derrick Harmon (foot) was carted to the locker room during the second quarter and did not return. CB Jamel Dean (ankle) went out late in the second quarter and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Browns: DT Mason Graham (left knee) was injured in the first quarter and did not return. DT Mike Hall Jr. (right ankle) went out late in the second quarter but came back in the third.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Steelers: Host Indianapolis on Oct. 11.\n\n\r\n              Browns: At the New York Jets on Oct. 11.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1001/00566164e84141c19d2c4cf8f1167ad31438/00566164e84141c19d2c4cf8f1167ad31438.jpg"},"401872965":{"h":"Tea time as Jonathan Taylor runs for 2 TDs and Colts beat Commanders 30-13 in London","d":"— Sip some tea, score some touchdowns.","b":"LONDON -- — Sip some tea, score some touchdowns. \n\n\r\n              Jonathan Taylor's secret is out.\n\n\r\n              The Colts running back rushed for two touchdowns, quarterback Daniel Jones also ran one in and Indianapolis beat Washington 30-13 after the Commanders lost yet another quarterback to injury on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Taylor said he enjoyed some tea at dinner on the eve of the game at Tottenham Hotspur Stadium. \n\n\r\n              And then he mimicked the same after one of his 5-yard touchdown runs to help the Colts (2-2) win their second straight game.\n\n\r\n              “I had to show the world, hey, I had a really good tea,” Taylor said.\n\n\r\n              Marcus Mariota, who was starting in place of the injured Jayden Daniels, left the game after Washington’s second drive because of a knee injury. The veteran quarterback was sacked and didn’t return for the Commanders (1-3). \n\n\r\n              Coach Dan Quinn said it looks like an MCL injury and it's unknown how long he'll be out.\n\n\r\n              Third-string rookie quarterback Athan Kaliakmanis threw his first NFL touchdown pass to cut Indy's lead to 17-13 in the third quarter, but the Colts answered with Spencer Shrader's 52-yard field goal and Jones' 4-yard run early in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Taylor rushed for 95 yards on 20 carries as the Colts overcame two turnovers from Jones, who completed 19 of 34 passes for 143 yards with an interception and a fumble.\n\n\r\n              “Can’t afford to turn the ball over like that,” Jones said. “Got to understand when to take a chance, when not to, when to protect the ball, when you have a chance to make a play.”\n\n\r\n              Kaliakmanis threw a costly interception early in the third quarter and the Colts quickly capitalized. Linebacker Jaylon Carlies picked off Kaliakmanis' pass at the Washington 27 and returned it to the 20. Taylor’s second 5-yard run of the game made it 17-6.\n\n\r\n              Kaliakmanis bounced back on the next drive, though, hitting Treylon Burks on a 47-yard touchdown pass that closed the gap to 17-13. Burks got past cornerback Sauce Gardner and found space before hauling in the deep ball.\n\n\r\n              “It was surreal, man, it was absolutely surreal,” Kaliakmanis said, “but I've got to be honest, right now I'm just thinking about the loss. It's not about me. It's about this team.”\n\n\r\n              Shrader later added a 44-yard field goal — his third of the game — to extend Indy's lead to 30-13.\n\n\r\n              Kaliakmanis completed 15 of 33 passes for 186 yards with one touchdown and the interception.\n\n\r\n              The Colts led 10-6 at halftime after Taylor's first 5-yard touchdown run and then Shrader’s 48-yard field goal.\n\n\r\n              The Commanders failed to capitalize on two red zone opportunities in the first quarter, settling for field goals.\n\n\r\n              Odafe Oweh drove Colts left tackle Bernhard Raimann backward into Jones causing the quarterback to fumble on Indy's first drive. Oweh then recovered it at the 21. The Commanders had fourth-and-2 from the 13 but opted for Drew Stevens' 31-yarder.\n\n\r\n              Washington then stalled at the Colts 5 on their next drive. On third down, Mariota was sacked at the 11 by Adetomiwa Adebawore and Laiatu Latu. Mariota walked off gingerly to the sideline, and another Stevens field goal made it 6-0, but the quarterback didn't return.\n\n\r\n              Attendance was 60,907 for the 13th NFL game played at Tottenham. The league had recently reduced remaining ticket prices to 79 pounds ($105) including for some high-end seats. Attendance has never been below 60,000 in previous games. The venue opened in 2019 and features an NFL field below the Premier League team’s moveable soccer pitch. It was 70 degrees Fahrenheit at kickoff.\n\n\r\n              Global success\r\n              Taylor's big day made him the all-time leading rusher in NFL international games, the Colts said. Taylor had rushed for 244 yards and three touchdowns last season in Indy's 31-25 overtime victory over the Atlanta Falcons in Berlin.\n\n\r\n              “I hope that I can continue to stack that, now that I’m apparently the leader,” Taylor said.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Colts: LT Bernhard Raimann was hurt early in the third quarter but returned.... WR Ashton Dulin and DE Arden Key both sustained hamstring injuries.\n\n\r\n              Commanders: In addition to Mariota, WR Jaylin Lane had a “serious ankle injury,” Quinn said. DE Charles Omenihu had a groin problem... S Tyler Owens left late with a hamstring injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Colts: at Pittsburgh next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              Commanders: host New York Giants next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1004/e23b2653a5334a359f7406db206804df1339/e23b2653a5334a359f7406db206804df1339.jpg"},"401872971":{"h":"Maye overcomes his two turnovers with 3 TD passes in Patriots' 29-26 win over Bills","d":"— Drake Maye didn’t have to be perfect after his rough start to the season.","b":"ORCHARD PARK, N.Y. -- — Drake Maye didn’t have to be perfect after his rough start to the season. \n\n\r\n              The New England Patriots quarterback understood he simply needed to be more consistent.\n\n\r\n              Maye delivered by shaking off two first-half turnovers to finish with three touchdown passes — including Efton Chism’s one-handed catch with 1:50 remaining — in rallying New England to a 29-26 win over Josh Allen and the previously unbeaten Buffalo Bills on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              The three touchdowns were two more than Maye combined for in New England losing two of its first three games to open the season. The 29 offensive points scored against Buffalo matched the Patriots’ total from their first three outings.\n\n\r\n              “It’s relieving a little bit,” said Maye, who was facing criticism after opening the season throwing six interceptions and losing a fumble. “I know I’ve got to play better and still left some stuff out there today. But we’ve got to build on it.”\n\n\r\n              Patriots coach Mike Vrabel was far more enthusiastic about his third-year quarterback’s performance in an outcome that allowed the defending AFC East champions to improve to 2-2 and close the gap on their division rival Bills (3-1).\n\n\r\n              “Gets us back in the race. Josh (Allen) was unbelievable,” Vrabel said. “Felt like Drake went toe to toe with him.”\n\n\r\n              Indeed he did.\n\n\r\n              With New England trailing 26-21 following Allen’s 34-yard touchdown pass to Keon Coleman, Maye led an eight-play, 73-yard drive, which he capped with the completion to Chism.\n\n\r\n              Facing third-and-5, Maye rolled to his right and lobbed a perfectly placed pass to the second-year receiver, who caught it with his right hand and maintained control falling to the ground.\n\n\r\n              “Shoot, when I’m out there and the ball is in the air, I kind of black out,” said Chism, who was getting extended playing time after Mack Hollins left with a calf injury. “Drake gave me an opportunity and you have to come down with those opportunities.”\n\n\r\n              Maye finished 22 of 37 for 269 yards. Romeo Doubs had touchdown catches of 2 and 14 yards, and Rhamondre Stevenson scored on a 1-yard run.\n\n\r\n              The Bills, meanwhile, frittered away a chance to widen their division lead, losing for the first time under coach Joe Brady and first time at their new Highmark Stadium.\n\n\r\n              “The wins and losses are all the same to me. I don’t mean that in a negative way,” Brady said, before explaining his objective is always finding ways to be better.\n\n\r\n              “We’re not going to make excuses. We’re not gonna let others make excuses for us,” he added. “Let’s just start with ourselves and then we’ll continue to clean it up and obviously start with myself.”\n\n\r\n              Buffalo has much to clean up after committing two turnovers a week after a five-giveaway outing in a 24-16 win over the Los Angeles Chargers.\n\n\r\n              Receiver Khalil Shakir lost a fumble, and Allen’s interception — his third of the season — essentially sealed the loss in ending Buffalo’s final drive.\n\n\r\n              Despite having a minute left and the ball at the Buffalo 40, Allen turned overly aggressive. On second-and-6, he spun out of Dre’Mont Jones’ sack attempt, stepped up in the pocket and went deep only to have his underthrown pass intended for Josh Palmer intercepted by Craig Woodson.\n\n\r\n              “Should have thrown that one out of bounds and live another day. That’s what I should have done,” Allen said. “It stinks. I feel like we hurt ourselves.”\n\n\r\n              Allen finished 23 of 33 for 253 yards with a touchdown and also scored on a 2-yard run. James Cook scored on an 8-yard run.\n\n\r\n              The offense also appeared to struggle after DJ Moore left with a shoulder injury late in the first half. Defensively, tackle Ed Oliver was sidelined by a knee injury, and the unit was already missing its top cornerback, Christian Benford (toe).\n\n\r\n              Maye put the finishing touches on the win after Allen’s interception by rolling out to his right and lobbing a 12-yard pass to Hunter Henry in allowing the Patriots to run out the clock.\n\n\r\n              “I thought he just competed today, whether it was a bad snap, dropping the ball, whatever it may have been,” Vrabel said of Maye, who’s turnover issues extended to the start of last season's playoffs. “I’m happy for him, I’m proud of him. He deserves all the credit.”\n\n\r\n              Allen milestones\r\n              Allen’s 2-yard touchdown run to tie the game at 14 with one minute left in the first half was his 88th career from scrimmage (rushing/receiving) to break the team record held by Hall of Famers Thurman Thomas and Andre Reed. It was also Allen’s 86th TD rushing, moving him into a tie with Priest Holmes for 16th place on the NFL's career list.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Patriots: host the Las Vegas on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bills: at the Los Angeles Rams on \"Monday Night Football\" on Oct. 12\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1004/d91042a77f0b4922993942f7f90b16261042/d91042a77f0b4922993942f7f90b16261042.jpg"},"401872972":{"h":"Kyle Monangai scores 2 TDs as the Chicago Bears beat the New York Jets 23-12","d":"— The Chicago Bears kept handing the ball to Kyle Monangai and the New York Jets had precious little time to respond.","b":"CHICAGO -- — The Chicago Bears kept handing the ball to Kyle Monangai and the New York Jets had precious little time to respond.\n\n\r\n              It was that kind of day.\n\n\r\n              Monangai rushed for 146 yards and two touchdowns, and Chicago stuffed New York for a 23-12 victory on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “Shout out to the offensive line, the running backs, the whole offensive crew as well as the defense as well for having a great day,” wide receiver Rome Odunze said.\n\n\r\n              Devin Bush, Austin Booker and Dayo Odeyingbo each had a sack as Chicago limited New York to seven first downs and 157 yards overall. The Bears have allowed a total of 28 points in three games since their wild 59-37 victory at Carolina in their season opener.\n\n\r\n              “I thought those guys all stood out,” Bears coach Ben Johnson said. “It was another one of those days where it’s hard to single out any particular guy. I just thought everyone played really good football on that side.”\n\n\r\n              Chicago (3-1) had 34 first downs and ran 89 plays while possessing the ball for almost 43 minutes. The Bears also did not punt in a game for the first time since 2014.\n\n\r\n              New York (1-3) went 0 for 7 on third down and ran a total of 32 plays.\n\n\r\n              “We’ve got to execute and do whatever we can to keep our defense off the field,” Jets quarterback Geno Smith said. \"That’s a part of playing complementary football. I think we can do a lot better job in that area.”\n\n\r\n              It was the first time an NFL team had more first downs than its opponent had offensive plays since Pittsburgh's 43-0 victory over Cleveland on Sept. 12, 1999. It also was the first time since 1997 that the Bears held their opponent without a third-down conversion.\n\n\r\n              “We have to do a better job of complementing each other and that did not show up today at all,” Jets coach Aaron Glenn said. “When you have the discrepancy of 89 plays and 32 plays, it’s something we have to look at and something we have to continue to work on. That’s the only thing we can do is continue to work.”\n\n\r\n              Tyson Bagent passed for a career-high 268 yards for Chicago and Cairo Santos kicked three field goals. Odunze had six receptions for 94 yards.\n\n\r\n              Minkah Fitzpatrick stopped New York's NFL-record interception drought at 20 games, but the Jets dropped their third consecutive game since their season-opening win at Tennessee.\n\n\r\n              Smith found Isaiah Williams for a 58-yard touchdown pass in the fourth quarter, trimming New York's deficit to 23-12 with 9:16 left. It was Williams' first TD in his 27th career game.\n\n\r\n              But Jason Sanders missed the ensuing extra point and Chicago ran out the clock with an 18-play, 77-yard drive.\n\n\r\n              “Toward the end of the game, if they don’t want to be out there anymore, you just keep running at them,” Monangai said, “running at them, 4-yard gain, 4-yard gain, 20-yarder come out of nowhere, big play in the passing game, and then 4-yard gain, 4-yard gain, it gets tiring. So you definitely feel it.”\n\n\r\n              Monangai finished with 30 carries. He was dealing with a thumb injury at the end of the game and he said he will know more about it Monday.\n\n\r\n              Bagent was 25 for 34 while subbing for Caleb Williams, who missed his second straight game because of a right hamstring injury. Case Keenum started Monday night's 27-7 win over Philadelphia, passing for 247 yards and two touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              Keenum was behind center for Chicago's first play against New York and Bagent was split out wide. Keenum pitched the ball to D’Andre Swift for a 2-yard gain before Bagent took over at QB.\n\n\r\n              “I got all these memos saying that Tyson was starting before I ever made an announcement so I wanted to get Case out there the first time,” Johnson said. “It was like a ceremonial first pitch out there, handing off the torch.”\n\n\r\n              Chicago turned the ball over on downs at the New York 1 on its first drive. But Monangai's 1-yard TD on fourth-and-goal gave the Bears a 7-0 lead late in the first quarter.\n\n\r\n              The Bears opened a 20-6 lead on Monangai's 5-yard touchdown with 2:26 left in the third.\n\n\r\n              “No flinch from him,” Bagent said. “He’s been outstanding ever since he got here. Does the right thing.” \n\n\r\n              Fitzpatrick stepped in front of Colston Loveland and picked off a pass by Bagent in the second quarter for New York's first interception since Ashtyn Davis had two against Miami in the 2024 season finale. But the Jets had three more interceptions taken off the board because of penalties.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Jets: Edge rusher Braiden McGregor departed with a chest injury. ... Cornerback Jarvis Brownlee Jr. departed with a concussion.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Jets: Host Cleveland next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bears: At Green Bay next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1004/dm_261004_Bears_beat_Jets_IA/dm_261004_Bears_beat_Jets_IA.jpg"},"401872969":{"h":"Trevor Lawrence finally gets 1st win over Bengals as Jaguars hold on for 22-17 victory","d":"— The Jaguars didn't have a nightmare finish in Cincinnati for the second straight year.","b":"CINCINNATI -- — The Jaguars didn't have a nightmare finish in Cincinnati for the second straight year.\n\n\r\n              Jacksonville's defense made two key stops in the red zone — including stopping Bengals running back Chase Brown 1 yard shy of a first down with 50 seconds remaining — and Trevor Lawrence passed for 227 yards and a touchdown as the Jaguars held off the Bengals 22-17 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Being able to hold on for a tight victory marked a milestone for Liam Coen's team. Jacksonville lost to the Bengals 31-27 in Week 2 last year on Jake Browning's 1-yard sneak with 18 seconds remaining.\n\n\r\n              The Jags had another tough Week 2 this season at Denver, when the Broncos rallied with two fourth-quarter touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              “You look at the last time we were in this locker room, we weren’t able to close out a game where we weren’t able to get it done offensively and the defense had to hold strong and we end up (in) a game of inches,” Coen said. “Proud of the growth and the group for sticking with it and overcoming a lot of bad stuff, a lot of self-inflicted wounds.”\n\n\r\n              Lawrence completed 18 of 23 passes to beat the Bengals for the first time in four attempts. The sixth-year quarterback, the top pick in the 2021 NFL draft, has at least one win over every AFC opponent.\n\n\r\n              It was Lawrence's first victory over Bengals counterpart Joe Burrow in four matchups, dating to the 2020 College Football Playoff title game between Clemson and LSU.\n\n\r\n              “It felt good. You look at all the different games we’ve played and they all went about like today went, except we were on the other end of it,” Lawrence said. “You’re going to have to win games like this throughout the season where you maybe don’t play your best and it’s not pretty, but find a way.”\n\n\r\n              Brian Thomas Jr. and Chris Rodriguez scored touchdowns for the Jaguars and Cam Little kicked three field goals.\n\n\r\n              Burrow passed for 428 yards for the Bengals, who lost All-Pro wide receiver Ja'Marr Chase to a concussion in the second quarter. Tee Higgins had 11 receptions for 151 yards before he went down with a groin injury in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Burrow, who completed 39 of 54 passes and had his eighth 400-yard game, also threw two interceptions — one that Jacksonville converted into a touchdown and the other when the Bengals were in the red zone.\n\n\r\n              “We know we had some chances. We've got to find a way to not turn the ball over there, specifically the one in the red zone. That's the one I'm the most upset about. I got pressured and was trying to get rid of it and didn't see (the defender),” said Burrow, who had a TD pass to Mike Gesicki along with a QB sneak for a score, both in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Bengals' last drive comes up short\r\n              Rodriguez ran 25 yards off left guard to put the Jaguars ahead 22-10 with 6:25 remaining. The 2-point conversion failed.\n\n\r\n              The Bengals answered three minutes later with a nine-play touchdown drive, capped by Burrow’s 1-yard sneak.\n\n\r\n              Parker Washington appeared to put it away for the Jaguars on third-and-10 when he caught a screen pass from Lawrence and went 39 yards to the Bengals 16. But replays showed Washington stepped out of bounds at the Bengals 48.\n\n\r\n              After a punt, the Bengals got the ball at their own 17 with 1:44 remaining. Burrow completed three straight passes and moved Cincinnati to the Jaguars 21, but then misfired on three straight passes.\n\n\r\n              On fourth-and-10, Burrow found Brown in the flat. Brown beat linebacker Foyesade Oluokun but safety Rayuan Lane III stopped him 1 yard short at the 12.\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati has dropped two straight after winning its first two for the second straight year, which did not make coach Zac Taylor happy. The eighth-year coach could be heard from the adjoining interview room ripping into his team in the locker room.\n\n\r\n              “I believe in these guys. Really disappointed that we lost. We’re not that kind of a team this year that’s going to take consolation in losses. I think we lost tough games to some really good teams,” Taylor said.\n\n\r\n              Jaguars seize momentum late in first half\r\n              Jacksonville had a 6-3 advantage before Jourdan Lewis picked off Burrow's pass intended for Dohnte Meyers and returned it 45 yards up the left sideline to the Bengals 18.\n\n\r\n              It was the ninth straight game in which the Jaguars have recorded an interception, a franchise record and the NFL's longest active streak.\n\n\r\n              Four plays later, Lawrence found Thomas wide open in the back of the end zone for a 7-yard touchdown to make it 13-3 with 1:23 remaining in the first half.\n\n\r\n              Flag day\r\n              Referee Alex Moore's crew called 24 penalties for 244 yards. That crew came in with the fourth-most penalties called during the first three weeks after leading the league last season. The first half saw 16 penalties for 149 yards.\n\n\r\n              “I’ve just never quite seen something that much the first half of the football game,” Coen said.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Jaguars: Safety Caleb Ransaw went out with a knee injury in the first quarter and did not return. Safety Eric Murray was ruled out in the second half because of a concussion.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Jaguars: Face the Philadelphia Eagles in London next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Bengals: At Miami next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1005/0422f7d9-ed41-4be4-b67d-b30dc036eda5/0422f7d9-ed41-4be4-b67d-b30dc036eda5.jpg"},"401872966":{"h":"Winston throws 3 TD passes and Banks' pick-6 seals Giants' 36-24 win over Cardinals","d":"— Jameis Winston showed off the good, bad and ugly of his football career and it required another late defensive stand to get it done. It was far from a masterpiece, but the New York Giants will take it.","b":"EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. -- — Jameis Winston showed off the good, bad and ugly of his football career and it required another late defensive stand to get it done. It was far from a masterpiece, but the New York Giants will take it.\n\n\r\n              Winston threw three touchdown passes to offset a pair of interceptions, Deonte Banks had a 98-yard pick-6 to seal it and the Giants won another not-so-pretty game, defeating the Arizona Cardinals 36-24 on Sunday to take sole possession of first place in the NFC East.\n\n\r\n              “It wasn’t the most beautiful game, as far as playing perfectly: It was a winning game,” coach John Harbaugh said. “That’s what winning teams do: They find ways to win games, and that’s why they’re winning teams.”\n\n\r\n              Making his second start in place of injured franchise quarterback Jaxson Dart, Winston again did what he needed to do to engineer a victory with TD passes to Malachi Fields, Malik Nabers and Theo Johnson. Winston's 12-yard toss to Fields with 4:30 remaining in the second quarter was the team's first touchdown since the season opener, ending a drought that lasted nine-plus quarters.\n\n\r\n              Winston finished 18 of 29 for 250 yards in a roller-coaster showing of highs and lows. J.J. McCarthy was in uniform six days after the Giants (3-1) acquired him in a trade with Minnesota but did not play.\n\n\r\n              \"It just gave us an option in case we wanted to put J.J. out there and run certain plays that we had prepared for him,\" said Harbaugh, who added McCarthy was not close to entering the game.\n\n\r\n              Nabers more than doubled his receiving yardage this season by making six catches for 112 yards. He had 96 through his first three games back from a serious knee injury. \n\n\r\n              “I knew it was coming this game,” Nabers said. “I don’t know — I just felt good. I felt good through the whole week. I just didn’t want to jinx myself, so I was just trying to be humble and just wait for the game to come to me.”\n\n\r\n              The defense also made some big plays, sacking Brissett twice and rebounding from allowing two long touchdown drives in the first 12 minutes. For a second game in a row, New York got an interception after its opponent reached the red zone, and Banks made up for his pass interference penalty a few plays earlier that Harbaugh called crazy.\n\n\r\n              “I thought you had to touch a guy for it to be pass interference,” Harbaugh said. “He didn’t bat an eye. Nobody flinched, nobody blinked.”\n\n\r\n              Dominic Zvada continued to validate Harbaugh's trust in another rookie kicker by improving to 9 of 9 on field goal attempts.\n\n\r\n              Jacoby Brissett was 21 of 35 and threw three interceptions — the last coming on the final play — which turned out to be the difference. Arizona (1-3) blew a 14-3 lead after Brissett connected with Marvin Harrison Jr. for a TD and Tyler Allgeier ran into the end zone in the first quarter.\n\n\r\n              “We kind of got in our own way,\" Brissett said. \"We made some plays after getting stops, but you can’t have three interceptions.”\n\n\r\n              The Cardinals gained just 138 yards the rest of the way, losing their third in a row since winning coach Mike LaFleur's debut in Week 1 at the Los Angeles Chargers.\n\n\r\n              “All three loses we’ve had over the last three weeks have been different,\" LaFleur said. \"This one is going to sting. It's not going to be the most fun ride home. There are certainly things I know I could have done better. We have to get right back to work because no one is going to feel sorry for us.”\n\n\r\n              Arvell Reese impresses in front\r\n               of ‘LT’\r\n              Rookie linebacker Arvell Reese picked off Brissett for his first interception in the NFL as part of a dominant performance that included nine tackles. And the No. 5 pick in the draft did so with Hall of Famer Lawrence Taylor in attendance as part of the 40th anniversary celebration of the 1986 Super Bowl championship team at halftime.\n\n\r\n              “He has been great,” Taylor said. “I think he’s a good player.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Cardinals: Safety Andrew Wingard was carted off with a knee injury and right guard Isaiah Adams with an ankle injury. ... Tight end Hunter Long left with a knee injury. ... Left tackle Paris Johnson walked off the field holding his left arm late.\n\n\r\n              Giants: Receiver/returner Braxton Berrios (foot), defensive lineman Chauncey Golston (neck) and safety Jason Pinnock (concussion) left with injuries.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Cardinals: Host Detroit next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Giants: Visit Washington next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1004/ba35d786-ab90-4eef-9877-d60ffb9071f4/ba35d786-ab90-4eef-9877-d60ffb9071f4.jpg"},"401872970":{"h":"Rams rally behind Stafford, Williams from 10 points down in 4th quarter to beat Eagles 24-20","d":"— Sean McVay had seen enough of a Rams team mired in a funk. Los Angeles' coach huddled the entire offense around him as the clock ticked down toward the end of the third quarter and his team down by 10.","b":"PHILADELPHIA -- — Sean McVay had seen enough of a Rams team mired in a funk. Los Angeles' coach huddled the entire offense around him as the clock ticked down toward the end of the third quarter and his team down by 10.\n\n\r\n              The rallying message, per Matthew Stafford: “Just trying to put some belief in our team.”\n\n\r\n              A week after blowing a 16-point lead in a loss at Denver, perhaps that belief in the Rams was underserving. It was instead unwavering.\n\n\r\n              Oh, and a poor fourth-down decision by the Philadelphia Eagles played a role in the rally, too. \n\n\r\n              Kyren Williams ran for two touchdowns in the final four minutes as Stafford and the Rams rallied from a 10-point deficit to beat the Eagles 24-20 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “I think a lot of teams would have folded in those situations,” McVay said. “I think our defense kept giving us a reason to believe.”\n\n\r\n              The Rams (2-2) had struggled offensively all afternoon and a win seemed out of reach until late in the fourth quarter, when Eagles coach Nick Sirianni decided against kicking a chip-shot field goal that would have made it 23-10 and instead went for it on fourth down.\n\n\r\n              The Eagles (2-2) failed to convert, giving the Rams new life.\n\n\r\n              Stafford and the Rams were aided by an unnecessary roughness penalty that helped them cap a 98-yard drive on Williams’ 1-yard TD run with 3:59 left that made it 20-17.\n\n\r\n              The Eagles — who failed to convert a single third down on 12 tries — punted to set up Stafford for the winning drive. He connected with Puka Nacua three times and had a clutch 8-yard scramble on the 10-play, 79-yard march, and Williams finished it off when he broke a few tackles for a 7-yard touchdown with 1:10 remaining.\n\n\r\n              “Plenty of plays where we didn’t get the result we wanted,” Stafford said. “But we got the result we wanted at the end of the game.”\n\n\r\n              The Rams have seen their share of blown late leads of late under McVay, and Sirianni was 4-0 lifetime against his coaching counterpart.\n\n\r\n              Sirianni took the fall for the Eagles' failure to convert the fourth down that could have sealed the win.\n\n\r\n              “You go for it and you get it, great,” Sirianni said. “You go for it and don’t get it, that’s on the head coach.”\n\n\r\n              Once the Rams completed the comeback, McVay skipped and sprinted down the sideline in celebration and nearly leapfrogged safety Quentin Lake.\n\n\r\n              Did it all come down to the speech?\n\n\r\n              “It was just more of what is on my heart to just say, ‘Guys, we’re capable. You got to believe and we got to just string good plays together. Our defense is doing everything that they can to keep us in this game,'” McVay said.\n\n\r\n              Just don't call this one an upset. Sportsbooks had installed the Rams as road favorites.\n\n\r\n              Jalen Hurts was sacked twice on Philadelphia’s final possession and had a desperation pass knocked down. He threw two touchdown passes to Darius Cooper in the first half but finished just 12 of 27 for 93 yards.\n\n\r\n              The Eagles have been outscored 31-0 in the second half of their last two games, both losses. They had only 68 net yards passing and a measly 172 yards of total offense.\n\n\r\n              “Poor execution,” Hurts said. “I don't think there's any other way to put it.”\n\n\r\n              Cooper, who made the game-winning TD catch in a Week 2 win at Tennessee, had touchdown catches of 10 and 18 yards.\n\n\r\n              The Eagles entered with a depleted roster that had them down six starters. That number increased to 10 as star running back Saquon Barkley was among the Eagles who got hurt as the team prepares for a trip to London next week. Offensive lineman Lane Johnson was a late scratch for personal reasons.\n\n\r\n              The Rams, coming a late pass-interference penalty that swung their loss at Denver, played without three-time AP Defensive Player of the Year Aaron Donald. He sat out with a sore back. Nacua returned after a two-game absence with injuries and had a 2-yard rushing TD in the second quarter that cut the Eagles’ lead to 13-10. He finished with nine catches for 125 yards.\n\n\r\n              Stafford, the reigning MVP, was 32 of 51 for 317 yards and two interceptions. His picks led to 10 Eagles points in the first half and Philadelphia led 20-10 at halftime.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Rams WR Konata Mumpfield suffered a shoulder injury.\n\n\r\n              Barkley injured his right hamstring. Eagles cornerback Quinyon Mitchell suffered a knee injury and linebacker Jonathan Greenard left with an ankle injury. Tank Bigsby, who replaced Barkley, needed help off the field after a hard tackle late in the game.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              The Rams host Buffalo on Monday, Oct. 12.\n\n\r\n              The Eagles play Jacksonville next Sunday in London.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1004/768bf0e4-32e6-4119-8a9a-71ff9a0c0129/768bf0e4-32e6-4119-8a9a-71ff9a0c0129.jpg"},"401872968":{"h":"Jordan Love throws 2 touchdown passes, Packers edge Buccaneers 17-14","d":"— An ugly win left the Green Bay Packers dissatisfied.","b":"TAMPA, Fla. -- — An ugly win left the Green Bay Packers dissatisfied.\n\n\r\n              Jordan Love threw two touchdown passes and the Packers overcame two fourth-quarter turnovers to beat the Tampa Bay Buccaneers 17-14 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Undrafted rookie Jalon Daniels threw two picks that led to 10 points in his first career start filling in for Baker Mayfield, who missed a game for the first time in his three-plus seasons in Tampa Bay because of a dislocated thumb.\n\n\r\n              The Packers (2-2) wasted two opportunities to extend a three-point lead in the fourth quarter but the Buccaneers couldn't capitalize.\n\n\r\n              “There were some positive things but we’re never going to be satisfied with scoring 17 points in a game,” Packers coach Matt LaFleur said. “That’s just not to our standard and we have to find ways when we’re in the red area to convert. We can’t be turning the ball over. We can’t have penalties. That is a stingy defense. You have to give them a lot of credit. … But we also have to clean up a lot of stuff. It’s gotta get fixed and it’s gotta get fixed now.”\n\n\r\n              Tykee Smith forced Kaleb Johnson to fumble inside the 5 with just over nine minutes left in the game. A’Shawn Robinson recovered at the 4 but the ensuing drive went nowhere.\n\n\r\n              “A great teacher is the bench. If guys don’t start holding onto the ball, they’re gonna be sitting on the bench,” LaFleur said bluntly.\n\n\r\n              The Packers then had a first-and-15 at the Buccaneers 26 when Jacob Parrish picked Love's pass and gave Tampa Bay the ball at the 15 and a chance for a comeback win. \n\n\r\n              Instead, Daniels threw a short screen pass to Emeka Egbuka on third-and-16 and incomplete on fourth down from the Packers 47. Coach Todd Bowles passed up a 65-yard field-goal attempt by Chase McLaughlin, who made one from that distance last year.\n\n\r\n              Bowles said a little wind factored into the decision not to try it.\n\n\r\n              The Buccaneers are 0-4 for the first time since 2013. Only the 1992 Chargers overcame an 0-4 start to make the playoffs. \n\n\r\n              “I’m the head coach. I can do more. I can always do more. I’ll always take the blame,\" Bowles said. \"We’re gonna fight, we’re gonna stick together and we’re gonna figure this thing out. And I’ll lead them to that. It’s my job.”\n\n\r\n              Love tossed a 2-yard touchdown pass to Matthew Golden on fourth down to give the Packers a 7-0 lead in the second quarter. Evan Williams made a sliding interception near the sideline to give Green Bay the ball at the 32. Love connected with Tucker Kraft for 10 yards on fourth-and-1 to keep the drive going. Kraft also took a direct snap and sneaked for a first down on another fourth-and-1 at the Buccaneers 10.\n\n\r\n              The Packers turned an interception by Edgerrin Cooper in the final minute of the first half into a field goal. Cooper stepped in front of Daniels' pass to Kameron Johnson, caught the ball falling to the ground, got up and returned it 18 yards to the Buccaneers 44. Trey Smack kicked a 58-yarder to give Green Bay a 10-7 lead.\n\n\r\n              Love's 2-yard TD pass to Kraft on the opening drive of the third quarter extended the lead to 17-7. \n\n\r\n              “We’ll take the win for sure. It was sloppy on those last drives,” Love said. “We have a chance to put the game out of reach. Two turnovers is unacceptable. That’s something we have to clean up. … That was the disappointing part of the game.”\n\n\r\n              Sean Tucker ran in from the 2 to tie it at 7. Daniels completed both of his passes for 27 yards combined on the drive and scrambled 9 yards before Tucker's score. \n\n\r\n              Daniels connected with Kenneth Gainwell on a 10-yard TD pass to cut the deficit to 17-14 early in the fourth quarter. \n\n\r\n              Depleted Packers \r\n              Cooper was carted off the field in the third quarter with an Achilles tendon injury. The Packers are still missing three-time All-Pro edge rusher Micah Parsons, who is recovering from surgery for a torn ACL suffered last December.\n\n\r\n              Love got his best protection this season despite playing played behind an offensive line missing three starters: Zach Bako-Bewele, Aaron Banks and Jacob Monk. Laken Tomlinson became the fourth player to start at left guard. Veteran guard Kevin Zeitler signed earlier in the week was inactive. \n\n\r\n              After taking 31 QB hits in the first three games, Love took two.\n\n\r\n              “The O-line did a great job,” Love said. “Pass protection, run game, just keeping me clean and giving me time. They did a great job and you can feel the difference back there for sure.”\n\n\r\n              I\r\n              njuries\r\n              Packers: CB Brandon Cisse hurt his neck and was being evaluated for concussion. ... LB Isaiah McDuffie replaced Cooper and briefly left with an eye injury. ... RB Chris Brooks and DT Devonte Wyatt both left in the first half with ankle injuries. ... Banks (toe, knee) and Monk (quadriceps) will return while Bako-Bewele (knee) is out for the season. \n\n\r\n              Buccaneers: Edge rusher Rueben Bain Jr. (groin), CB Benjamin Morrison (quadriceps), TE Ko Kieft (elbow) were inactive. ... S Antoine Winfield Jr. hurt his abdomen and a rib late in the fourth quarter. ... LB SirVocea Dennis injured his ankle. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Packers: Host Chicago next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Buccaneers: Visit Dallas on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1004/ceeddd1b-66c9-4927-a4e8-50db5e796a2f/ceeddd1b-66c9-4927-a4e8-50db5e796a2f.jpg"},"401872973":{"h":"Ravens beat Titans 24-18 but lose Lamar Jackson to ankle injury. Henry scores against his old team","d":"— The Baltimore Ravens could have used a quiet, boring victory as a heavy favorite at home.","b":"BALTIMORE -- — The Baltimore Ravens could have used a quiet, boring victory as a heavy favorite at home.\n\n\r\n              They were halfway toward exactly that — before Lamar Jackson's latest injury.\n\n\r\n              Jackson's ankle appeared to roll under a Tennessee defender in the final seconds of the second quarter, and although the Ravens held on for a 24-18 win over the Titans on Sunday, they didn't score after their star quarterback left the game. Tyler Huntley, no stranger to replacing Jackson when needed, took the snaps for Baltimore in the second half.\n\n\r\n              “We obviously played maybe a little more bit more conservative in the second half with the weather and the lead,” Ravens coach Jesse Minter said. “Proud of ‘Snoop’ coming in there when his number's called.”\n\n\r\n              Jackson threw for 222 yards and two touchdowns before leaving the game, and Derrick Henry scored against his former team. Zay Flowers had eight catches for 118 yards and a TD for the Ravens, who snapped a four-game home losing streak but now have to deal with more health concerns about their two-time MVP quarterback.\n\n\r\n              Jackson took a sack at the end of first half. Baltimore kicked a field goal on the next play, and when the third quarter started, Huntley replaced Jackson.\n\n\r\n              By then, the Ravens (3-1) were already up 24-7. With something of a patchwork offensive line at the moment, they built the lead mostly through passing. Huntley went 8 of 9 for 63 yards, but Baltimore did little offensively after halftime on a drizzly day in Charm City.\n\n\r\n              “It was just take care of the ball,” Huntley said. “It was raining, weather, and you know we had the lead, so it was just take care of the ball and just do what I could do for the team.”\n\n\r\n              Tennessee (0-4) is now 6-32 since Henry left the Titans to join the Ravens via free agency before the 2024 season. This was the teams' first meeting since then.\n\n\r\n              Henry opened the scoring, powering through safety Kevin Winston Jr. for a 5-yard touchdown run on fourth-and-1. That drive was set up when Tennessee's Cam Ward tried to flip a desperation pass to Tony Pollard on the second play from scrimmage, and the ball went through Pollard's arms and was picked off by Trenton Simpson.\n\n\r\n              The Titans, however, were more functional offensively than they'd been in previous first halves. They drove 70 yards for a tying touchdown, a 3-yard run by Pollard.\n\n\r\n              Jackson's 19-yard scoring pass to Flowers put Baltimore back in front, and then the Titans were stopped on fourth-and-goal from the 2 when they tried a direct snap to Pollard.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens drove 98 yards and went up 21-7 on Jackson's 3-yard TD pass to Mark Andrews.\n\n\r\n              Tyler Loop, fresh off his game-winning 56-yard kick against Dallas the previous weekend, connected from a career-long 64 yards on the last play of the half, but Jackson was hurt on the snap before.\n\n\r\n              “Offense I thought came out and played with a lot of juice,” Titans coach Robert Saleh said. “Obviously, we had the two turnovers that resulted into 10 points, and then the turnover on downs inside the 5. Was a goofy game in that it didn’t feel like 24-7 at halftime.”\n\n\r\n              Ward threw a 3-yard scoring pass to Wan'Dale Robinson with 1:12 to go in the fourth quarter, and a 2-point conversion pass to Gunnar Helm made it a six-point game, but Malaki Starks snatched the onside kick for Baltimore to seal the game.\n\n\r\n              Henry finished with 73 yards on 23 carries.\n\n\r\n              The Titans committed the game's only two turnovers, but the Ravens were sloppy in other ways, with 13 penalties for 131 yards.\n\n\r\n              Receiver battle\r\n              Tennessee rookie Carnell Tate had nine catches for 145 yards, but he lost a fumble at the end of a 33-yard reception in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Flowers did almost all of his damage in the first half, contributing catches of 52 and 23 yards in addition to his touchdown.\n\n\r\n              “Wide receiver one, man. He’s so good. He’s so versatile, and each and every game is so explosive,” Andrews said. “It’s really impressive to see, honestly, just how good he is.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Baltimore was losing players all game. CB Marlon Humphrey (calf) went down in the first half. G Andrew Vorhees (foot) and TE Durham Smythe (Achilles tendon) were also injured. The Ravens did get T Ronnie Stanley (toe) back after he missed Week 3.\n\n\r\n              Tennessee S Amani Hooker was evaluated for a concussion in the fourth quarter and cleared to return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Titans: Host Houston next Sunday in a matchup of winless AFC South teams.\n\n\r\n              Ravens: At Atlanta next Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1004/25e61c06815d42a7b6b69bce6a5f60791008/25e61c06815d42a7b6b69bce6a5f60791008.jpg"},"401872967":{"h":"CeeDee Lamb’s late TD caps a wild Cowboys rally to beat winless Texans 34-30","d":"— CeeDee Lamb shined in his first NFL game in the city he considers his second home to carry the Dallas Cowboys to a victory on Sunday.","b":"HOUSTON -- — CeeDee Lamb shined in his first NFL game in the city he considers his second home to carry the Dallas Cowboys to a victory on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              Lamb had a career-high 17 catches for 189 yards and his 12-yard TD reception with 40 seconds left lifted Dallas to the 34-30 win over Houston to keep the Texans winless.\n\n\r\n              “I knew this one was a big one for him,” coach Brian Schottenheimer said. “I know how much Houston means to him and his family means to him. And he put on a show.”\n\n\r\n              Lamb was born in Louisiana and lived in New Orleans until relocating to the Houston area after the city was destroyed by Hurricane Katrina in 2005. \n\n\r\n              “My grandma came out here, which was really good to see,” Lamb said. \"She doesn’t really come to many of my games and do all the hoorah and screaming. It was good to see my family here. It’s just been a long road to end up in this situation and I’m grateful for it.”\n\n\r\n              Javonte Williams ran for a career-high three touchdowns — all in the second half — to help the Cowboys to the victory.\n\n\r\n              Dallas (2-2) trailed by 4 after a 6-yard touchdown run by Woody Marks with about 2 ½ minutes to play. The Texans were facing third-and-14 when a hook-and-ladder play with a 2-yard catch by Dalton Schultz and a 19-yard run by David Montgomery set up that score.\n\n\r\n              Dak Prescott then orchestrated an 11-play, 74-yard drive that included a fourth down conversion and capped it with the throw to Lamb to give the Cowboys the victory. \n\n\r\n              “As soon as it was called,” Prescott said, \"I knew it was a touchdown.”\n\n\r\n              The Texans (0-4) had a last chance after that, but C.J. Stroud’s desperation pass fell incomplete.\n\n\r\n              “Our guys moved the ball really well,” coach DeMeco Ryans said. “We just did not play well enough defensively and it’s really a bad feeling.” \n\n\r\n              Prescott threw for 335 yards and Stroud had 347 yards passing. \n\n\r\n              Nico Collins had seven receptions for 118 yards and two touchdowns in his return after missing two games with a hamstring injury. But his big day wasn't enough to get the Texans in the win column. \n\n\r\n              “It's tough,” Collins said. “We’re 0-4 right now. You've got to move forward. Go to the next week, man. You can’t look back on this.”\n\n\r\n              The Cowboys took their first lead on the third score by Williams, a 2-yard run that made it 27-23 with about 11 minutes to go.\n\n\r\n              Dallas cut the lead to 16-13 on an 8-yard run by Williams on the opening drive of the third quarter. A 30-yard reception by Lamb set up the score.\n\n\r\n              The Texans had great field position on their next drive thanks to a 53-yard kickoff return by Jaylin Noel. The second score by Collins made it 23-13 with about 11 minutes left in the third.\n\n\r\n              George Pickens grabbed a 53-yard reception over Kamari Lassiter to get the Cowboys to the 11-yard line after that. Lassiter was flagged for pass interference on Pickens on third down to give Dallas a first down at the 1.\n\n\r\n              Jadeveon Clowney stuffed Williams for a loss of 3 on first down and the Cowboys couldn’t move the ball after that to bring up fourth down. Dallas went for it and a holding penalty by Lassiter gave the Cowboys another first down at the 1.\n\n\r\n              Williams plunged in for the touchdown on the next play to cut the lead to 23-20 with seven minutes left in the third.\n\n\r\n              Collins had a 45-yard catch-and-run on Houston’s first play and made it 7-0 when he capped the drive with a 7-yard TD reception.\n\n\r\n              Houston led by 4 when Will Anderson blocked Brandon Aubrey’s 62-yard field-goal attempt about 4 ½ minutes before halftime.\n\n\r\n              Kaʻimi Fairbairn’s 39-yard field goal on Houston’s next drive extended the lead to 13-6.\n\n\r\n              Aubrey’s 65-yard attempt with about 30 seconds left in the second quarter sailed wide left.\n\n\r\n              A 54-yard field goal by Fairbairn made it 16-6 at halftime.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Houston: CB Jaylin Smith left in the second quarter with a hamstring injury. … DT Jaden Crumedy injured his shoulder in the third quarter. \n\n\r\n              Anniversary celebration\r\n              The Texans, who are in their 25th season, had an anniversary celebration at halftime. Texans greats including Hall of Famer Andre Johnson, Arian Foster, DeAndre Hopkins, Brian Cushing and Billy Miller along with former coach Gary Kubiak were honored in the ceremony. J.J. Watt did not attend but recorded a message that was played on the video boards.\n\n\r\n              Porter’s debut\r\n              Cornerback Joey Porter Jr. played extensively in his debut with the Cowboys after being traded from the Steelers on Wednesday. It was his first action of the season after he was inactive for the first three games while upset about his contract situation and dealing with a back issue.\n\n\r\n              “Man that’s the main thing I was missing was to play football,” he said. “So the fact that I got out there, got the dub with the guys, man it’s a blessing.”\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Cowboys: Host Tampa Bay Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              Texans: Visit Tennessee next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1004/5350583c-499d-4660-aa9f-3f1316e1dada/5350583c-499d-4660-aa9f-3f1316e1dada.jpg"},"401872974":{"h":"Unbeaten Vikings keep Dolphins winless with dominant defense, Reichard's kicks, Hockenson's catches","d":"— Kyler Murray and the Minnesota Vikings ' offense would've appreciated a touchdown for their progress down the stretch, but there was only one way to truly secure this victory.","b":"MINNEAPOLIS -- — Kyler Murray and the Minnesota Vikings ' offense would've appreciated a touchdown for their progress down the stretch, but there was only one way to truly secure this victory. \n\n\r\n              T.J. Hockenson fittingly took the winning slide. \n\n\r\n              The Vikings stayed undefeated with more dominance by their defense and five field goals from Will Reichard, sidestepping their early passing game problems to beat the winless Miami Dolphins 15-10 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Hockenson tied a career high with 13 receptions for 119 yards on 13 targets from Murray, including the game-sealing 8-yard catch on third-and-5 from the 10. The eighth-year tight end was wide open and could've coasted into the end zone, but he wisely slid in front of the goal line to drain the clock to the two-minute warning.\n\n\r\n              “Once you get the first down, you’ve got to get down,” said Hockenson, who had his highest yardage total since Week 10 of the 2023 season. “It was tough. I saw that goal line and I wanted it, but it's for the betterment of the team that we ended on offense.”\n\n\r\n              With the Dolphins out of timeouts, the Vikings had Murray take three straight kneel-downs to move to 4-0 for the second time in three years — despite going without a touchdown in two games.\n\n\r\n              “If we want to score, we should do it earlier,” Vikings coach Kevin O'Connell said.\n\n\r\n              Murray finished 27 for 37 for 247 yards without a turnover, but he took four sacks for 34 yards against a Dolphins defense that had none in the first three games and was often tentative and unsteady in the pocket until Hockenson helped him open up the field with a handful of clutch and difficult catches.\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins, who are 0-4 for the first time since starting 0-7 in 2019 in their first season under then-coach Brian Flores, were crushed by a combination of unforced mistakes — including nine penalties for 55 yards.\n\n\r\n              New Dolphins quarterback Malik Willis went just 9 for 17 for 85 yards, losing a fumble during one of the two sacks by Dallas Turner. The four teams the Dolphins have faced in coach Jeff Hafley's first year are 15-1.\n\n\r\n              “You hate the feeling of continuing to get better without actually gaining wins on the win column, but as we see, we’re going out there and competing,” Willis said.\n\n\r\n              Willis thought he had escaped the relentless Vikings pass rush to float a 68-yard touchdown pass to Chris Bell early in the second quarter, but Bell was flagged for illegal touching after stepping out of bounds as he moved to get open.\n\n\r\n              The Vikings had 11 penalties for 85 yards, but they dodged one when Ivan Pace Jr. had a call for roughing the kicker waved off by the video replay assist system's determination that he tipped Bradley Pinion's punt and therefore didn't commit a penalty.\n\n\r\n              After netting just 76 yards on 21 plays in the first half and failing to finish any possession across midfield, the Dolphins finally found a couple of creases in Minnesota’s suffocating defense directed by Flores.\n\n\r\n              Ollie Gordon, in an elevated role following the season-ending knee injury to star running back De’Vone Achane, ripped off a career-best 53-yard run on third-and-5 to set up Riley Patterson’s 27-yard field goal. Gordon had a 12-yard touchdown run on the next drive to pull the Dolphins within 12-10.\n\n\r\n              Vikings offense builds some momentum\r\n              Reichard, who stretched the NFL 's longest active streak to 31 consecutive made field goals, drew a loud roar when he was shown on the videoboard during the two-minute warning. Murray, on the other hand, heard some mild rounds of boos as the unproductive possessions began to pile up.\n\n\r\n              But the Vikings had the ball for 14 of 15 minutes in the fourth quarter, accumulating eight first downs.\n\n\r\n              Aaron Jones also carried another heavy load with 20 carries for 94 yards and four receptions for 34 yards, with the Vikings missing star wide receiver Justin Jefferson (ankle) for the first time since Week 12 of the 2023 season and running back Jordan Mason (thumb) for the third game in a row.\n\n\r\n              “That door will get kicked down soon,” Murray said. \n\n\r\n              Injury report\r\n              The Vikings played most of the second half without two of their best blockers. Left guard Donovan Jackson (knee) was sidelined in the first quarter and left tackle Christian Darrisaw (neck) was hurt in the second after going head-first into the pile at the end of a run. Darrisaw returned after halftime, only to be pulled again as a precaution for a concussion evaluation following a second hit. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Dolphins: Host Cincinnati next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Vikings: Play at New Orleans next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1004/dm_261004_Vikings_beat_Dolphins_IA/dm_261004_Vikings_beat_Dolphins_IA.jpg"},"401872976":{"h":"Chiefs beat the Raiders 30-27 to move to 4-0 behind Patrick Mahomes and Kenneth Walker III","d":"— Patrick Mahomes said he loves playing in Allegiant Stadium, and it's no wonder, given his perfect record in one of the NFL's newer venues.","b":"LAS VEGAS -- — Patrick Mahomes said he loves playing in Allegiant Stadium, and it's no wonder, given his perfect record in one of the NFL's newer venues.\n\n\r\n              He's rarely been pushed as much as he was Sunday against a Raiders team out to show its quick start wasn't a fluke.\n\n\r\n              As he has so often, Mahomes came through, passing for 225 yards and two touchdowns. He got a big assist from Kenneth Walker III, who rushed for 177 yards and two TDs to lead the Kansas City Chiefs to a 30-27 victory over Las Vegas.\n\n\r\n              Walker's 2-yard touchdown run with 3:21 left put the Chiefs (4-0) ahead for good at 23-19. It was set up by Tyquan Thornton's 55-yard reception on the previous play. Thornton, who had five catches for 111 yards and two touchdowns, suffered a gruesome-looking left ankle injury on the play and was carted off.\n\n\r\n              “I think you have to stay with it,” Mahomes said, describing the back-and-forth game. “I keep saying it, but it’s not always going to be pretty. That’s a good football team, a good defense. It’s not going to always be light it up and go out there and scoring 50 points or whatever. It’s if you stay with the process, it’s going to come.”\n\n\r\n              Kirk Cousins passed for 365 yards and two touchdowns for the Raiders, ending his seven-game winning streak that included the final four games last season with Atlanta. Brock Bowers, who played with an illness, caught six passes for 86 yards and a TD.\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas' running game produced just 72 yards, averaging 2.8 per carry.\n\n\r\n              “We've got to find that answer,” Raiders coach Klint Kubiak said of the run game struggles. “That will be a big factor in us being a better football team.”\n\n\r\n              One of the NFL's early-season surprises, the Raiders put up a strong fight against their AFC West rivals, and Kubiak expressed confidence in his team at halftime.\n\n\r\n              “We're winning,” Kubiak said on the CBS broadcast. “We're going to win the game.”\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas certainly gave it a shot, taking a 13-10 advantage into the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              The lead didn't last long. Walker ended the third by busting free for a 76-yard run before he was chased down from behind by Raiders cornerback Taron Johnson at the 8-yard line. Walker ran 21.21 mph on that play, according to the NFL's Next Gen Stats.\n\n\r\n              “(Johnson) probably was going 23,” Walker said. “I thought I was out of there until I started hearing breathing.”\n\n\r\n              Two plays later, the Chiefs took their first lead on Mahomes' 8-yard scoring pass to Thornton.\n\n\r\n              It was the first time the Raiders' defense had been scored on in the fourth quarter this season.\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas came right back, with Matt Gay making field goals of 44 and 48 yards for a 19-17 lead with 4:53 left.\n\n\r\n              But it's hard to count out Mahomes in Las Vegas — or most places.\n\n\r\n              He led the go-ahead touchdown drive and then took advantage of a fumble on the ensuing kickoff by handing the ball to Walker for another TD. Mahomes has won all six of his starts at Allegiant Stadium against the Raiders, plus the Super Bowl after the 2023 season.\n\n\r\n              “I trust him to do that,” Chiefs coach Andy Reid said. “I don’t expect anything, but I do trust that he’s going to do the right thing when the ball’s in his hand.”\n\n\r\n              Reid, amazingly, has the most wins of any coach at Allegiant with seven. He broke a tie with former Raiders coaches Josh McDaniels and Antonio Pierce. Reid is 7-1 at the stadium, which opened in 2020.\n\n\r\n              Backed by plenty of their red-clad fans, the Chiefs held off a frantic comeback attempt in the final minutes. Cousins hit Bowers for a 20-yard touchdown with 1:46 left and the Raiders got the ball back, moving to midfield before Cousins was intercepted.\n\n\r\n              Chiefs left guard benched\r\n              Reid said left guard Kingsley Suamataia didn't start for disciplinary reasons. Mike Caliendo took his place.\n\n\r\n              “That's between Kingsley and I,” Reid said. “We'll figure it out, and down the road, we'll be better as a team for it and Kingsley will be better for it.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Chiefs: WR Rashee Rice (hamstring) pulled up limping in the first quarter and didn't return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Chiefs: Are off next weekend before hosting the Los Angeles Chargers on Oct. 18.\n\n\r\n              Raiders: Visit New England next Sunday for their third road game in four weeks.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1004/2ef0269972a1425588d181b804e7520f1278/2ef0269972a1425588d181b804e7520f1278.jpg"},"401872975":{"h":"Brock Purdy throws 2 TD passes to lead the undefeated 49ers past the Broncos 24-14","d":"— Once the San Francisco 49ers got going on offense, Brock Purdy and crew once again couldn't be stopped.","b":"SANTA CLARA, Calif. -- — Once the San Francisco 49ers got going on offense, Brock Purdy and crew once again couldn't be stopped.\n\n\r\n              Brock Purdy threw two touchdown passes and Christian McCaffrey ran for a score as the 49ers scored touchdowns on three straight second-half possessions to beat the Denver Broncos 24-14 on Sunday to remain undefeated.\n\n\r\n              Both offenses struggled to generate offense on a hot day in the Bay Area, but the 49ers (4-0) took advantage of a fumbled punt return by Marvin Mims Jr. to set up a short touchdown drive in the third quarter that gave them the lead for good.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco had been held to three points on seven possessions before the takeaway that set the stage for three straight touchdowns as the Niners took advantage of injuries that knocked out both of Denver's starting cornerbacks, including two-time All Pro Patrick Surtain II.\n\n\r\n              “We wanted to break through whatever that was and just be ourselves,” Purdy said. “I'm glad we could get some momentum going in the second half. That's why it's a four-quarter game.”\n\n\r\n              Purdy capped the 28-yard drive with a 1-yard touchdown pass to Deebo Samuel that made it 10-3 late in the third quarter. He then engineered a 74-yard drive that McCaffrey finished with a 1-yard run to extend the lead to 17-6 midway through the fourth after the Broncos (2-2) had settled for a field goal on fourth-and-2.\n\n\r\n              Bo Nix, who has thrived in late-game comeback situations, tried to mount another for Denver with his 13-yard touchdown pass to Jaylen Waddle cutting the Niners' lead to 17-14 with a 2-point conversion with 4:05 to play.\n\n\r\n              But the 49ers sealed the win when Kittle took a screen pass behind the line of scrimmage and outraced former teammate Talanoa Hufanga for a 56-yard touchdown with 2:15 to go. \n\n\r\n              “Any time you see green grass, I think I ran the entire time with a smile on my face,” said Kittle, who'll turn 33 on Friday. “Always outrunning younger, old teammates is always a good time, especially when they’re (27).”\n\n\r\n              Purdy finished 19 of 30 for 218 yards and avoided taking a sack for a fourth straight game and had no turnovers.\n\n\r\n              Nix struggled to push the ball downfield most of the game with Denver's running game unable to loosen up the defense. He finished 25 of 42 for 214 yards and one touchdown.\n\n\r\n              “We just didn’t have enough offense to get a lead and then go beyond that,” coach Sean Payton said. “We just didn’t have enough answers to get past that and get a lead.”\n\n\r\n              The 49ers came in scoring touchdowns on nearly half of their possessions, but struggled to move the ball against Denver's stout defense. They punted on two of their first three possessions with Purdy getting stopped on a fourth-down sneak on the other.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco got on the board with a 24-yard field goal to tie it late in the first half, but had a chance to take the lead thwarted when a field goal was blocked on the final play of the first half to keep the game tied at 3.\n\n\r\n              “I think we had plays to be made,\" Nix said. “I think as a team there were plays out there for us that needed to be made.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Broncos: Surtain (ankle), CB Riley Moss (ribs) and WR Pat Bryant (ankle) all left with injuries and didn't return. Payton had no immediate word on the extent of the injuries.\n\n\r\n              49ers: FB Kyle Juszczyk left with a sprained knee injury after being hit by Jahdae Barron and didn't return. ... S Marques Sigle (ankle) and CB Upton Stout (shoulder) also left. Shanahan had no immediate word on the extent of the injuries.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Broncos: Visit the Los Angeles Chargers next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              49ers: Visit Seattle next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1004/3290a2c0-438f-4ebe-b174-bfc13b15a7be/3290a2c0-438f-4ebe-b174-bfc13b15a7be.jpg"},"401872977":{"h":"Emanuel Wilson sparks Seahawks past Chargers 30-23, handing Los Angeles a fourth straight loss","d":"— The Seattle Seahawks got their rebound win, but it was far from perfect.","b":"SEATTLE -- — The Seattle Seahawks got their rebound win, but it was far from perfect. \n\n\r\n              Emanuel Wilson caught a touchdown pass and ran for another score as the Seahawks handed the Los Angeles Chargers a fourth straight loss to start the season with a 30-23 victory on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              Sam Darnold threw for 168 yards and two touchdowns for the defending Super Bowl champions, who are 3-1 after bouncing back from last week's 33-31 loss to the Washington Commanders.\n\n\r\n              But Darnold was also intercepted once and sacked three times. The Seahawks had 14 penalties for a franchise-record 150 yards and they let the Chargers get back in it after leading 27-6 in the third quarter. \n\n\r\n              “We're really grateful for where we're at right now and excited to move forward,\" Seahawks coach Mike Macdonald said. \"But we also understand that we have definitely a lot of room for improvement with our operation, how we take care of the football and obviously the penalties are something we need to address.”\n\n\r\n              Wilson rushed for 81 yards on 21 carries and caught three passes for 39 yards. The backup running back, a four-year pro in his first season with the Seahawks, was thrust into a greater role when rookie Jadarian Price was placed on injured reserve on Saturday after aggravating a chest injury during practice. \n\n\r\n              Justin Herbert threw for 189 yards a touchdown and two interceptions for the Chargers (0-4). He has been intercepted six times this season and sacked 12 times, including four for 35 yards on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              The only team to reach the playoffs after a four-loss start is the 1992 San Diego Chargers. That squad started 0-4, then beat Seattle 17-6 on Oct. 4 and went on to finish 11-5 and win AFC West.\n\n\r\n              Chargers coach Jim Harbaugh said he has hope. \n\n\r\n              “What I saw from our players, that will, that never say die attitude that they have,” Harbaugh said. “I mean, it's throughout the team.”\n\n\r\n              The game was chaotic at the start with both teams giving up early turnovers. Seattle's Drake Thomas intercepted Herbert's attempted pass to Oronde Gadsden on the Chargers' opening play. Then on Seattle's first play from scrimmage, Darnold's pass intended for Wilson was intercepted by Tuli Tuipulotu, giving the ball back to the Chargers. \n\n\r\n              Los Angeles' drive ended with Cameron Dicker's 40-yard field goal. According to Sportradar, it was the first game since at least 1991 when both teams threw interceptions on their opening play from scrimmage. \n\n\r\n              Derius Davis muffed a punt return and it was recovered by Seattle's Connor O'Toole. On the next play, Darnold hit Wilson with a 23-yard touchdown pass to put Seattle up 7-3. \n\n\r\n              “Man, it was just like Moses parting the red sea,” Wilson said. “Everybody got in the blocks.” \n\n\r\n              Rashid Shaheed's 28-yard scoring run made it 13-3 midway through the second quarter. Jason Myers' extra point attempt went wide right, breaking a streak of 80 consecutive converted PAT dating to 2024. \n\n\r\n              Ernest Jones intercepted Herbert again on a pass intended for Ladd McConkey and returned it 20 yards. Wilson capped Seattle's next series with an 8-yard touchdown run. \n\n\r\n              The Chargers closed the gap with Dicker's 39-yard field goal before halftime that made it 20-6. Los Angeles finished the half with just 92 yards of total offense. \n\n\r\n              The Seahawks opened the second half with Darnold's 6-yard TD pass to Tory Horton, extending the lead to 27-6. The Chargers have allowed 24 or more points in each of their first four games. \n\n\r\n              The Chargers showed life with Herbert's 9-yard touchdown pass to Kimani Vidal. After a field goal by Seattle, Omarion Hampton ran for a 2-yard touchdown and Dicker added a 36-yarder to pull the Chargers within 30-23 with 2:14 to go. \n\n\r\n              Los Angeles got the ball back with 1:42 to go, but Herbert was sacked and fumbled, and Seattle recovered to end it. \n\n\r\n              There were plenty of penalties for both teams\r\n              It wasn't just the Seahawks that got hit with penalties, the Chargers had 16 penalties for 130 yards. \n\n\r\n              Darnold said he expects those mistakes to diminish with time. \n\n\r\n              “Obviously you don't want the penalties, but as we continue to play these games and continue to get this season going, I feel like our guys on both sides of the ball, special teams, too, we'll continue to get better,\" he said. \n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Chargers: CB Donte Jackson left in the first quarter with a hamstring injury. ... T Joe Alt left in the second quarter with a neck injury and didn't return. ... WR Ladd McConkey left at halftime after aggravating a foot injury. \n\n\r\n              Seahawks: DT Leonard Williams limped to the medical tent in the third quarter with an ankle injury, but returned on the Chargers' final series. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Chargers: Return home to face Denver next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              Seahawks: Host San Francisco next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              ------ \n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1004/62346bd2-6c9d-4c71-a926-e8e5d091dd58/62346bd2-6c9d-4c71-a926-e8e5d091dd58.jpg"},"401872978":{"h":"Young, McMillan connect for 2 TDs to lead Panthers past Lions 32-26","d":"— Carolina Panthers coach Dave Canales was a little worried after Bryce Young and Tetairoa McMillan had some issues connecting on passes in practice.","b":"CHARLOTTE, N.C. -- — Carolina Panthers coach Dave Canales was a little worried after Bryce Young and Tetairoa McMillan had some issues connecting on passes in practice.\n\n\r\n              You'd never know it by the way they played Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              Young completed 29 of 41 passes for 329 yards and two touchdowns to McMillan, who had 14 catches for a career-high 192 yards, and the Panthers overcame an injury-filled week to beat the Detroit Lions 32-26.\n\n\r\n              “Everything just clicked tonight,” McMillan said. “There were some plays throughout the week that we may not have hit on, but (offensive coordinator) Brad Idzik called the plays and Bryce trusted it. He made good throws and we converted. I can't tell you what happened tonight. Maybe it was just the Charlotte air.”\n\n\r\n              Young, who was serenaded with “M-V-P! M-V-P!” chants by fans in the fourth quarter, said he never second-guessed throwing McMillan's way because he has so much confidence in him.\n\n\r\n              “I know what kind of player he is. He's special,” Young said. “I always trust him. I have confidence in him and I know one (bad) practice isn't going to change that.\"\n\n\r\n              Also, Young didn't have a lot of other experienced options.\n\n\r\n              With two of Carolina's top three wide receivers in Jalen Coker and Xavier Legette out with injuries, Young was forced to heavily on McMillan, and the 2025 Offensive Rookie of the Year.\n\n\r\n              And he responded in a big way early on for the Panthers (2-2).\n\n\r\n              McMillan had nine catches for 147 yards in the first half, including a 32-yard touchdown.\n\n\r\n              He joined Steve Smith Sr. and Patrick Jeffers as the only Carolina players with at least 10 receptions, 150 yards receiving and two touchdowns in a game. And he came close to breaking the franchise records for receptions (15) set by Christian McCaffrey and yards receiving (201) held by Smith.\n\n\r\n              Young entered Week 4 leading the league in yards passing and the No. 1 overall pick in 2023 opened the game by completing his first 11 passes.\n\n\r\n              Chuba Hubbard gave the running game a needed spark carrying 20 times for a season-high 122 yards and two touchdowns as Carolina scored on six straight possessions after punting on its opening drive.\n\n\r\n              Jared Goff threw for 412 yards and a touchdown to Sam LaPorta for Detroit (2-2) and Jahmyr Gibbs added 77 yards from scrimmage and scored his seventh touchdown of the year, but the Lions didn't look as explosive as they had been the first three games of the season.\n\n\r\n              Detroit was able to overcome McMillan's big first half and tie the game before the break.\n\n\r\n              Jameson Williams hauled in a 49-yard reception down the middle against an injury-depleted Carolina secondary that lost starting cornerback Jaycee Horn and Mike Jackson to eight-week-long injuries last week.\n\n\r\n              That set up Jake Bates' field goal to make it 16-all.\n\n\r\n              But Detroit's defense couldn't get off the field in the second half as Young repeatedly found an open McMillan.\n\n\r\n              Even after the Lions cut the lead to 32-26 with 3:38 left in the game on LaPorta's TD catch, McMillan responded with a toe-tapping reception along the right sideline for a first down that kept the clock moving.\n\n\r\n              Still, drive ended in a sack and the Panthers were forced to punt.\n\n\r\n              The Lions had a chance to win the game on their final drive, which started at their own 13 with 2:12 remaining. But Goff couldn't pick up a first down and Young knelt down twice to end it.\n\n\r\n              “We got ourselves in a hole there, which is frustrating,” Lions coach Dan Campbell said. “At the end of the day, we had our opportunities. Our defense came up with a stop and we had the ball with about with two minutes left to go win the game. And we just couldn’t get it done.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Lions: Cornerback Rock Ya-Sin left in the second half with a hamstring injury.\n\n\r\n              Panthers: Rookie right tackle Monroe Freeling, the team's first-round draft pick, injured a knee in the third quarter and did not return. Coker was inactive with a quadriceps injury. Linebacker Jaelan Phillips left in the fourth quarter with a hamstring injury. Canales had no update on either player after the game.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Lions: At Arizona next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Panthers: Will have a chance to heal up with a bye next week before visiting Philadelphia on Oct. 18. \n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1005/c041a6d6-504f-4441-a16a-b122c5e2ae86/c041a6d6-504f-4441-a16a-b122c5e2ae86.jpg"},"401872979":{"h":"Bijan Robinson powers Falcons past Saints 45-24 on the 20th anniversary of the Superdome's reopening","d":"— Bijan Robinson ensured there would be no punt to block this time — and the Atlanta Falcons were off and running.","b":"NEW ORLEANS -- — Bijan Robinson ensured there would be no punt to block this time — and the Atlanta Falcons were off and running. \n\n\r\n              Robinson rushed for 145 yards and two touchdowns — starting with a 59-yard scoring run on Atlanta's third play — and the Falcons routed the New Orleans Saints 45-24 on Monday night, dampening celebrations commemorating the 20th anniversary of the Superdome's reopening after Hurricane Katrina.\n\n\r\n              Robinson said the Falcons wanting to silence the crowd early “and make sure that pin drop happens in the first quarter.”\n\n\r\n              “For it to come after that third play was pretty special,” Robinson said. “We just went back to the sideline and said, ‘That’s what we talked about — make sure that we put it on them early so the crowd doesn’t become an issue.'”\n\n\r\n              Brian Robinson Jr. ran for three short touchdowns for the Falcons (2-2), who never trailed and controlled the line of scrimmage while outgaining the Saints (1-3) on the ground, 205 yards to 50.\n\n\r\n              Atlanta quarterback Michael Penix Jr., who returned from a major 2025 knee injury last week for a 35-14 victory at Green Bay, was an efficient 15 of 20 for 223 yards and a touchdown. His 31-yard scoring strike to Jahan Dotson in fourth quarter sent Saints fans toward the exits.\n\n\r\n              “Mike played well; he took care of the football and made some really good throws,” Falcons coach Kevin Stefanski said. “I really like his demeanor and how he is operating.” \n\n\r\n              New Orleans fans booed at times and Saints QB Tyler Shough didn't blame them, calling his team's performance “embarrassing.”\n\n\r\n              “Fans understandably are upset,\" said Shough, who passed for 286 yards and a late touchdown to Devaughn Vele. ”You’re playing the Falcons and you lose like that? I’d boo, too.”\n\n\r\n              Saints running back Alvin Kamara scored his first two touchdowns of the season, the second cutting Atlanta's lead to 31-17 early in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              “We're all frustrated, all disappointed with where we’re at,” Saints second-year coach Kellen Moore said.\n\n\r\n              “This was a special night,\" said Moore, who had gathered the team to watch videos about Hurricane Katrina in 2005 and the Superdome's reopening on a Monday night against the Falcons in Week 3 of the 2006 season.\n\n\r\n              “We understood the opportunity and I thought there was great energy,” but the Falcons \"were able to get things going early and we weren’t able to catch up,” Moore said.\n\n\r\n              Ignited by Steve Gleason’s punt block for a touchdown on the 2006 game’s opening possession, the Saints beat the Falcons 23-3.\n\n\r\n              Like that meeting two decades ago, the Falcons, wearing red, received the opening kickoff. The similarities ended soon after.\n\n\r\n              For a fourth time in four games, the Saints were playing from behind early — a challenge made more difficult by Daniel Carlson’s missed 52-yard field goal attempt and running back Kendre Miller’s lost fumble on the New Orleans 22-yard line one possession later.\n\n\r\n              Atlanta cashed in on the turnover when Bijan Robinson made it 14-0 on a 4-yard power run, set up in part by cornerback Kool-Aid McKinstry’s third-down defensive holding penalty.\n\n\r\n              Brian Robinson Jr. summed up the Falcons' past two games, during which they've scored 80 points, as “the most fun I’ve had in my career.”\n\n\r\n              “We can be as good as we want to be,” he said. \n\n\r\n              Pregame ceremonies feature 2006 Saints players\r\n              Wearing white jerseys, retired players Drew Brees, Scott Fujita, Scott Shanle, Deuce McAllister, Devery Henderson and Curtis Deloatch joined Gleason on the field before kickoff to lead fans in the club’s familiar “Who dat!” chant. Gleason, using a wheelchair because of ALS, was joined by his son, Rivers, who raised his father's arm and then pulled it down to signal the start of the chant.\n\n\r\n              NFL Commissioner Roger Goodell attended, spending pregame warmups on the home sideline conversing with Saints owner Gayle Benson and other dignitaries.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Falcons: Defensive end Za’Darius Smith received several minutes of attention on the field, but was able walk to the sideline after a stretcher had been brought out for him. He was placed in the concussion protocol. Left tackle Jake Matthews (groin) left the game in the first quarter.\n\n\r\n              Saints: The Saints began without two key defensive starters: Ed Carl Granderson (ankle) and linebacker Kaden Ellis (calf), who were ruled out on Saturday. Tight end Noah Fant (abdomen) was a game-night scratch.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Falcons: Host Baltimore on Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              Saints: Host Minnesota on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1006/ss_20261006_001718093_3686691/ss_20261006_001718093_3686691.jpg"},"401872980":{"h":"Bucky Irving and rookie QB Jalon Daniels help Bucs beat sloppy Cowboys 24-16 for 1st win of season","d":"— Bucky Irving already had his first 100-yard game since his rookie year in 2024 when the Tampa Bay running back finished off the Dallas Cowboys with a run that gave him a career high.","b":"ARLINGTON, Texas -- — Bucky Irving already had his first 100-yard game since his rookie year in 2024 when the Tampa Bay running back finished off the Dallas Cowboys with a run that gave him a career high.\n\n\r\n              Jalon Daniels, the undrafted rookie filling in for franchise quarterback Baker Mayfield, enjoyed the view while doing plenty himself to get the Buccaneers their first win of the season in just his second start. \n\n\r\n              Irving ran for 165 yards, including a 72-yard run to set up the touchdown that put Tampa Bay ahead for good while scoring twice himself, and the Buccaneers beat the Cowboys 24-16 on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              Daniels threw a TD pass to Irving in the first half while directing a turnover-free offense and amid several Dallas miscues, finishing an efficient 19 of 25 for 189 yards as the Bucs piled up 241 yards rushing.\n\n\r\n              Daniels is filling in for Baker Mayfield, who's expected to be sidelined at least one more game with a dislocated thumb on his throwing hand.\n\n\r\n              “Six months ago, if you would have told me that my first win would have been on a Thursday night, (Amazon) Prime football game, I don’t know what I would have told you,” said Daniels, who threw three interceptions in a 17-14 loss to Green Bay in his debut. “Like I keep saying, through all the highs and lows, the bads, the goods, go through every single emotion because at the end of the day, it makes you better and prepares you for any situation that comes down the line.”\n\n\r\n              Irving had 21 carries and a 7.9-yard average while scoring on a 5-yard pass and a 1-yard plunge as the Bucs (1-4) finally found some footing in a season that started with expectations of winning the NFC South.\n\n\r\n              “We lost the first quarter of the season,” Bucs coach Todd Bowles said. “We didn’t lose the second, third or fourth. We’ve still got to play those. So we started the second quarter tonight. It’s a long way to climb out, but at the same time, we’re upbeat and if we played as hard and played as effective, we’ll be OK.”\n\n\r\n              Dak Prescott led a 99-yard drive and threw a 13-yard TD pass to George Pickens in the fourth quarter before his second interception off a tipped pass helped Tampa Bay clinch the win in the final two minutes.\n\n\r\n              The Cowboys (2-3) continued an up-and-down start to the season, this time with an offense that was as times as sloppy as the defense has been in both seasons under coach Brian Schottenheimer.\n\n\r\n              “I didn’t plan on being 2-3, disappointed by that for sure,” Schottenheimer said. “But we’re just getting started. There’s a lot of football left and we’re gonna play better. We know that.”\n\n\r\n              After Irving's long run on the first play of the second half was followed by receiver Emeka Egbuka's 14-yard TD run on a jet sweep, Prescott threw his first interception and the Dallas defense left Tez Johnson wide open on a 64-yard grab that led to Irving's 1-yard plunge for a 21-10 lead.\n\n\r\n              KaVontae Turpin's fumble on the subsequent kickoff led to a Tampa Bay field goal, but only after the Bucs converted a third-and-21 from the Dallas 48-yard line with Daniels' 21-yard pass to Egbuka. \n\n\r\n              The Cowboys were missing one of the pieces they recently added to try to fix a porous defense. Cornerback Joey Porter Jr. was a late scratch in what was supposed to be his second appearance since arriving in a trade from Pittsburgh last week.\n\n\r\n              Prescott was 24 of 42 for 316 yards in his first two-interception game of the season — his first picks since the opener.\n\n\r\n              Bucs have long and short TD drives\r\n              Irving's 5-yard TD catch capped a 15-play, 73-yard drive that ran the final 10:44 off the clock in the first quarter and had a final count of 10:58. Then his 72-yard run was the first of two plays on an 86-yard drive that took exactly 1 minute.\n\n\r\n              Feast, then famine for Lamb\r\n              Dallas receiver CeeDee Lamb followed his career high of 17 catches, when he had 189 yards receiving and a touchdown last weekend against Houston, with just two catches for 9 yards on five targets. He was targeted 21 times against the Texans. Lamb exited the game with a quadriceps injury.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Buccaneers: LT Tristan Wirfs was sidelined by cramps in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Cowboys: LT Tyler Guyton left with a back issue in the second half. ... DB Alijah Clark exited with a hamstring injury in the first half and didn't return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Buccaneers: Home against Pittsburgh on Oct. 18.\n\n\r\n              Cowboys: At Green Bay on Oct. 18.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1008/c45625c728be428db843f1cffd134b131391/c45625c728be428db843f1cffd134b131391.jpg"},"401872981":{"h":"Cam Little's 57-yard FG gives Jaguars 19-17 win over Eagles in wild finish in London","d":"— Cam Little grew up playing soccer and watching Premier League games every weekend.","b":"LONDON -- — Cam Little grew up playing soccer and watching Premier League games every weekend.\n\n\r\n              So to line up a game-winning kick for the Jaguars in London was something special.\n\n\r\n              Little connected from 57 yards as time expired and Jacksonville beat the Philadelphia Eagles 19-17 in a wild finish at Tottenham Hotspur Stadium on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “It was the coolest moment of my career,” said Little, who wore a Manchester United jersey at his postgame news conference.\n\n\r\n              Little sprinted from midfield into the opposite end zone with his teammates in pursuit and struck a Jude Bellingham-style arms wide celebration in tribute to the England and Real Madrid star.\n\n\r\n              The improbable scenario was made possible by Ameer Abdullah’s 53-yard kickoff return that left 1 second on the clock.\n\n\r\n              The Eagles had taken a 17-16 lead on Jake Elliott's 48-yard field goal with 8 seconds left — after Little's extra-point attempt was wide left following Parker Washington's 27-yard touchdown reception late in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              The Jaguars were using backup snapper DaVon Hamilton after Ross Matiscik left the game with an injury.\n\n\r\n              Philadelphia, which slipped to 2/3 for the first time since 2021, played without leading rusher Saquon Barkley and receiver DeVonta Smith and with a makeshift offensive line. The Eagles rushed for just 22 yards.\n\n\r\n              Trevor Lawrence completed 24 of 39 passes for 331 yards and two touchdowns — both to Washington — along with an interception for the Jaguars (4-1).\n\n\r\n              “One of the craziest games, maybe the craziest game I’ve ever been a part of,” Lawrence said, “and I’ve been in some pretty wild ones. Just how cool is this sport? The game’s never over.”\n\n\r\n              Washington, who caught a 51-yard touchdown pass in the first quarter, had six receptions for 124 yards.\n\n\r\n              Eagles linebacker Zack Baun ensured his team went into halftime with a 14-7 lead by getting past Chris Rodriguez Jr. and tackling Bhayshul Tuten for a loss when Jacksonville went for it on fourth-and-goal from the 1 with 1 second left in the half. Eagles coach Nick Sirianni jumped and pumped his first in reaction.\n\n\r\n              Sirianni was asked about not kicking the ball out of bounds at the end.\n\n\r\n              “I didn’t make the right decision, and I lost us that game,” the Eagles coach said. “That’s what I said to our guys, and what hurts there is that, you know, the guys fought so hard and then I don’t make the decision that, you know, that I should make at the end of the game, and I cost us that game, and I put that fully on me.”\n\n\r\n              The Baltimore Ravens had used some late-game heroics in Rio de Janeiro last month to beat the Dallas Cowboys, who took heat for coaching decisions.\n\n\r\n              Earlier, Eagles backup running back Will Shipley scored on a 2-yard run and Darius Cooper caught a 62-yard touchdown pass from Jalen Hurts.\n\n\r\n              Hurts completed 21 of 40 passes for 220 yards and one touchdown. Jalen Carter had two sacks and two forced fumbles for the Eagles.\n\n\r\n              Little's 34-yard field goal in the third quarter made it 14-10.\n\n\r\n              Wild start\r\n              Jaguars rookie Josh Cameron returned the opening kickoff 90 yards for a would-be touchdown, but it was called back because of offsetting penalties including a hold by Jacksonville’s Branson Combs.\n\n\r\n              Meanwhile, there was a scrum at midfield, with pushing and shoving on both sides. Two players were ejected: Jacksonville’s Bryan Thomas Jr. and Philadelphia’s Jeremiah Trotter Jr.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Eagles: Edge rusher Jonathan Greenard (ankle) warmed up, but was ruled out ahead of the game... OLB Jalyx Hunt was injured on the last play of the first half and was helped off the field. ... DB Michael Carter was ruled out in the third quarter.... CB Quinyon Mitchell was slow to get up after intercepting a pass in the third quarter but returned... Shipley left the game late in second quarter with a foot injury.\n\n\r\n              Jaguars: LT Anton Harrison was carted off in the third quarter with what appeared to be a right knee injury... Long snapper Ross Matiscik left the game with an apparent shoulder injury. ... S Eric Murray (concussion) didn’t dress... DB Jaylon Jones (hamstring) had been ruled out Friday.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Eagles: Host the Carolina Panthers next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Jaguars: Stay in London and host the Houston Texans at Wembley Stadium next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1011/fcc326ec71d3485c8b5009ed83d591e5938/fcc326ec71d3485c8b5009ed83d591e5938.jpg"},"401872990":{"h":"Love-Watson connection helps Packers take some revenge on Bears with a 31-7 rout","d":"— Jordan Love and the Green Bay Packers wanted to make sure they didn’t let a big lead against the Chicago Bears slip away again.","b":"GREEN BAY, Wis. -- — Jordan Love and the Green Bay Packers wanted to make sure they didn’t let a big lead against the Chicago Bears slip away again.\n\n\r\n              With help from a ball-hawking defense, the Packers poured it on instead.\n\n\r\n              Christian Watson caught two long touchdown passes from Love, MarShawn Lloyd also scored twice and the Packers forced five turnovers as they exacted a measure of revenge against their rivals by trouncing the Bears 31-7 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “I love it,” said Love, whose 367 yards passing was the most by a Packers quarterback against the Bears at Lambeau Field. “We kept our foot on the gas. (Coach) Matt (LaFleur) was dialing it up. That's what it needs to look like, right there.”\n\n\r\n              The Packers (3-2) had blown double-digit, fourth-quarter leads while losing their last two matchups with the Bears, including a 31-27 NFC wild-card playoff loss in which they squandered a 21-3 halftime advantage. Bears quarterback Caleb Williams celebrated that come-from-behind victory by grating cheese on the field, poking fun at Green Bay’s Cheesehead fan base.\n\n\r\n              Without Williams, the Bears had no shot at staging a similar rally Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Williams missed a third straight game with a hamstring injury, and Tyson Bagent started in his place. The Bears (3-2) were coming off wins over the Philadelphia Eagles with Case Keenum at quarterback and the New York Jets with Bagent, but they never threatened Green Bay.\n\n\r\n              Xavier McKinney intercepted two passes from Bagent, who went 12 of 21 for 87 yards. Keenum took over early in the fourth quarter and went 11 of 19 for 108 yards with an interception and a fumble. \n\n\r\n              Bears coach Ben Johnson said after the game that Williams is nearing a return. \n\n\r\n              “He’s on track,” Johnson said. “We’ll see if it’s (next week against) Atlanta or after. I think it’s going to be close.”\n\n\r\n              Green Bay moved over .500 for the first time this season by repeatedly hitting long passing plays.\n\n\r\n              Watson said he was called crazy earlier in the week when he expressed optimism the Packers could exploit the Bears' pass defense. He caught six passes for 143 yards, including a 42-yard touchdown in the first quarter and a 53-yard score in the third.\n\n\r\n              Matthew Golden added a career-high 123 yards receiving while matching a career best with six catches.\n\n\r\n              “We watched the film,” Watson said. “We saw the opportunities there, regardless of how good they were in other games against certain teams. We knew what was going to be there and took advantage of it today.”\n\n\r\n              As Green Bay got a big lead, its defense kept attacking. Chicago turned the ball over on four straight second-half possessions.\n\n\r\n              Packers play keep-away to build early lead\r\n              Green Bay took command early by flipping the script on the Bears, who entered the day leading the NFL in average time of possession. The Packers, who had been ranked next-to-last in time of possession, outgained the Bears 130 yards to 9 in the first quarter while controlling the ball for 11 minutes, 45 seconds.\n\n\r\n              Watson's first touchdown capped a 91-yard drive on Green Bay’s first possession. Two plays later, McKinney's first interception gave Green Bay the ball at Chicago’s 45-yard line.\n\n\r\n              Lloyd capped Green Bay’s ensuing possession by running over Jaylon Johnson and scoring from 7 yards out on the first play of the second quarter. It was the first career TD run for Lloyd, who scored on a 6-yard reception against the Jets on Sept. 20.\n\n\r\n              “The things that we had done well in the three victories that we had this year, they didn’t show up today,” Ben Johnson said. “We didn’t take care of the football — five turnovers on offense. We didn’t execute at a very high level in the run game or the passing game. Defensively, we let the ball over our head too many times.”\n\n\r\n              Chicago cut Green Bay’s lead to 14-7 when D'Andre Swift scored from 1 yard out on fourth-and-goal in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Green Bay regained control after halftime on Watson's second touchdown. Watson was well covered, but he made the catch by reaching his arms over the helmet of safety Dillon Thieneman, whose back was to the play. \n\n\r\n              Chicago's giveaways prevented the Bears from rallying as Green Bay reasserted itself in this rivalry. The Packers had beaten the Bears 11 straight times from 2019-24 before losing three of the last four meetings.\n\n\r\n              “They’re still little bro, man,” Packers safety Javon Bullard said. “They’re still little bro. Sometimes big bro got to show up, and that’s what we did.”\n\n\r\n              One disappointment for Green Bay was the performance of rookie kicker Trey Smack, who had gone 6 of 7 on field-goal attempts in the Packers' first four games. Smack made a 35-yarder Sunday but missed from 43 and 47 yards and sent an extra-point attempt wide left.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Bears: RB Kyle Monangai (toe, thumb), Chicago's leading rusher, did not play. LB T.J. Edwards left with a hamstring injury in the first half. DL Dayo Odeyingbo (quadriceps), CB Tyrique Stevenson (hamstring) and DB Cam Lewis got hurt in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Packers: DT Javon Hargrave got hurt late in the fourth quarter. LaFleur said after the game that Hargrave is believed to have an Achilles tendon injury. WR Skyy Moore hurt an ankle in the first half and didn't return. CB Benjamin St-Juste injured a hamstring in the second half.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Bears: At Atlanta next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Packers: Host Dallas next Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1011/c9debc4d-f547-469a-ac52-428e524a0692/c9debc4d-f547-469a-ac52-428e524a0692.jpg"},"401872984":{"h":"Stroud runs for a TD, throws for another as Houston beats Titans for 1st win of season","d":"— C.J. Stroud carried the Houston Texans to their first victory of the season, then his coach announced the quarterback is being reunited with his old Ohio State teammate after a ","b":"NASHVILLE, Tenn. -- — C.J. Stroud carried the Houston Texans to their first victory of the season, then his coach announced the quarterback is being reunited with his old Ohio State teammate after a trade with Arizona.\n\n\r\n              A very nice Sunday indeed for both Stroud and the Texans. \n\n\r\n              Stroud threw for 261 yards and a touchdown and ran for another score and the Texans snapped their winless start to the season by routing the Tennessee Titans 26-3. \n\n\r\n              After the win, Stroud was excited at getting to work with wide receiver Marvin Harrison Jr. again.\n\n\r\n              “I loved him in college,” Stroud said. “He’s probably the hardest worker I’ve ever been around in my life. And then just who he is off the field, he’s a great dude, quiet guy, but definitely you feel his presence. So, I’m excited to get him in here and get him learning, and get him around other guys. And I think it’s perfect for him. I think fresh starts are good.”\n\n\r\n              This is the second straight season Houston (1-4) ended an opening winless skid by beating Tennessee. This time, the Texans forced two turnovers and Will Anderson Jr. had three of Houston's four sacks with coach DeMeco Ryans taking over the defensive play-calling this week. \n\n\r\n              “We’re thankful right now to get our first win,” Ryans said. “It’s been a long time coming. Our guys, they poured into work, and they came out, and the work showed itself again on game day. We just have to keep that formula. Everybody just stay humble, keep working the way we work, and we’ll be just fine.”\n\n\r\n              Ka’imi Fairbairn also kicked four field goals giving the Texans taking a 12-3 halftime lead. \n\n\r\n              Houston played keep-away over the second and third quarters outgaining the Titans 225-49 and outscoring them 16-0. The Texans held Tennessee to a season-low 187 total yards.\n\n\r\n              The Titans (0-5) now have lost 11 straight in the AFC South and six straight going back to the end of last season. It's the worst start to a season for this franchise since losing the first six games in 2009. \n\n\r\n              “We’ve got a lot of work to do,” first-year coach Robert Saleh said. “One game is not going to define us. We’re going to continue to work. We’re going to continue to find ways to get better. This organization and team will have its day in the sun.”\n\n\r\n              Tennessee led only after opening with a drive for a 30-yard field goal by Joey Slye. Cam Ward finished throwing for 170 yards.\n\n\r\n              Then the Texans forced the Titans three-and-out, safety Jalen Pitre recovered a fumble by Tyjae Spears on the next Tennessee drive. Jake Hansen picked off Ward's pass up the middle on the ensuing drive. Houston then forced another three-and-out by the Titans.\n\n\r\n              Tennessee managed only one sack as Stroud scrambled safely to the sideline. The Titans' secondary didn't have safety Amani Hooker still in the concussion protocol, and the injuries grew quickly after kickoff. Safety Kevin Winston Jr. will have an MRI exam for the tight quadriceps muscle that sidelined him, cornerback Cor'Dale Flott hurt his chest and Tony Adams returned after clearing a concussion evaluation.\n\n\r\n              Stroud picked apart the Titans, and only the Texans' inability to convert twice inside the Tennessee 20 in the first half kept the final score from being worse. Houston also had to settle for field goals of 35 and 26 yards from Fairbairn after the turnovers.\n\n\r\n              Houston put the game away in the third quarter with a 17-play drive using up 8 minutes, 23 seconds. Stroud kept the drive alive on third-and-11 escaping All-Pro tackle Jeffery Simmons to find Kayshon Boutte for a tiptoe-catch on the sideline for 23 yards. Stroud finished the drive with a 2-yard keeper for 19-3 lead.\n\n\r\n              Stroud added a 9-yard TD pass to Jaylin Noel to start the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Self-inflicted mistakes\r\n              Houston hurt itself repeatedly, piling up 11 penalties for 95 yards. The Titans didn't pick up a penalty until 2:49 left in the third quarter and finished with seven for 83 yards. \n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Texans running back David Montgomery hurt a groin muscle in the second quarter and didn't return. He had just five carries for 11 yards, and Ryans said Montgomery may miss some time. Pitre hurt a shoulder late and will have further tests.\n\n\r\n              Flott didn't return after hurting his chest. The injury happened when teammate Isaiah Oliver, promoted from the practice squad, hit both Flott and Texans tight end Dalton Schultz on an overthrown pass in the end zone. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              The Texans play the Jaguars in London on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              The Titans visit Indianapolis on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1011/c251b5e9-258f-47b4-9bb7-81336d93f9af/c251b5e9-258f-47b4-9bb7-81336d93f9af.jpg"},"401872982":{"h":"Burrow throws 4 TDs, Battle has pair of interceptions as Bengals beat Dolphins 31-24","d":"— The Joe Burrow and Ja'Marr Chase connection never seems to fail for the Cincinnati Bengals.","b":"MIAMI GARDENS, Fla. -- — The Joe Burrow and Ja'Marr Chase connection never seems to fail for the Cincinnati Bengals.\n\n\r\n              Chase missed practice time this week after suffering a concussion last Sunday against Jacksonville, but that didn't stop the All-Pro receiver from coming down with a highlight-reel, one-handed touchdown on a 45-yard throw from Burrow Sunday against the Miami Dolphins.\n\n\r\n              “I saw the protection that I really like. It buys me time and space,” Burrow said. \"Pushed the ball downfield, and when you push the ball downfield with the guys that we have and (with) that kind of time, you can see what number one can do whenever the ball’s in the air. Obviously, an unbelievable play. You don’t want to take it for granted, but we’re used to it at this point.”\n\n\r\n              Burrow threw four touchdown passes, safety Jordan Battle had a pair of interceptions and the Bengals beat the Dolphins 31-24 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “It’s a blessing to get these opportunities,” said Chase, who secured the bobbling pass with his right forearm while he was being defended by cornerback Jason Marshall Jr. late in the third. He caught six passes for 77 yards, and his touchdown made it 28-10.\n\n\r\n              Burrow completed 23 of 32 passes for 190 yards and also threw touchdowns to tight ends Mike Gesicki and Tanner Hudson and running back Samaje Perine. The Bengals quarterback took advantage of the Dolphins' young secondary, completing passes to nine different receivers.\n\n\r\n              Battle picked off Malik Willis on back-to-back drives in the second quarter. The veteran had a big game after saying this week he had a tell on how Willis throws the football — pointing out the Dolphins quarterback apparently taps the ball before he throws.\n\n\r\n              “You study players and see things,” Battle said Sunday, \"but it’s just trusting the game plan and putting me in position to make plays.”\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins (0-5) pulled within 7 with 45 seconds left on a Willis' 1-yard touchdown run, set up by a 23-yard catch by rookie Kevin Coleman Jr., who also caught a touchdown earlier in the fourth. Riley Patterson clanked the extra-point attempt off the left upright, and the ensuing onside kick went out of bounds to end it and snap the Cincinnati's two-game skid.\n\n\r\n              Willis had just five completions in the first half and finished 15 of 27 with 137 yards passing. The Dolphins offseason free-agent pickup was late on throws and missed several open receivers in addition to the pair of interceptions and a lost fumble.\n\n\r\n              “We've just got to go back to the drawing board,\" Willis said, \"watch these last few games, compare it to practice, understand what we’re good at, what we’re not good at, understand what guys are comfortable with and what they’re not comfortable with, and then just try to go and execute the next opportunity we can get.”\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins moved the ball inside the Cincinnati 1 on a 26-yard rush by rookie Chris Bell and runs by Ollie Gordon late in the third. But Willis took an 8-yard sack, then forced a pass to tight end Greg Dulcich that fell incomplete, and Miami settled for Patterson's 29-yard field goal to make it 21-10.\n\n\r\n              Cincinnati (3-2) led 21-7 at the half, mostly by capitalizing on numerous Dolphins blunders. Miami gave up an 81-yard return to Dohnte Meyers on the opening kickoff. That set up Gesicki's 4-yard TD catch three plays later, and the former Dolphin celebrated by Griddy-ing near the end zone.\n\n\r\n              The Dolphins responded with an 11-play, 60-yard drive that featured no completed passes — Willis was 0-for-2 — but 51 yards rushing. Ollie Gordon capped it with a 1-yard rush.\n\n\r\n              Gordon began the season as Miami's third-string running back, but has seemingly moved up the depth chart past backup Jaylen Wright in the absence of De'Von Achane, who suffered a season-ending ACL injury in Week 3. Gordon finished with 18 rushes for 88 yards.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Bengals: Linebacker Swayze Bozeman, who entered questionable with a groin injury, left in the third with a right foreman injury and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Dolphins: LB Jordyn Brooks injured his right foot in the second quarter but returned in the third. ... Right guard Jonah Savaiinaea left in the second with an ankle injury and did not return ... CB JuJu Brents was evaluated and cleared of a concussion in the second, but did not return because of a stinger. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Both teams are on a bye in Week 6.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1011/dm_261011_Bengals_beat_Dolphins_IA/dm_261011_Bengals_beat_Dolphins_IA.jpg"},"401872986":{"h":"Drake Maye accounts for 5 TDs as Patriots roll past Raiders 45-17","d":"— Drake Maye didn't have his top receiver, and the New England Patriots were missing both of their starting cornerbacks.","b":"FOXBOROUGH, Mass. -- — Drake Maye didn't have his top receiver, and the New England Patriots were missing both of their starting cornerbacks.\n\n\r\n              The third-year quarterback found plenty of other targets, and his defense still found a way to slow down Kirk Cousins and the Las Vegas Raiders.\n\n\r\n              Maye threw four touchdown passes and ran for another score, helping the Patriots pull away in the second half of a 45-17 victory on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Maye was 26 of 34 for 374 yards passing in the second 300-yard game of his career. Two TDs went to Pop Douglas and one each to Hunter Henry and Kyle Williams.\n\n\r\n              Rhamondre Stevenson added a scoring run in a second consecutive victory that answered a sluggish start for the defending AFC champions.\n\n\r\n              With receiver Mack Hollins missing the first of at least four games with a calf injury, Maye had five players catch at least three passes, including Stevenson. Two pass-catchers who didn't score — Efton Chism III and Eli Raridon — each had five catches, the team high, for 57 yards. \n\n\r\n              “It was beautiful to be a part of today,\" Maye said. \"There’s still some out there.” \n\n\r\n              On a day the Patriots celebrated the 25th anniversary of the first of their six first Super Bowl championships, Maye became the first with back-to-back games of at least three TDs passing for New England since Tom Brady in 2018. It was also Maye’s first game this season without a turnover. He scored on a 3-yard run.\n\n\r\n              The Patriots (3-2) finished with season-high 536 yards, and the defense forced while holding Las Vegas under 26 points for the first time this season without injured cornerbacks Christian Gonzalez and Carlton Davis III.\n\n\r\n              “That’s what it has to be,\" coach Mike Vrabel said. \"They realize when you get an opportunity, you do something with it.”\n\n\r\n              Cousins was 19 of 28 for 243 yards with a touchdown to Brock Bowers that pulled the Raiders (3-2) even early, but they were trailing by 28 points when they finally found the end zone again in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Cousins had limited time to operate throughout the game and was sacked three times. \n\n\r\n              \"They did a nice job with their pressures, getting home quickly. Made it hard for us,\" Cousins said.\n\n\r\n              Raiders coach Klint Kubiak said it's clear there is work to do after back-to-back losses.\n\n\r\n              “We’re not happy with what happened today, and we’ve got to go do something about it,” Kubiak said.\n\n\r\n              No. 1 overall draft pick Fernando Mendoza replaced Cousins with the game out of reach later in the fourth. Kubiak said Mendoza's appearance doesn't change the QB depth chart for now. \n\n\r\n              \"Just wanted to get (Mendoza) some exposure,\" Kubiak said.\n\n\r\n              Scoring quickly\r\n              Four of the Patriots seven scoring drives lasted less than three minutes. New England scored touchdowns on four of five trips inside the 20-yard line.\n\n\r\n              Fast start for Patriots\r\n              It didn’t take long for New England’s offense to get going.\n\n\r\n              After a quick three-and-out by the Raiders to open the game, New England needed just five plays to cap a 79-yard drive with a 35-yard TD pass from Maye to a wide-open Douglas.\n\n\r\n              The drive included a 16-yard pass to Romeo Doubs to get the Patriots into Las Vegas territory.\n\n\r\n              The Raiders responded on the next series. First, Cousins connected on long completions to Bowers and Coby White. He then capitalized on a pass interference call on Kevin Byard that set up a 13-yard scoring pass to Bowers, who had eight catches for 110 yards.\n\n\r\n              New England kept its momentum going after Bowers' TD, scoring on a 5-yard run by Stevenson.\n\n\r\n              Las Vegas had a chance before halftime after Corey Kiner fumbled at the New England 37. But Matt Gay’s 49-yard field goal try hit the left upright, and the Patriots took a 14-point halftime lead on Maye's 11-yard pass to Henry 24 seconds before the break.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Raiders: DT Tonka Hemingway left in the first quarter with a neck injury and was ruled out. ... DE Patrick Johnson (neck) left in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Patriots: RB TreVeyon Henderson limped to the locker room in the second quarter with an ankle injury. Two plays later, LT Will Campbell was helped off with a right knee injury. Neither returned. ... CB Charles Woods exited in the third quarter with a shoulder injury. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Raiders: Host the Bills next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Patriots: Host the Jets next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1011/806edf2e-e8ce-45f4-ac45-e6f4edc25ae5/806edf2e-e8ce-45f4-ac45-e6f4edc25ae5.jpg"},"401872987":{"h":"Jefferson, Jones and Murray all produce to help the unbeaten Vikings top the slumping Saints 23-13","d":"— Justin Jefferson's resolve to overcome an ankle injury and take the field in his hometown produced a gratifying payoff for himself and the Minnesota Vikings.","b":"NEW ORLEANS -- — Justin Jefferson's resolve to overcome an ankle injury and take the field in his hometown produced a gratifying payoff for himself and the Minnesota Vikings.\n\n\r\n              The four-time Pro Bowl receiver out of LSU “was living in the training room just trying to make sure he could do everything he could to give himself a chance,\" Vikings coach Kevin O'Connell said. “We worked him out before the game and he was ready.” \n\n\r\n              Jefferson highlighted his return to the lineup with a leaping touchdown catch over multiple defenders, Aaron Jones made up for an early fumble with a pivotal, fourth-quarter touchdown and the Vikings remained unbeaten with a 23-13 victory over the slumping New Orleans Saints on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “All of my friends and family were here,” said a beaming Jefferson, whose eagerness to speak with reporters was evident in the way he quickly emerged from the locker room — still in uniform — before even O'Connell took questions. \n\n\r\n              “A touchdown to celebrate and to get a victory here, I couldn’t ask for more,” Jefferson said. \n\n\r\n              Will Reichard made all three of his field-goal attempts from 40 or more yards for the Vikings (5-0), including a 55-yarder to put Minnesota up 10 in the fourth quarter and all but doom the Saints (1-4) to a third straight loss — all in the Superdome.\n\n\r\n              “You only get so many games at home in front of this crowd, who consistently shows up,\" Saints center Erik McCoy said. \n\n\r\n              Jones' first-half fumble, after which he limped off, favoring his left knee, led to New Orleans' only offensive points on Kendre Miller's 2-yard run in the second quarter. But the Vikings' leading rusher returned to finish with 113 yards rushing on 25 carries.\n\n\r\n              Jefferson, who sat out Week 4 with an ankle injury, caught six passes for 79 yards from Kyler Murray, who threw for 229 yards and a TD without a turnover and also scrambled for 51 yards.\n\n\r\n              Led by their blitz-happy defense, the Vikings came in allowing an NFL-low 12.8 points per game. In addition to allowing only one offensive TD, they forced three turnovers and held New Orleans to 142 total yards.\n\n\r\n              “When you do something like that, it's a special feeling and a special defense,” said cornerback Byron Murphy Jr., who intercepted Saints QB Tyler Shough twice.\n\n\r\n              Saints safety Jonas Sanker returned a fumble 87 yards for New Orleans’ other TD, briefly tying the score at 13 in the third quarter. \n\n\r\n              Saints second-year coach Kellen Moore, who designs and calls offensive plays, said he “felt like we had a plan\" for Minnesota's defensive pressure.\n\n\r\n              “Obviously, the plan wasn’t good enough,” Moore continued. \"That’s on me.”\n\n\r\n              Before Shough's interceptions, New Orleans lost a fumble — and ultimately, a 7-6 halftime lead — when returner Jayden Price was stripped on the kickoff to open the second half.\n\n\r\n              “We’ve got to learn how to not lose the game before we win it,” said Shough, who was held to 96 yards passing. “We’ve got to not shoot ourselves in the foot.”\n\n\r\n              Minnesota recovered Price's fumble on the Saints 37 and cashed in when Jefferson came down with Murray's 15-yard scoring pass behind three defenders in the back of the end zone.\n\n\r\n              “It was just Kyler giving me the opportunity to go up and make a play,” Jeffersons said. “I definitely wasn’t the most open, but it was a tremendous throw.”\n\n\r\n              Jefferson, who won a national title with LSU in the Superdome, had returned only once since his NFL career began — as a rookie in 2020, with attendance restricted by social distancing protocols during the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\n\r\n              “So, it wasn’t the whole stadium; it was a couple thousand,” Jefferson noted. “To be back and feel the energy and feel the love from our fans and the Saints fans and the people who grew up watching me at LSU, it was definitely a cool moment.”\n\n\r\n              Drew Brees received his Hall of Fame ring\r\n              Retired Saints QB Drew Brees was honored with a ring ceremony at halftime — and also had his name unveiled in the team's Ring of Honor, which encircles the upper deck.\n\n\r\n              Brees received a rousing ovation as he was introduced on the field, wearing his gold Hall of Fame jacket, and presented his ring by Pro Football Hall of Fame president Jim Porter.\n\n\r\n              “It has been one of the greatest honors of my life to be able to represent this city, this community, this fan base and our organization,” Brees said. “I will always love you, New Orleans. I will always represent you.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Vikings tight end T.J. Hockenson was helped off the field after hitting his head hard on the turf while being tackled during a 7-yard catch.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Vikings: Enter their bye week before hosting Indianapolis on Oct. 25.\n\n\r\n              Saints: Visit the New York Giants next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1011/43af3849842249fbb3a307c0defbfb84964/43af3849842249fbb3a307c0defbfb84964.jpg"},"401872983":{"h":"Allen has late 22-yard TD run and Jets' defense, special teams come up big in 17-12 win over Browns","d":"— Demario Davis has seen a lot in his 15 NFL seasons. So when the New York Jets linebacker makes a declarative statement, it holds some weight.","b":"EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. -- — Demario Davis has seen a lot in his 15 NFL seasons. So when the New York Jets linebacker makes a declarative statement, it holds some weight.\n\n\r\n              This time, it might also raise some eyebrows.\n\n\r\n              Braelon Allen ran 22 yards for the go-ahead touchdown late in the fourth quarter after New York recovered a fumbled punt snap, and the Jets ended a three-game skid with a 17-12 victory over the Cleveland Browns on a rain-soaked Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “I would go as far as to say this was a historic win for this organization,” Davis said.\n\n\r\n              Whoa.\n\n\r\n              Rookie David Bailey had three of New York’s eight sacks of Deshaun Watson, and Geno Smith had a touchdown pass to Garrett Wilson on the first drive for the Jets (2-3), who ended the Browns’ three-game winning streak.\n\n\r\n              Cleveland (3-2) had a final chance with 1:41 left and no timeouts. But New York’s defense, as it did all game, stiffened and sealed it when Will McDonald had a strip-sack of Watson on fourth down and recovered the fumble.\n\n\r\n              “You're talking about a team that had lost the last three games and when you're going through that type of adversity, it can weigh on you,” Davis said. “It can weigh on you emotionally, it can weigh on your faith, it can weigh on your belief. And it can weigh on an organization that has historically been disappointed.”\n\n\r\n              Davis said the Jets didn't waver or flinch. Even though it looked as if they might be headed toward another tough loss.\n\n\r\n              “A good team win,” coach Aaron Glenn said. “Every part had some bright moments.”\n\n\r\n              Smith finished 17 of 30 for 182 yards and the TD to Wilson, who had seven catches for 104 yards, and Allen ran for 116 yards and the winning touchdown on 24 carries.\n\n\r\n              This one wasn't pretty, with a steady rain falling throughout as the remnants of Hurricane Isaias blew through the Northeast. But the struggling Jets were able to bounce back after giving up a fourth-quarter lead.\n\n\r\n              “That’s the feeling we’re hunting every weekend,” Wilson said. “It felt good.”\n\n\r\n              Watson, who was 15 of 33 for 155 yards, threw a 29-yard touchdown pass to rookie Denzel Boston to give the Browns their first advantage. Andre Szmyt missed the extra point, putting Cleveland up 12-10 with 6:15 left.\n\n\r\n              The Browns got the ball back moments later when Carson Schwesinger sacked Smith, and Adin Huntington recovered the fumble.\n\n\r\n              The Jets forced a three-and-out and then their special teams came through when a high snap from Cleveland's Rex Sunahara went through punter Corey Bojorquez's hands — perhaps with an assist from the rain — and bounced behind him as several players chased after it and Trevin Wallace recovered for New York.\n\n\r\n              “The ball was wet the whole game,” Browns coach Todd Monken said. “Is that a part of it? Probably. Is that a part of some of the drops? Probably. Is that a part of some of the ball security? Probably. But we both had to play in it.”\n\n\r\n              Three plays later, Allen took a handoff, found a crease off the left side and scooted 22 yards for a touchdown that put the Jets ahead with 3:01 remaining and sent the poncho-wearing fans in the MetLife Stadium stands into a frenzy.\n\n\r\n              The Jets got off to a fast start\r\n              The Jets forced a three-and-out on the Browns' first drive — notable after Chicago had no punts against New York last Sunday in a 23-12 win.\n\n\r\n              “I give those guys all the credit in the world,” Glenn said. “They flushed whatever happened last week and they moved on to this week.”\n\n\r\n              Smith and the offense went to work quickly and Wilson capped an efficient opening drive by taking a short pass, making a pretty spin move out of a tackle attempt by Ronnie Hillman and scoring a 32-yard touchdown.\n\n\r\n              The Browns were driving on their second possession and facing first-and-10 from the Jets 25 when Watson threw a deep pass to KC Concepcion, who appeared to make a catch — just as Minkah Fitzpatrick put a crushing hit on the receiver, making contact with his helmet and causing the ball to drop.\n\n\r\n              Concepcion went face down on the turf and appeared to be unconscious. The rookie first-round draft pick eventually walked off under his own power but was quickly ruled out.\n\n\r\n              “I saw him after the game. He was in good spirits,” said Monken, who said he assumed Concepcion is in the concussion protocol.\n\n\r\n              Szmyt kicked a 25-yard field goal on that drive and added a 41-yarder early in the second quarter to make it 7-6.\n\n\r\n              The Jets appeared on their way to adding to their lead with just under six minutes left in the third when Kenyon Sadiq caught a 6-yard pass, but Quincy Williams — against his former team — forced a fumble that Hickman recovered at the Cleveland 7.\n\n\r\n              New York scored for the first time since its opening drive on Jason Sanders' 54-yard field goal 23 seconds into the fourth quarter, putting the Jets up 10-6.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Browns: RB Raheim Sanders (hip) and RT Tytus Howard (knee) left in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Jets: Glenn said Sadiq is in the concussion protocol after he was injured on his fumble.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Browns: Host Baltimore next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Jets: Play at New England next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              —\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1011/dm_261011_Browns_lose_to_Jets_IA/dm_261011_Browns_lose_to_Jets_IA.jpg"},"401872985":{"h":"Jaylen Warren's brilliance leads the Steelers past the Colts 34-22","d":"— Jaylen Warren spent time before Pittsburgh's game against Indianapolis watching old highlights of himself, back when the fifth-year running back was just 5-foot-8 and 220 pounds of raw material playing for a junior college in Utah, a time in his...","b":"PITTSBURGH -- — Jaylen Warren spent time before Pittsburgh's game against Indianapolis watching old highlights of himself, back when the fifth-year running back was just 5-foot-8 and 220 pounds of raw material playing for a junior college in Utah, a time in his life when the spotlight of the NFL seemed a million miles away.\n\n\r\n              That spotlight is considerably closer now.\n\n\r\n              So close that Warren is practically standing in it.\n\n\r\n              Warren had a star turn during the Steelers' 34-22 victory over the Colts on Sunday, piling up 188 total yards and two long touchdowns while outplaying Indy's Jonathan Taylor in a three-hour showcase for Warren's journey from undrafted rookie free agent to maybe one of the NFL's best players at his position.\n\n\r\n              “I've always believed in myself,” Warren said.\n\n\r\n              So have the Steelers (3-2), who relied heavily on Warren's churning legs to bounce back from a loss to Cleveland.\n\n\r\n              Warren's 80-yard catch-and-run score in the first quarter gave Pittsburgh an early jolt, and his 50-yard sprint to the end zone through the middle of the Indianapolis defense in the third quarter ended with Warren clearing an imaginary hurdle at the goal line, a nod to the teenager who would occasionally do the same while scoring for Snow College in Ephraim, Utah.\n\n\r\n              In between the splashy plays were grimy yards between the tackles, including a 1-yard gain on fourth-and-1 in Indianapolis territory in the fourth quarter that set up the second of tight end Pat Freiermuth's touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              “He's the man,” Steelers coach Mike McCarthy said of Warren. “He's hauling it right now for us.”\n\n\r\n              Warren hasn't really had a choice. With backfield mate Rico Dowdle missing a third straight game with a toe injury, Warren was Pittsburgh's only experienced option. He showed again he is capable — and more than willing — to carry a heavy workload.\n\n\r\n              “Whenever my name is called, I've got to seize the moment,” said Warren, who has 490 total yards in the three games since Dowdle went out. “With Rico being down, obviously it sucks, but knowing he's one day closer to coming back, I feel like I can hold it down until then.”\n\n\r\n              The Steelers needed Warren, Aaron Rodgers and their offense to be productive on a day the Colts (2-3) hung around well into the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Rodgers completed 22 of 31 passes for 235 yards and three scores. While Warren did most of the work on their 80-yard connection, the 42-year-old Rodgers paid the price by absorbing a crushing hit from 314-pound Colts defensive tackle Grover Stewart as he flicked the ball to the do-everything running back.\n\n\r\n              “I feel like I kind of earned it a bit,” Rodgers said about his ninth touchdown pass of at least 80 yards or more, which tied Ben Roethlisberger's NFL record. “Maybe not 80 yards and a touchdown, but yeah.”\n\n\r\n              Pat Freiermuth caught five passes for 44 yards and two scores for Pittsburgh, the second a 17-yard toss from Rodgers with 6:40 remaining that gave the Steelers enough cushion to beat the Colts for the 10th time in 12 meetings.\n\n\r\n              Colts have too many field goals, not enough TDs\r\n              Daniel Jones threw for 267 yards for Indianapolis (2-3) and avoided the turnovers that have plagued him whenever he's faced the Steelers, but the Colts also had drives consistently stall in Pittsburgh territory. \n\n\r\n              Spencer Shrader kicked five field goals for Indianapolis, including three in the second half from 40 yards and in.\n\n\r\n              “We settled for way too many field goals today,” Colts coach Shane Steichen said. “Those points are huge. They’re four-point plays. Spence did a great job making the kicks, but we have to score touchdowns when we’re in the red zone.”\n\n\r\n              Three times, the Colts pulled within a point, and Pittsburgh responded each time behind Warren.\n\n\r\n              Warren said earlier in the week he always wants to be the best running back in the stadium on game day, and he outperformed Taylor, who was limited to 82 total yards and held out of the end zone.\n\n\r\n              “We know it’s tough to score touchdowns, but in this league, touchdowns is how you win games,” Taylor said.\n\n\r\n              Tyler Warren finished with seven receptions for 83 yards and the second rushing touchdown of his career, but it wasn't enough for the Colts to win at Pittsburgh for the first time since 2008.\n\n\r\n              Not with Warren becoming the first player since Herschel Walker for Dallas in 1986 to have a touchdown run of at least 50 yards and a TD catch of at least 80 yards in a game.\n\n\r\n              “That's unbelievable,” Warren said before deflecting praise as usual, adding later, “It's an honor. It's a credit to my teammates for achieving stats like that.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Steelers: WR/special teams ace Ben Skowronek left in the second quarter with a calf injury. ... WR Roman Wilson exited in the second quarter after injuring a hand while trying to make a catch. ... LB T.J. Watt was treated for a facial injury in the second quarter after getting gouged by Indianapolis RT Jalen Travis.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Colts: Host AFC South rival Tennessee next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Steelers: Visit Tampa Bay next Sunday. \n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1011/3b612187-8391-407a-8c80-0694f90e8263/3b612187-8391-407a-8c80-0694f90e8263.jpg"},"401872988":{"h":"Jayden Daniels returns and scores twice, but overtime gaffe costs Commanders in 27-27 tie vs. Giants","d":"— Jayden Daniels' return to the lineup, for just under 70 minutes of game time, looked to be exactly what the Commanders were hoping for.","b":"LANDOVER, Md. -- — Jayden Daniels' return to the lineup, for just under 70 minutes of game time, looked to be exactly what the Commanders were hoping for.\n\n\r\n              He completed 68% of his passes, combined for 302 yards passing and rushing, and showcased his speed with two long touchdown runs. Most important, he stayed healthy.\n\n\r\n              But a gaffe in overtime, when Daniels took off on an ill-advised scramble in the closing seconds, cost Washington a chance at victory, ending Sunday's game against the New York Giants in a 27-27 tie. With 12 seconds left and the ball at the Giants 24, Washington called a pass play, but Daniels held onto the ball and went down for no gain.\n\n\r\n              Washington had no timeouts left and couldn't spike the ball before the clock hit zero, ending a mistake-filled final period with zero points by either team.\n\n\r\n              “The sequencing of that does not fall on (Daniels),” Commanders coach Dan Quinn said. “It's an outside throw or a clock — an out of bounds throw, or a clock. That has to come from me. I have to put him in that spot. I can do that earlier and we can finish the kick earlier. We don't need to gain more yards.”\n\n\r\n              The final play overshadowed Daniels' return from a dislocated left elbow. Daniels suffered the ugly-looking injury in a Week 2 loss to the Cowboys, the second time he has dislocated his non-throwing arm.\n\n\r\n              Wearing a brace on the arm, Daniels showed the playmaking ability he brought to the Commanders during his standout rookie season in 2024. He was 17 of 25 passing for 199 yards, and he rushed for 103 yards and two TDs on nine carries.\n\n\r\n              He wasn't the only player to make a critical mistake in overtime.\n\n\r\n              Washington (1-3-1) got the ball with 2 minutes left in OT when Daron Payne stripped the ball from Cam Skattebo with New York in range for a game-ending field goal.\n\n\r\n              “I’ve got to hold onto the ball, and make sure that these guys know that when my name is called, that we’re going to be OK,” Skattebo said.\n\n\r\n              Coach John Harbaugh said the Giants' goal on their final drive was to run the ball twice, get as close as they could to the end zone and then kick a field goal.\n\n\r\n              “It’s a heck of a lot better than a loss, but it’s not as good as a win,” Harbaugh said. “I know that just sounds so obvious, but that’s kind of how you feel. We’ll take it, we’ll take it right now.”\n\n\r\n              A kick by the Giants wouldn't have been a sure thing, given the struggles of Dominic Zvada, who missed his third field goal of the game on New York's first possession of OT.\n\n\r\n              “He'll learn from that,” Harbaugh said. “He was going to miss at some point, now he's got his first miss — he's got his first three misses.”\n\n\r\n              Winston, Nabers and Likely rally the Giants\r\n              The Commanders had a two-touchdown lead in the fourth quarter before Jameis Winston led a furious comeback for the NFC East-leading Giants (3-1-1). He connected with Isaiah Likely for a 17-yard touchdown midway through the fourth quarter — Likely's second TD catch of the game — and then found Malik Nabers for the tying score, also from 17 yards out, with 1:43 left.\n\n\r\n              Winston finished with 326 yards passing and three touchdowns. \n\n\r\n              “I thought he was amazing in the fourth quarter, I thought he was amazing in the second half,” Harbaugh said. “I thought his ability to come out and change like that, we changed our offense up and tried some different things, he really settled into the things we did offensively. He was amazing. First half, he wasn’t, and he’ll be the first to tell you that.”\n\n\r\n              Daniels had rushing TDs of 38 and 26 yards in the second half, the latter of which gave the Commanders a seemingly comfortable 27-13 lead.\n\n\r\n              But the Giants rallied behind Winston and his two top targets, Nabers (11 catches for 121 yards and a TD) and Likely (11 receptions for 94 yards and the two scores). On his second TD, Likely had the ball punched out just after he crossed the goal line, and the score was awarded after a replay review.\n\n\r\n              After Nabers' touchdown, the Commanders had one last chance to win in regulation, but their drive stalled at midfield.\n\n\r\n              The Commanders opened the game with a four-play drive that included two passes by Daniels that covered 34 yards, a scramble by the elusive QB and a 33-yard touchdown run by Jacory Croskey-Merritt.\n\n\r\n              Washington's Drew Stevens missed the extra point. He kicked two first-half field goals, while the Giants missed two field goals of their own to trail 12-3 at halftime.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Giants: RT Jermaine Eluemunor had to be carried off the field with a left calf injury. CB Colton Hood was injured on a kickoff late in the third quarter and was helped off the field. He was evaluated for a concussion. S Tyler Nubin was helped off the field in the fourth with a neck injury.\n\n\r\n              Washington WR Terry McLaurin returned after missing last week's game with a hamstring injury, finishing with four catches for 62 yards.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Giants: Host New Orleans next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Commanders: Visit San Francisco on Monday, Oct. 19.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/wsc/2026/1011/adade2e7-2a22-4d4c-8194-73a7fb26d219/adade2e7-2a22-4d4c-8194-73a7fb26d219.jpg"},"401872989":{"h":"Broncos keep Chargers winless with key stop that fuels 4th-quarter rally in 23-17 win","d":"— Jim Harbaugh is blaming the winless Los Angeles Chargers’ latest loss on himself.","b":"INGLEWOOD, Calif. -- — Jim Harbaugh is blaming the winless Los Angeles Chargers’ latest loss on himself.\n\n\r\n              The Bolts blew a seven-point lead in the fourth quarter, when the Denver Broncos scored the final 13 points, including a tiebreaking 19-yard field goal from Wil Lutz with 5:07 remaining, in a 23-17 win Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Los Angeles quarterback Justin Herbert failed to pick up a first down on a crucial fourth-and-1 try early in the fourth, and the Broncos capitalized by tying the score at 17-all and dominating the rest of the way.\n\n\r\n              “It was the wrong decision,” Harbaugh said after the Chargers dropped to 0-5 for the first time since 2003. “Can’t think of a time where I’ve ever been more mad at myself.”\n\n\r\n              The failed fourth-down conversion sent the Chargers spiraling, with Herbert’s fumble and interception leaving them one of three winless teams in the league. He tied his career record with a turnover in five consecutive games.\n\n\r\n              “When I saw them gap down, I felt like I should call timeout out there and punt the ball,” Harbaugh said. “Right now, the only thing I can think of is that I screwed that up.”\n\n\r\n              Lutz’s go-ahead kick came with 5:07 remaining, and his 36-yarder with 1:08 left extended the lead for the Broncos (3-2), who had plenty of orange-clad fans in the stands cheering their first win at SoFi Stadium since 2023.\n\n\r\n              Denver earned its first division win coach Sean Payton calling the plays. He took those duties away from offensive coordinator Davis Webb earlier in the week. The Broncos won without injured cornerbacks Pat Surtain II (ankle) and Riley Moss (ribs).\n\n\r\n              “There were some things that were good,” Payton said, “and then specifically staying on the field and just converting four or five more third downs. You know, just I’ve got to be a little bit better, too.”\n\n\r\n              Denver quarterback Bo Nix called the play calling switch “smooth,” adding, “It was a collaboration.”\n\n\r\n              The Chargers came in with a decimated offensive line that featured four rookies trying to protect Herbert, who was 14 of 24 for 121 yards passing with a touchdown and an interception while getting sacked five times.\n\n\r\n              The stuffed run on fourth-and-1 might have been bigger than all the sacks.\n\n\r\n              “Got to find a way to get a yard and I didn’t do that,” a curt Herbert said. “It’s on me to go get a yard. I have complete faith in our guys and myself, and it’s on us to go get a yard and convert.”\n\n\r\n              Jake Slaughter, one of the four rookie linemen, said, “We’re just going to keep swinging.”\n\n\r\n              Courtland Sutton caught a 5-yard touchdown pass from Nix, tying the score at 17 midway through the fourth. The Broncos got the ball back after stopping Herbert short, and the Chargers sputtered the rest of the way.\n\n\r\n              The ensuing LA drive fell apart when Herbert got sacked for an 8-yard loss at his 24-yard line by Zach Allen, who forced a fumble that was recovered by Que Robinson.\n\n\r\n              After Lutz’s go-ahead field goal, Herbert fumbled but recovered, then scrambled up the middle for a 12-yard gain before getting intercepted by Brandon Jones on fourth down.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers took a 17-10 lead late in the third quarter on Kimani Vidal’s 42-yard TD run the first play after Tuli Tuipulotu forced and recovered a fumble when Nix tried to scramble.\n\n\r\n              Nix was 16 of 27 for 123 yards and a TD.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers tied the game 10-all on Hayden Rucci’s first career TD, a 7-yard pass from Herbert in the third. Playing his 100th career game, Herbert set up the score with a quarterback sweep. With Charlie Kolar blocking, Herbert ran 23 yards to the Denver 7 for the first down. \n\n\r\n              The Broncos took a 7-0 lead in the first quarter after Kolar’s first career fumble. After a face mask penalty by the Chargers, J.K. Dobbins scored a 2-yard TD — his first rushing score of the season.\n\n\r\n              The Chargers cut the lead to 7-3 on Cameron Dicker’s 42-yard field goal in the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              Lutz’s 42-yard field goal gave Denver a 10-3 lead on its next possession, when Harbaugh twice unsuccessfully challenged calls, making him 16 of 45 on challenges in his NFL career.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Broncos: S Talanoa Hufanga (ankle) went out in the third quarter and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Chargers: WR Tre’ Harris went out in the third quarter. ... LB Bud Dupree (neck) left in the second quarter and did not return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Broncos: Host Seattle on Thursday.\n\n\r\n              Chargers: Visit AFC West-leading Kansas City on Sunday. \n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              This story correct a previous version to show the Chargers are off to their first 0-5 start since 2003, not 2023.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1011/dm_261011_Broncos_beat_Chargers_IA/dm_261011_Broncos_beat_Chargers_IA.jpg"},"401872991":{"h":"Jared Goff, Jahmyr Gibbs lead the Lions' offensive romp in a 45-21 win over the Cardinals","d":"— Dan Campbell felt his team was teetering on the edge of a lost season after four shaky performances to start the year so the Detroit head coach dialed up...","b":"GLENDALE, Ariz. -- — Dan Campbell felt his team was teetering on the edge of a lost season after four shaky performances to start the year so the Detroit head coach dialed up the intensity for Week 5, putting in an ultra-aggressive game plan that featured the fearlessness he wanted to see.\n\n\r\n              The Lions rewarded him with their best game of the year.\n\n\r\n              Jared Goff threw three touchdown passes, Jahmyr Gibbs added 151 yards rushing and two more scores and Detroit shook off a slow start by scoring on seven consecutive drives to roll past the Arizona Cardinals 45-21 on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              “The important thing is you go all in, no matter what you're going to do,” Campbell said. “And either it crumbles or they rise up, but you're going all in. You've got to push your chips all the way in and you've got to believe in it.\n\n\r\n              “So we did. We applied a little pressure and the players really responded.”\n\n\r\n              Detroit (3-2) scored three touchdowns on fourth down and the defense had a bounce-back performance, led by cornerback Roger McCreary, who had six passes defended while playing mostly man-to-man coverage. D.J. Wonnum had two sacks.\n\n\r\n              Gibbs barreled into the end zone on a 1-yard run in the second quarter and added a 3-yard TD run in the fourth, bouncing off the offensive line before reversing field and finding room to run. The three-time Pro Bowl selection has scored an NFL-record 58 touchdowns before his 25th birthday, passing Lions great Barry Sanders, who had 57.\n\n\r\n              “He's unbelievable, he really is,” Goff said. “We did a good job in the run game and still probably didn't do as well as we could. He made a lot of guys miss in the backfield and got a lot after the first touch. Gosh, he's electric.”\n\n\r\n              Jameson Williams had his first multi-TD receiving game, catching touchdowns for 8 and 39 yards.\n\n\r\n              Harrison's trade caps tough day for Cardinals\r\n              Arizona (1-4) has lost nine straight home games, a streak that's lasted more than a year.\n\n\r\n              It was a disappointing, weird afternoon for the Cardinals, who finalized a trade to send receiver Marvin Harrison Jr. to the Houston Texans in the middle of the first quarter. Harrison was one of the team's five inactives against the Lions and wasn't seen at the stadium.\n\n\r\n              The No. 4 overall pick in 2024 out of Ohio State has been a major disappointment in the desert and he had just five catches for 85 yards and a touchdown through four games this season. \n\n\r\n              But the non-Harrison version of the Cardinals wasn't any better Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Arizona took an early 7-0 lead after a 2-yard touchdown pass from Jacoby Brissett to tight end Teagan Quitoriano, but right about the time news of the Harrison deal started spreading, the momentum shifted.\n\n\r\n              Lions pour it on in second quarter\r\n              Detroit scored three straight touchdowns and a field goal in the second quarter to take a 24-7 lead into halftime. Tight end Sam LaPorta caught the second of the three TDs, finding himself wide open for a 38-yard catch and run. \n\n\r\n              The Cardinals are giving up 37 points per game over their four-game losing streak, putting pressure on defensive coordinator Nick Rallis' job status. First-year Arizona coach Mike LaFleur didn't say if changes were imminent, but that he understands there are a lot of problems to fix.\n\n\r\n              “Anytime there's a blowout like this, it starts with coaching,\" LaFleur said.\n\n\r\n              Williams added a 39-yard touchdown catch in the third quarter on a perfectly-thrown ball by Goff, who completed 19 of 30 passes for 279 yards. The quarterback also ran for a 1-yard touchdown.\n\n\r\n              “Goff is just Goff,” Campbell said. “He's as good as they come.”\n\n\r\n              Lions' defense shows improvement\r\n              While the Lions were piling up points, the Cardinals had five straight three-and-out drives during the first half, gaining just 12 total yards. On the sixth drive, Brissett threw an interception that ended the second quarter.\n\n\r\n              It was a better performance by the Lions' defense, which came into Sunday giving up 31.8 points per game to rank No. 31 in the NFL.\n\n\r\n              Brissett completed 29 of 47 passes for 292 yards, three touchdowns and one interception. He is 2-15 as the team's starter since taking over for Kyler Murray last season. \n\n\r\n              Quitoriano finished with two receiving touchdowns, adding a 13-yard catch in the third quarter. Trey McBride also had a 9-yard TD catch late in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Lions: LB Devin White (forearm) and KR/PR Tom Kennedy (knee) both left the game and didn't return. Campbell said that White would likely need surgery and would miss extended time.\n\n\r\n              Cardinals: S Wydett Williams Jr. (concussion) was hurt late in the first half.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Lions: Host Green Bay on Oct. 25.\n\n\r\n              Cardinals: At the Los Angeles Rams next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1012/dm_261012_Lions_IA/dm_261012_Lions_IA.jpg"},"401872992":{"h":"Brock Purdy leads 2 late scoring drives to deliver a 32-29 win for the 49ers over the Seahawks","d":"— When the game was on the line and San Francisco's struggling offense needed to move the ball, Mike Evans stepped up and showed why the 49ers went after him so hard this offseason.","b":"SEATTLE -- — When the game was on the line and San Francisco's struggling offense needed to move the ball, Mike Evans stepped up and showed why the 49ers went after him so hard this offseason.\n\n\r\n              Purdy found Evans repeatedly on the go-ahead touchdown drive and then led the Niners down the field again for a 43-yard field goal by Eddy Pineiro with 1:56 to play that gave San Francisco a 32-29 win over the Seattle Seahawks on Sunday in a playoff rematch.\n\n\r\n              The win for the 49ers (5-0) offered a small bit of payback for their two late-season losses to Seattle (3-2) last season, including a 41-6 defeat in the divisional round of the playoffs with a short-handed roster.\n\n\r\n              San Francisco was healthier on defense for the first meeting this season, and also had Evans after signing him in March. That was a big reason why Purdy was able to deliver late for the undefeated Niners.\n\n\r\n              “He's a game changer,” Purdy said. “True X receiver that if you get the right look and stuff and you give him a chance, he can make defenses pay. I thought today he did a great job with that, going up and getting the ball multiple times and just using his length and everything to create some separation. So as a quarterback, any time I have an (opportunity) to go to him, it always feels good.”\n\n\r\n              The game featured an NFL season-high seven combined turnovers, an ejection of 49ers linebacker Dre Greenlaw and no touchdown drives of more than 25 yards before San Francisco took over trailing 26-21 late in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              “That’s what we expected, honestly,” Niners linebacker Fred Warner said. “It was going to be a heavyweight bout. Seattle, they’re the guys who won it all last year and they’re ones that you have to go through.”\n\n\r\n              Purdy and Evans responded with an impressive drive against the vaunted Seahawks defense, connecting four times for 75 yards with the biggest play coming on a 28-yard pass to Evans after an intentional grounding penalty left the Niners in a precarious second-and-21 situation.\n\n\r\n              “I do feel like I’m one of the best receivers in the game,” Evans said. “I got a big frame, so if you throw it around me, I’m going to do my best to try to make those contested grabs.”\n\n\r\n              That helped set up Purdy's 7-yard touchdown pass to newcomer Brandin Cooks, and San Francisco took a 29-26 lead following a 2-point conversion to Evans.\n\n\r\n              Sam Darnold responded by leading Seattle on a game-tying field goal drive before Purdy and the 49ers moved the ball 43 yards for Pineiro's go-ahead kick.\n\n\r\n              The Seahawks had one last chance, but Jaxon Smith-Njigba dropped a third-down pass from the 50 at the San Francisco 25, and Cooper Kupp was stopped 1 yard short of the first down by Malik Mustapha on fourth down.\n\n\r\n              “We had an opportunity to go down there and win the game,” Smith-Njigba said. “I have to reel those ones in. I know that.”\n\n\r\n              Purdy finished 19 of 29 for 189 yards with two touchdowns and one interception, with almost all of his production coming in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              “That’s one of the best defenses in the league,” coach Kyle Shanahan said. “There were some ups and downs, by no means was everything perfect. But I thought he was really good in the fourth quarter.”\n\n\r\n              The 49ers failed to score a touchdown in their final two games against Seattle last season. They got into the end zone three times in the first half to take a 21-13 lead but it was the defense that did much of the work.\n\n\r\n              They scored on drives of 11 yards and 18 yards after turnovers by Seattle and extended the lead to 21-13 when the Seahawks' decision to use timeouts on defense to get the ball back late in the half backfired. The Seahawks took over on their 4 with 18 seconds left and San Francisco holding three timeouts. Gracen Halton then stripped George Halani on the first play and recovered the fumble in the end zone for a touchdown.\n\n\r\n              “We want to stay aggressive,” coach Mike Macdonald said. “We got to be able to execute our run game and hold onto the football.”\n\n\r\n              The Seahawks took advantage of turnovers by San Francisco to take the lead in the second half. They turned an interception by Nehemiah Pritchett into a 23-yard drive capped by Darnold's 16-yard pass to Smith-Njigba for a 23-21 lead. Deebo Samuel then lost a fumble on the next possession to set up another field goal to make it a five-point game.\n\n\r\n              Greenlaw ejected for 49ers\r\n              Greenlaw was ejected after head-butting Seattle’s Ernest Jones IV after he delivered a late hit on the Seahawks sideline. The infraction came in the second quarter when Darnold scrambled and was hit late by Greenlaw after going out of bounds. Greenlaw was flagged for a personal foul.\n\n\r\n              Several Seahawks defenders immediately confronted Greenlaw after the hit and Greenlaw responded by head-butting Jones, who wasn’t wearing a helmet. Jones was bleeding after the hit and Greenlaw was ejected. Greenlaw has been ejected at least two other times in his career.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              49ers: Samuel injured his hamstring on the play where he fumbled and didn't return.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              49ers: Host Washington on Monday, Oct. 19.\n\n\r\n              Seahawks: Visit Denver on Thursday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1011/fdeea296f25241fc9e128c382a6634351343/fdeea296f25241fc9e128c382a6634351343.jpg"},"401872993":{"h":"Huntley leads late TD drive to force OT and then scores on 1-yard run as Ravens beat Falcons 30-23","d":"— With Tyler Huntley filling in at quarterback, the Baltimore Ravens were on Snoop patrol.","b":"ATLANTA -- — With Tyler Huntley filling in at quarterback, the Baltimore Ravens were on Snoop patrol.\n\n\r\n              When “Snoop” Huntley is leading the offense, the Ravens don't feel like a backup is on the field.\n\n\r\n              Huntley led Baltimore to a tying touchdown and 2-point conversion in the last minute of regulation and then scored on a 1-yard sneak in overtime as the Ravens rallied to beat the Atlanta Falcons 30-23 on Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              “There's not a locker room in the NFL that has more confidence in their backup quarterback than we have in Snoop,” coach Jesse Minter said.\n\n\r\n              Added safety Kyle Hamilton, whose interception in overtime ended the game: “When Snoop gets a chance to go out there, it's a Snoop game. It's not a backup quarterback, and he's done it so any times. It's a blessing to have him.”\n\n\r\n              Derrick Henry ran for 102 yards against the Falcons' top-ranked run defense for the Ravens (4-1).\n\n\r\n              “If we let it be, this can be a launching point for us to really get this thing rolling,” said Minter after the Ravens took sole possession of first place in the AFC North.\n\n\r\n              After Michael Penix Jr. suffered a rib injury late in the fourth quarter, Tua Tagovailoa took over at quarterback for the Falcons (2-3) in overtime. Tagovailoa's pass for Drake London was intercepted by Hamilton to end the game.\n\n\r\n              “We didn't do enough to win the game,” Falcons coach Kevin Stefanski said.\n\n\r\n              Huntley completed 24 of 32 passes for 201 yards and two touchdowns as the fill-in starter for Lamar Jackson, who has an ankle injury and cheered from the sideline in street clothes.\n\n\r\n              Penix overcame a slow start to pass for 240 yards and a touchdown but could only watch from the bench with a towel on his head as the Ravens put the game away.\n\n\r\n              Jessie Bates III returned a recovered fumble 36 yards for a score for Atlanta in the first quarter.\n\n\r\n              Bijan Robinson ran for 86 yards, but Penix's 5-yard scoring pass to Kyle Pitts was Atlanta's only offensive touchdown.\n\n\r\n              With Baltimore trailing 23-15, Huntley threw a 7-yard scoring pass to Devontez Walker with 40 seconds remaining in regulation and Henry took the direct snap and ran left for the tying conversion. Huntley targeted Falcons backup cornerback Mike Ford for the touchdown pass a play after Mike Hughes left the game with an injury.\n\n\r\n              After Huntley's third-down pass to tight end Mark Andrews was broken up by a big hit from Falcons safety Xavier Watts with 6:28 remaining, the Ravens were forced to punt.\n\n\r\n              Hometown hero\r\n              Hamilton grew up in Atlanta and attended Marist School. There was a strong showing from Ravens fans at Mercedes-Benz Stadium, and many were there to cheer for Hamilton.\n\n\r\n              “I probably know 500 people or something in the stadium right now,” Hamilton said. “It was a pretty special moment.”\n\n\r\n              Sneaky Snoop\r\n              Huntley scored on a second-down run from the 1 to give Baltimore the lead late in regulation.\n\n\r\n              “I was just doing my job,” Huntley said. “Once you get in the end zone you give off an ”Ah!\" and I was ready to get on the sideline and cheer our defense on.\"\n\n\r\n              Penix finds his form to help Falcons build first-half lead\r\n              Bates' return for a first-quarter touchdown gave Atlanta the early lead. Safety Sydney Brown forced the fumble by Andrews.\n\n\r\n              Penix was a combined 0-for-4 passing on Atlanta's first three possessions before finally reclaiming the form he showed while leading the Falcons to back-to-back wins. A screen pass to Pitts seemed to spark Penix, who was 5-for-5 on a touchdown drive that included a 25-yard completion to London and a 36-yard pass to Jahan Dotson to the Ravens 6.\n\n\r\n              Penix found Pitts again over the middle for a 5-yard touchdown pass.\n\n\r\n              The Ravens were more effective than the Falcons in moving the ball most of the first half but had to settle for Tyler Loop's field goals of 21, 51 and 51 yards as Atlanta led 14-9 at halftime.\n\n\r\n              Baltimore opened the second half with a drive built around a 47-yard run by Justice Hill. Huntley's 18-yard scoring pass to Henry cut the Falcons' lead to 17-15 when the 2-point pass to Andrews was incomplete.\n\n\r\n              Matthews almost gets a TD in 200th straight start\r\n              Falcons left tackle Jake Matthews almost added a touchdown to the celebration of his 200th consecutive start, the longest active streak in the NFL.\n\n\r\n              The son of Pro Football Hall of Fame lineman Bruce Matthews lined up as an eligible receiver on second down from the Baltimore 5-yard line in the third quarter.\n\n\r\n              Matthews slipped into the end zone and was wide open, and Penix lofted a pass that forced Matthews to jump. He touched the ball with both hands, but the pass was too high and fell incomplete.\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Ravens: Starting RG Andrew Vorhees (foot) was inactive after being listed as questionable. Emery Jones Jr. made his first career start. Rookie TE Matt Hibner left in the first half with a hand injury but returned in the third quarter. DLs Broderick Washington (ankle) and Travis Jones left in the fourth quarter.\n\n\r\n              Falcons: Penix predicted he will return next week from the rib injury. CB C.J. Henderson was listed as questionable with a groin injury in the third quarter. Matthews played after having been listed as questionable with a groin injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Ravens: At Cleveland next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Falcons: Host Chicago next Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1012/98d13f909e214bcf8fea33b4b4e1de0d31/98d13f909e214bcf8fea33b4b4e1de0d31.jpg"},"401872994":{"h":"Josh Allen racks up 331 yards and Bills outlast Rams with help from blocked FG for 37-34 win in OT","d":"— Josh Allen realized that his extraordinary efforts throughout this frenetic game might all amount to nothing Monday night when the Buffalo Bills fumbled deep in Rams territory early in overtime.","b":"INGLEWOOD, Calif. -- — Josh Allen realized that his extraordinary efforts throughout this frenetic game might all amount to nothing Monday night when the Buffalo Bills fumbled deep in Rams territory early in overtime.\n\n\r\n              The Bills' defense — and Greg Rousseau's outstretched hand — somehow gave him one more chance, and Allen again did everything necessary to get Tyler Bass in position to kick the field goal that ended a memorable evening.\n\n\r\n              Allen accounted for 331 total yards and two touchdowns, Bass made a 45-yard field goal with 1:55 left in overtime shortly after Rousseau blocked the Rams’ field goal attempt, and the Bills outlasted Los Angeles for a 37-34 victory.\n\n\r\n              “That’s the beautiful thing about football: You get another drive,” Allen said. “All you can do is keep moving forward. You’re hoping someone on defense or special teams can make a play, and that’s what happened. Greg Rousseau made a play.”\n\n\r\n              Allen passed for 210 yards and rushed for 121 more for the Bills (4-1), who rallied to win a back-and-forth thriller befitting the teams with the NFL’s last two MVPs at quarterback. Buffalo is off to a 4-1 start for the sixth time in eight seasons, but the first under rookie head coach Joe Brady.\n\n\r\n              The Bills appeared to be in trouble after James Cook fumbled early in OT, but Rousseau gave Allen another chance — and the star quarterback finally won an overtime game for the first time in his career while besting Matthew Stafford, who passed for 383 yards and three touchdowns.\n\n\r\n              “That overtime kind of showed the type of character of who our team is,” Brady said. “You don’t want to fumble. That can’t happen. But we just keep playing.”\n\n\r\n              Cook scored his second touchdown with 1:39 left in regulation for Buffalo, but Kyren Williams scored on a 1-yard surge with 4 seconds left to force overtime. The Bills began OT by driving again, but Aaron Donald recovered Cook’s fumble after Nate Landman punched it out.\n\n\r\n              Stafford drove the Rams to the Buffalo 30, but Sean McVay made a curiously conservative play-call on third down before Harrison Mevis missed his second field goal attempt of the night when Rousseau got a piece of it.\n\n\r\n              “The kicking game, with our PAT, field goal (unit) — not even close to good enough,” McVay said. “It cost us the game tonight. ... I'm going to evaluate everything.”\n\n\r\n              Allen then made scrambles of 19 and 13 yards, moving the Bills into range before Brady elected to try the field goal on second down with nearly two minutes still on the clock. Bass blasted his kick and ran away to celebrate before the officials signaled it good.\n\n\r\n              “The confidence I had in T-Bass was, ‘Hey, 45 yards, he’s going to make this kick,'” Brady said of his decision process.\n\n\r\n              Puka Nacua had a career-high 17 receptions for 138 yards and a score for the Rams (2-3), who are under .500 after five games for the fourth time in five seasons.\n\n\r\n              Along with McVay's fury over the Rams' latest special teams debacle, the coach rued his decision to get conservative before going to Mevis, whose three missed field goals this season are the most in the league.\n\n\r\n              “I wish I would have had a better play call and gone for it, got it tighter,” McVay said. “I'm sick right now with myself, and I'm so proud of that group. This is the tough part of sports, but I'm proud of that team. I'm proud of the way we battled, and I'm sick that we came up short.”\n\n\r\n              With superior special teams play and Allen's relentless effort, Buffalo held on to win another offensive shootout between these teams. The most recent time they met in December 2024, Los Angeles and Buffalo combined for 902 yards in the Rams’ 44-42 win.\n\n\r\n              After Davante Adams’ second TD catch with 7:14 left for the Rams in a game featuring six consecutive lead changes, Allen drove the Bills 71 yards with two scrambles through Los Angeles’ defense. After Cook scored the tiebreaking touchdown, Stafford completed six of seven throws down to the Buffalo 1, where the Rams eschewed a timeout so Williams could punch it in.\n\n\r\n              Adams tied Larry Fitzgerald for the sixth-most scoring receptions in NFL history (121). Blake Corum also rushed for a score, and tight end Tyler Higbee had five catches for 83 yards.\n\n\r\n              Keon Coleman caught Allen's only TD pass for Buffalo.\n\n\r\n              Buffalo's running game\r\n              Cook rushed for 111 yards before his latest fumbling woes for the Bills, while Allen got more than 100 of his yards rushing on scrambles, repeatedly escaping the pocket and rendering useless the Rams’ tenacious secondary coverage. Buffalo finished with 234 yards rushing.\n\n\r\n              “When it’s there, it’s there,” Allen said. “I don’t want to make a living on that, I understand that, but I do know how much of a backbreaker that can be on a defense when you think you’re in pretty good coverage. I felt like there was some room to maneuver there and keep the chains moving.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Bills: RT Spencer Brown left in the second half with a back injury. ... Top WR DJ Moore sat out with a shoulder injury.\n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Bills: At Vegas on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              Rams: Host Arizona on Sunday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1013/dm_261013_Josh_Allen2/dm_261013_Josh_Allen2.jpg"},"401872995":{"h":"Broncos beat the Seahawks 17-14 in OT on Ja'Quan McMillian's pick, Wil Lutz's 53-yarder","d":"— The Denver Broncos are atrocious on offense, depleted on defense and shaky on special teams. All they do is find ways to win.","b":"DENVER -- — The Denver Broncos are atrocious on offense, depleted on defense and shaky on special teams. All they do is find ways to win.\n\n\r\n              Ja’Quan McMillian intercepted Sam Darnold’s pass in overtime Thursday night and Wil Lutz kicked a 53-yard field goal that gave the Broncos a 17-14 victory over the Seattle Seahawks.\n\n\r\n              The Broncos emerged from a tough six-week season-opening stretch with a 4-2 record despite an injury-riddled roster and a shaky quarterback, and the Seahawks (3-3) lost for the second time in five days. They also matched their loss total from last season, when they won their second Super Bowl title. \n\n\r\n              The Broncos won despite Bo Nix throwing for just 107 yards, J.K. Dobbins leading the rushing attack with a mere 41 yards and managing a meager three first downs and 168 yards of total offense — less than half the 353 yards piled up by the Seahawks.\n\n\r\n              “I don't know why we can't really push the ball vertically right now,” Nix said. “But defenses are just sitting back and hanging out. ... Hopefully we find a way to get more out of it.”\n\n\r\n              Nix was as honest as he's been to date in his postgame comments about Denver's scuffling offense.\n\n\r\n              “We’re definitely capable, we’ve shown it,\" he said. \"And, you know I don’t know where the disconnect is right now, to be honest with you. ... I just think we’re really, for some reason, struggling to find those explosive plays, and that’s what makes an offense really spectacular: Is finding explosive plays. And right now, we don’t have those.” \n\n\r\n              Denver's depleted defense came to the rescue yet again, hitting Darnold 17 times and collecting five sacks. \n\n\r\n              “The quarterback's getting hit way too much,” Seahawks coach Mike Macdonald said. “Everybody sees that.”\n\n\r\n              “You’ve got to give credit to those guys,\" Darnold said. “They’ve got a really tough defense, really good defense coordinator in Vance (Joseph).”\n\n\r\n              On Seattle's opening drive in overtime, McMillian intercepted a pass intended for Rashid Shaheed at the Denver 43, the second pick of the night by Darnold, who completed 27 of 51 passes for 278 yards without a touchdown.\n\n\r\n              Those stats were better than Nix's. He barely topped 100 yards on 17-of-27 passing after throwing for 123 last week in a tight win over the Chargers.\n\n\r\n              Jason Myers' 34-yard field goal tied it at 14-all with 3 seconds left in regulation and capped an 11-0 Seattle run over the final 5 1/2 minutes. The Seahawks started their game-tying drive at midfield after a 31-yard punt return by Shaheed.\n\n\r\n              “No moral victories in the league,” Macdonald said. “It’s a bottom-line business, production business. But I’m proud of our effort. I’m proud of the resolve of the team. I’m proud of our connectedness of our team.\n\n\r\n              “However, there’s things that we need to get better at quickly, and we know those things; they’re within our control, and that’s what we do. We find solutions, and we get things figured out, we move forward, and we evolve.”\n\n\r\n              That's essentially what Sean Payton was saying in the winning locker room.\n\n\r\n              “Defensively, we were magnificent,” said Payton, who acknowledged, “I’m a little bit of a realist to know that 4-2 could easily be 2-4.”\n\n\r\n              Denver appeared to have victory in hand when Kris Abrams-Draine intercepted Darnold's pass at the Denver 30 with 1:25 left in regulation, but the Broncos burned just 15 seconds of clock before punting.\n\n\r\n              After scoring just three points on their first eight drives, the Seahawks put together an 11-play, 67-yard masterpiece that ended with running back George Holani scoring from a yard out. Darnold’s 2-point pass to Shaheed pulled Seattle within 14-11 with 5:41 left.\n\n\r\n              Dobbins had a 4-yard touchdown run and fellow running back R.J. Harvey took a direct snap and scored from a yard out on Denver’s next drive for a 14-3 halftime lead.\n\n\r\n              The Seahawks’ most promising first-half drive ended at the Denver 34 when Nik Bonitto punched the ball from Shaheed’s grasp and inside linebacker Justin Strnad recovered.\n\n\r\n              True to form\r\n              The Seahawks franchise fell to 19-30-1 in overtime and the Broncos improved to 33-23-2. That's second-best in the NFL.\n\n\r\n              “It was a good win,” Payton said, “but we've got a lot of work to do.”\n\n\r\n              Injuries\r\n              Seahawks TE Elijah Arroyo left late in the first half with an apparent right shoulder injury sustained while diving for a fumbled ball.\n\n\r\n              The Broncos were without star CB Patrick Surtain II and starting safety Talanoa Hufanga, both out with ankle injuries. RB Tyler Badie injured a thumb in the second quarter. \n\n\r\n              Up next\r\n              Seahawks: Host the Kansas City Chiefs on Oct. 25.\n\n\r\n              Broncos: Host the Arizona Cardinals on Oct. 25.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                See AP’s full NFL coverage here","by":null,"img":"https://espnmedia-cdn.akamaized.net/espn/media/common/2026/1016/791decdcd838465bbb8734d90159a71829/791decdcd838465bbb8734d90159a71829.jpg"}});
